ADDART({id:"hitler",t:"Adolf Hitler",c:"People",i:"📕",w:"Adolf Hitler",r:["fascism","ww2","holocaust","propaganda","genocide","antisemitism"],
p:[["Dictator of Germany","Adolf Hitler led Nazi Germany from 1933 to 1945."],["War and genocide","His policies started World War II in Europe and led to the Holocaust."],["Remembered as a warning","His rule is studied as an example of how democracy can be destroyed."]],
l:"Adolf Hitler (1889–1945) was the leader of the Nazi Party and the dictator of Germany from 1933 to 1945. Born in Austria, he served as a soldier in World War I and afterwards joined a small nationalist party in Munich that became the National Socialist German Workers’ Party, or Nazi Party. Using powerful speeches, propaganda, violence and hatred of Jews and other groups, he exploited Germany’s anger over defeat in World War I and the hardship of the Great Depression. He became chancellor in January 1933 and within months turned Germany into a one-party dictatorship. His aggressive expansion started World War II in Europe in 1939, and his regime murdered about six million Jews in the Holocaust, along with millions of others. Facing defeat, he killed himself in Berlin on 30 April 1945. Historians study his rise to understand how a modern democracy collapsed into dictatorship and genocide.",
s:[["Early life","Hitler was born on 20 April 1889 in Braunau am Inn, Austria. He hoped to become an artist but was twice rejected by the Academy of Fine Arts in Vienna and lived in poverty there. In 1913 he moved to Munich and in 1914 joined the German army, serving as a messenger on the Western Front and receiving the Iron Cross."],["Rise to power","In 1923 Hitler led a failed attempt to seize power in Munich, the Beer Hall Putsch, and was jailed, where he dictated his book Mein Kampf (‘My Struggle’). After his release he built the Nazi Party into a mass movement. In the 1932 elections it became the largest party in parliament, and on 30 January 1933 President Hindenburg appointed him chancellor. The Enabling Act of March 1933 let him make laws without parliament, and after Hindenburg’s death in 1934 he made himself Führer (‘leader’), combining the posts of president and chancellor."],["Dictatorship","The Nazi state banned other parties, crushed trade unions, controlled the press and used the Gestapo secret police and concentration camps against opponents. The Nuremberg Laws of 1935 stripped Jews of citizenship, and the Kristallnacht attacks of November 1938 destroyed synagogues and Jewish shops. Rearmament and public works reduced unemployment, which won the regime support."],["War and the Holocaust","Hitler annexed Austria in 1938 and took Czechoslovakia in 1938–39. His invasion of Poland on 1 September 1939 started World War II in Europe. In 1941 he invaded the Soviet Union. Behind the front lines, Nazi forces carried out the Holocaust, the systematic murder of about six million Jews, as well as killings of Roma, disabled people, Poles, Soviet prisoners and others."],["Defeat and death","After defeats at Stalingrad and in North Africa, and the Allied landings in France in 1944, Germany was invaded from east and west. Hitler survived an assassination attempt by German officers on 20 July 1944. As Soviet troops reached Berlin, he killed himself in his bunker on 30 April 1945; Germany surrendered a week later."]],
k:[["Born","20 April 1889, Braunau am Inn, Austria"],["Died","30 April 1945, Berlin"],["Party","Nazi Party (NSDAP)"],["Leader of Germany","1933–1945"],["Book","Mein Kampf (1925)"],["Title","Führer"]],
d:[["1889","Born in Austria"],["1923","Beer Hall Putsch fails"],["1925","Mein Kampf is published"],["1933","Becomes chancellor of Germany"],["1934","Makes himself Führer"],["1939","Invades Poland, starting World War II in Europe"],["1945","Dies in Berlin as Germany is defeated"]],
s2:[["How democracy failed","Germany’s Weimar Republic was weakened by defeat, hyperinflation in 1923, the Great Depression and street violence. Many conservatives thought they could control Hitler by giving him power. Historians point to propaganda, fear, the failure of other parties to unite and the use of emergency powers as lessons for protecting democracy."],["Laws against Nazi symbols","After the war, Germany and Austria banned Nazi symbols and Holocaust denial, except for education, art and research. Germany teaches the Nazi period in all schools, and its memorials, such as the Memorial to the Murdered Jews of Europe in Berlin, are part of a culture of remembrance."]],
sp:["The Nuremberg trials","From 1945 to 1946, an international court at Nuremberg tried leading Nazis for war crimes and crimes against humanity. Twelve were sentenced to death. The trials established that following orders is no excuse for atrocities and shaped modern international law."],
g:[["Dictator","A ruler with total power, not limited by law or elections"],["Chancellor","The head of government in Germany"],["Propaganda","Information spread to persuade, often misleading"],["Genocide","The deliberate destruction of a people"]],
f:["Hitler was Austrian by birth and became a German citizen only in 1932.","The Nazis never won a majority in a free election; their best result was about 37 percent.","Hitler was rejected twice by an art academy in Vienna."],
fun:"The word ‘Nazi’ comes from the first part of Nationalsozialist, the party’s name in German.",
tl:"Si Adolf Hitler ang diktador ng Germany mula 1933 hanggang 1945 at pinuno ng Partidong Nazi. Sinimulan ng kaniyang pagsalakay sa Poland ang Ikalawang Digmaang Pandaigdig sa Europa, at pinatay ng kaniyang rehimen ang mga anim na milyong Hudyo sa Holocaust."});

ADDART({id:"stalin",t:"Joseph Stalin",c:"People",i:"📕",w:"Joseph Stalin",r:["lenin","socialism","coldwar","gulag","ww2","stalingrad"],
p:[["Soviet dictator","Joseph Stalin ruled the Soviet Union from the late 1920s until 1953."],["Industry and terror","He turned the country into an industrial power at the cost of millions of lives."],["War leader","He led the Soviet Union to victory over Nazi Germany."]],
l:"Joseph Stalin (1878–1953) was the leader of the Soviet Union from the late 1920s until his death. Born in Georgia, he became a Bolshevik revolutionary and, after Lenin’s death in 1924, outmaneuvered his rivals to take total control. His Five-Year Plans rapidly built factories, dams and cities, and his forced collectivization of farms caused famines that killed millions, including in Ukraine, where the famine is known as the Holodomor. In the Great Terror of 1936–38 hundreds of thousands were shot and many more sent to Gulag labor camps. After first signing a pact with Hitler, the Soviet Union was invaded by Germany in 1941 and, at enormous cost, played a decisive part in defeating it. Stalin then imposed communist governments across Eastern Europe, beginning the Cold War. He died in 1953, and his successor Nikita Khrushchev later denounced his crimes.",
s:[["Revolutionary","Stalin was born Ioseb Jughashvili in Gori, Georgia, in December 1878. He trained as a priest but became a Marxist revolutionary, robbing banks to fund the Bolsheviks and spending years in Siberian exile. He took the name Stalin, from the Russian word for steel."],["Taking power","After the 1917 revolution, Stalin became general secretary of the Communist Party in 1922. Lenin warned in his last writings that Stalin was too rude and should be removed, but after Lenin died in 1924 Stalin defeated Leon Trotsky and others. Trotsky was exiled and murdered in Mexico in 1940."],["Five-Year Plans and famine","From 1928, Stalin forced rapid industrialization and combined peasant farms into collective farms. Resisting peasants, labelled kulaks, were deported or shot. Grain was taken even as people starved; the famine of 1932–33 killed millions, most of them in Ukraine and Kazakhstan. Many countries recognize the Ukrainian famine, the Holodomor, as a genocide."],["The Great Terror","In 1936–38, secret police arrested well over a million people, held show trials of old Bolshevik leaders and executed about 750,000 people. Army officers, scientists, writers and ordinary citizens were accused of being spies or traitors. Millions passed through the Gulag camps during his rule."],["War and Cold War","In 1939 Stalin signed a non-aggression pact with Hitler and divided Poland with him. When Germany invaded in June 1941, the Soviet Union suffered around 27 million deaths but won the battles of Moscow, Stalingrad and Kursk and captured Berlin. After 1945 Stalin kept control of Eastern Europe, tested an atomic bomb in 1949 and supported the communist side in Korea."]],
k:[["Born","December 1878, Gori, Georgia"],["Died","5 March 1953, near Moscow"],["Real name","Ioseb Jughashvili"],["Led the Soviet Union","Late 1920s–1953"],["Policies","Five-Year Plans, collectivization"],["Successor","Nikita Khrushchev"]],
d:[["1878","Born in Gori, Georgia"],["1922","Becomes general secretary of the Communist Party"],["1928","First Five-Year Plan begins"],["1932–33","Famine kills millions"],["1936–38","The Great Terror"],["1941–45","Leads the Soviet Union in war against Germany"],["1953","Dies near Moscow"],["1956","Khrushchev denounces him in a secret speech"]],
s2:[["Cult of personality","Stalin was shown as a wise, all-knowing father of the nation in posters, songs and statues, and cities were named after him, such as Stalingrad. Photographs were altered to remove people who had fallen from favor. After 1956 most statues were taken down."],["How he is remembered","Some Russians remember Stalin as the leader who won the war and made the country a superpower, and his popularity in Russian polls has risen in recent years. Historians and the families of victims stress the millions who died in famines, purges and camps."]],
sp:["Khrushchev’s secret speech","On 25 February 1956, Nikita Khrushchev told a closed meeting of the Communist Party that Stalin had committed crimes and built a cult of personality. The speech shocked communists around the world, led to the release of many prisoners and was followed by the removal of Stalin’s body from Lenin’s tomb in 1961."],
g:[["Collectivization","Forcing private farms into large state-controlled farms"],["Purge","Removing people seen as disloyal, often by killing or imprisoning them"],["Show trial","A public trial held for propaganda, with the verdict decided in advance"],["Gulag","The Soviet system of forced labor camps"]],
f:["Stalin chose his name from the Russian word stal, meaning steel.","Stalingrad, scene of a turning-point battle in World War II, was renamed Volgograd in 1961.","Stalin was from Georgia, not Russia, and spoke Russian with a Georgian accent."],
fun:"Soviet photo retouchers became so skilled at erasing Stalin’s fallen rivals that some pictures were changed several times.",
tl:"Si Joseph Stalin ang pinuno ng Unyong Sobyet mula huling bahagi ng dekada 1920 hanggang 1953. Mabilis niyang pinaunlad ang industriya, ngunit milyon-milyon ang namatay sa taggutom, pagpatay at mga kampo ng Gulag. Pinamunuan din niya ang bansa sa tagumpay laban sa Nazi Germany."});

ADDART({id:"mussolini",t:"Benito Mussolini",c:"People",i:"📕",w:"Benito Mussolini",r:["fascism","ww2","hitler","italycountry","propaganda"],
p:[["Founder of fascism","Benito Mussolini founded Italian fascism and ruled Italy from 1922 to 1943."],["Ally of Hitler","He joined Nazi Germany in World War II."],["Downfall","He was overthrown in 1943 and killed in 1945."]],
l:"Benito Mussolini (1883–1945) was an Italian journalist and politician who founded the fascist movement and ruled Italy as dictator from 1922 to 1943. A socialist in his youth, he turned to fierce nationalism during World War I. In 1919 he formed the Fasci di Combattimento, whose black-shirted squads attacked socialists and trade unionists. After the March on Rome in October 1922, the king made him prime minister, and by 1925 he had made himself dictator, known as Il Duce (‘the leader’). His regime banned opposition, controlled the press and schools, and promised to restore the glory of ancient Rome. He invaded Ethiopia in 1935, allied with Hitler and entered World War II in 1940. After military defeats and the Allied invasion of Sicily, he was overthrown in July 1943. Rescued by German troops, he led a puppet state in northern Italy until partisans captured and shot him in April 1945.",
s:[["From socialist to fascist","Mussolini was born on 29 July 1883 in Predappio, the son of a blacksmith. He became editor of the socialist newspaper Avanti! but was expelled from the party in 1914 for supporting Italy’s entry into World War I. He served in the army and afterwards blamed liberals and socialists for Italy’s disappointment at the peace settlement."],["The March on Rome","In October 1922, tens of thousands of fascist Blackshirts converged on Rome. Rather than use the army against them, King Victor Emmanuel III invited Mussolini to form a government. After the murder of the socialist leader Giacomo Matteotti in 1924 caused a crisis, Mussolini declared himself responsible and moved to full dictatorship."],["The fascist state","Fascism glorified the nation, the leader, discipline and war, and rejected democracy and communism. The regime organized youth groups, controlled newspapers and radio, and built roads and public buildings. In 1929 the Lateran Treaty with the Pope created Vatican City. In 1938 Italy passed racial laws against Jews."],["War and defeat","Italy conquered Ethiopia in 1935–36, using poison gas, and joined Germany in the Pact of Steel in 1939. Italian armies suffered defeats in Greece and North Africa. On 25 July 1943 the Fascist Grand Council and the king removed Mussolini. German commandos freed him, and he led the Italian Social Republic in the north until he was caught while fleeing and shot on 28 April 1945."]],
k:[["Born","29 July 1883, Predappio, Italy"],["Died","28 April 1945, near Lake Como"],["Title","Il Duce"],["Ruled Italy","1922–1943"],["Movement","Fascism"],["Supporters","Blackshirts"]],
d:[["1883","Born in Predappio"],["1919","Founds the fascist movement"],["1922","March on Rome; becomes prime minister"],["1925","Declares dictatorship"],["1929","Lateran Treaty creates Vatican City"],["1935","Invades Ethiopia"],["1940","Italy enters World War II"],["1943","Overthrown"],["1945","Killed by partisans"]],
s2:[["The word ‘fascism’","Fascism comes from the Italian fascio, a bundle, and the fasces, a bundle of rods around an axe carried before magistrates in ancient Rome as a symbol of authority. Mussolini used it to suggest strength through unity."],["Myth of efficiency","A popular saying claims Mussolini ‘made the trains run on time’. Historians note that much railway improvement happened before he came to power and that the claim was largely propaganda."]],
sp:["Italy after fascism","In 1946 Italians voted in a referendum to abolish the monarchy, which had cooperated with Mussolini, and became a republic. The 1948 constitution bans reorganizing the Fascist Party, though neo-fascist groups have continued to exist."],
g:[["Fascism","A far-right system based on extreme nationalism and a dictator"],["Blackshirts","Mussolini’s armed fascist squads"],["Partisans","Armed resistance fighters"],["Puppet state","A government controlled by a foreign power"]],
f:["Mussolini was a journalist before becoming a politician.","Vatican City was created by an agreement between Mussolini’s government and the Pope in 1929.","Hitler admired Mussolini and copied ideas from Italian fascism."],
fun:"Before entering politics, Mussolini worked as a schoolteacher and wrote a romance novel.",
tl:"Si Benito Mussolini ang nagtatag ng pasismo at naging diktador ng Italya mula 1922 hanggang 1943. Naging kakampi siya ni Hitler sa Ikalawang Digmaang Pandaigdig, pinatalsik noong 1943 at pinatay ng mga partisano noong 1945."});

ADDART({id:"polpot",t:"Pol Pot and the Khmer Rouge",c:"History",i:"📕",w:"Pol Pot",r:["genocide","vietnam","coldwar","mao","culturalrev"],
p:[["A deadly regime","The Khmer Rouge ruled Cambodia from 1975 to 1979 under Pol Pot."],["Emptying the cities","They forced people into the countryside to build a farming society."],["Mass death","About 1.5 to 2 million people died, nearly a quarter of the population."]],
l:"Pol Pot (about 1925–1998) was the leader of the Khmer Rouge, a communist movement that ruled Cambodia from 1975 to 1979 and renamed it Democratic Kampuchea. Determined to create a pure, classless farming society, the regime emptied the cities, abolished money, markets, schools and religion, and forced the whole population to work in rural labor camps. People suspected of being enemies, including former officials, teachers, monks, ethnic minorities and anyone educated, were imprisoned and killed. Through executions, starvation, overwork and disease, an estimated 1.5 to 2 million people died, close to a quarter of Cambodia’s population. Vietnam invaded in December 1978 and drove the Khmer Rouge from power in January 1979. Pol Pot died in the jungle in 1998 without facing trial, but a court backed by the United Nations later convicted surviving leaders of genocide and crimes against humanity.",
s:[["Rise of the Khmer Rouge","Pol Pot, born Saloth Sar, studied in Paris in the early 1950s, where he joined communist circles. Back in Cambodia, his movement grew in the jungle, gaining recruits after a 1970 coup and heavy American bombing during the Vietnam War. On 17 April 1975 Khmer Rouge troops captured Phnom Penh."],["Year Zero","The regime declared a new beginning, sometimes called Year Zero. Within days the cities were emptied at gunpoint; hospitals were cleared and the sick forced to walk. Families were separated, children were taken to be trained as cadres, and people worked long hours in fields and on irrigation projects with little food."],["Prisons and killing fields","The secret police ran nearly 200 prisons. At Tuol Sleng, known as S-21, a former school in Phnom Penh, around 14,000 to 18,000 prisoners were held, tortured into confessions and killed; only a handful survived. Mass graves across the country became known as the killing fields."],["Fall and justice","Border attacks on Vietnam led to a Vietnamese invasion, and Phnom Penh fell on 7 January 1979. The Khmer Rouge fought on as guerrillas for years and, because of Cold War politics, still represented Cambodia at the United Nations for more than a decade. The Extraordinary Chambers in the Courts of Cambodia, set up in 2006, convicted the S-21 chief Duch and the leaders Nuon Chea and Khieu Samphan."]],
k:[["Leader","Pol Pot (Saloth Sar)"],["Ruled Cambodia","17 April 1975 – 7 January 1979"],["Country name","Democratic Kampuchea"],["Estimated deaths","1.5–2 million"],["Infamous prison","Tuol Sleng (S-21)"],["Tribunal","ECCC, 2006–2022"]],
d:[["1975","Khmer Rouge captures Phnom Penh and empties the cities"],["1976","Pol Pot becomes prime minister"],["1978","Vietnam invades"],["1979","Khmer Rouge driven from Phnom Penh"],["1998","Pol Pot dies"],["2010","Tribunal convicts Duch"],["2018","Tribunal finds Nuon Chea and Khieu Samphan guilty of genocide"]],
s2:[["Remembering","Tuol Sleng is now a genocide museum, and the Choeung Ek killing field outside Phnom Penh has a memorial stupa holding thousands of skulls. Cambodia marks a Day of Remembrance each May. Many survivors did not speak about their experiences for years."],["The film The Killing Fields","The 1984 film The Killing Fields told the story of Cambodian journalist Dith Pran, who survived the labor camps. Haing S. Ngor, a survivor who played him, won an Academy Award."]],
sp:["Land mines","Decades of war left millions of land mines in Cambodia, especially near the Thai border. They have killed and injured tens of thousands of people. Demining groups have cleared huge areas, and trained rats that can sniff out explosives have helped speed up the work."],
g:[["Khmer Rouge","‘Red Khmers’, Cambodia’s communist movement"],["Cadre","A trained member of a political movement"],["Killing fields","Sites of mass executions and graves in Cambodia"],["Tribunal","A court set up to try particular crimes"]],
f:["The Khmer Rouge abolished money, markets and schools.","Only a handful of the thousands of prisoners held at S-21 survived.","Pol Pot was never tried; he died in 1998."],
fun:"A landmine-detecting rat named Magawa, who sniffed out more than 100 land mines and unexploded bombs in Cambodia, received a gold medal for animal bravery in 2020.",
tl:"Pinamunuan ni Pol Pot ang Khmer Rouge na namahala sa Cambodia mula 1975 hanggang 1979. Pinaalis nila ang mga tao sa mga lungsod at pinilit magtrabaho sa bukid, at mga 1.5 hanggang 2 milyon ang namatay, halos ikaapat na bahagi ng populasyon."});

ADDART({id:"gulag",t:"The Gulag",c:"History",i:"⛓️",w:"Gulag",r:["stalin","lenin","russianlit","coldwar","ussrfall"],
p:[["Soviet labor camps","The Gulag was a network of forced labor camps in the Soviet Union."],["Millions of prisoners","Around 18 million people passed through the camps."],["Exposed by writers","Survivors such as Alexander Solzhenitsyn told the world about it."]],
l:"The Gulag was the Soviet Union’s system of forced labor camps, which held both criminals and political prisoners. Its name is an abbreviation of the Russian for ‘Main Administration of Camps’. The system grew from camps set up after the 1917 revolution and expanded enormously under Joseph Stalin in the 1930s. Prisoners, many convicted on false charges of being enemies of the people, were made to cut timber, dig canals, build railways and mine gold, coal and uranium, often in the harsh Arctic north and in Siberia. Food was scarce, the cold extreme and work quotas brutal. Around 18 million people passed through the camps between 1930 and 1953, and well over a million died there. After Stalin’s death in 1953, most political prisoners were released and the Gulag administration was dissolved in 1960, although prison labor continued.",
s:[["How it grew","The first camps were set up in the early 1920s, including on the Solovetsky Islands in the White Sea. From 1930, the Gulag administration ran a growing empire of camps that supplied labor for Stalin’s Five-Year Plans. The White Sea–Baltic Canal, built by prisoners in 1931–33, cost thousands of lives."],["Who was sent","Prisoners included peasants resisting collectivization, people arrested in the purges, prisoners of war, whole ethnic groups deported during World War II, and ordinary people punished for small crimes such as stealing food or arriving late to work. The notorious Article 58 of the criminal code covered ‘counter-revolutionary’ crimes."],["Life in the camps","Camps in Kolyma, in far north-eastern Siberia, were among the worst, with winter temperatures below −50 °C. Prisoners lived in wooden barracks, received food according to how much they produced and were often worked to exhaustion. Guards and criminal gangs controlled daily life."],["The end and the memory","After Stalin died in 1953, uprisings broke out in several camps, and amnesties released millions. In 1962 Solzhenitsyn’s novel One Day in the Life of Ivan Denisovich was allowed to be published, and in 1973 his study The Gulag Archipelago appeared abroad. The organization Memorial collected the stories of victims until Russian courts closed it in 2021; it shared the Nobel Peace Prize in 2022."]],
k:[["Meaning","Main Administration of Camps"],["Active","1930–1960 (camps existed earlier)"],["Prisoners","About 18 million passed through, 1930–1953"],["Largest camp region","Kolyma, Siberia"],["Famous book","The Gulag Archipelago (1973)"],["Remembrance group","Memorial (closed 2021)"]],
d:[["1923","Camp opens on the Solovetsky Islands"],["1930","Gulag administration created"],["1931–33","Prisoners build the White Sea–Baltic Canal"],["1937–38","Camps swell during the Great Terror"],["1953","Stalin dies; mass amnesty follows"],["1960","Gulag administration dissolved"],["1973","The Gulag Archipelago published"]],
s2:[["Economy of the camps","The Gulag provided labor for remote projects that free workers would not take on, including the Norilsk nickel works and the Kolyma gold mines. Historians debate how productive it really was; many argue that forced labor was inefficient as well as cruel."],["Other camp systems","Forced labor camps have existed in other countries, including Nazi Germany’s concentration camps and China’s laogai (‘reform through labor’) system. Human rights groups report labor camps in North Korea today."]],
sp:["Solzhenitsyn","Alexander Solzhenitsyn was arrested in 1945 for criticizing Stalin in a private letter and spent eight years in camps. His writings revealed the Gulag to readers worldwide. He won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1970, was expelled from the Soviet Union in 1974 and returned to Russia in 1994."],
g:[["Forced labor","Work people are made to do against their will"],["Political prisoner","Someone jailed for their beliefs or opposition to a government"],["Amnesty","An official pardon for a group of prisoners"],["Archipelago","A group of islands"]],
f:["Gulag is an abbreviation, like an acronym.","Solzhenitsyn called the camps an archipelago because they were scattered like islands across the country.","Some camp prisoners were scientists who worked in special prison research labs."],
fun:"The ‘Road of Bones’, the Kolyma Highway in Siberia, got its name because prisoners who died building it were buried beneath and beside it.",
tl:"Ang Gulag ay sistema ng mga kampo ng sapilitang paggawa sa Unyong Sobyet. Mga 18 milyong tao ang dumaan dito mula 1930 hanggang 1953, at mahigit isang milyon ang namatay, lalo na sa malamig na Siberia."});

ADDART({id:"culturalrev",t:"The Cultural Revolution",c:"History",i:"📕",w:"Cultural Revolution",r:["mao","modernchina","china","socialism","propaganda"],
p:[["Mao’s campaign","The Cultural Revolution was a political campaign in China from 1966 to 1976."],["Red Guards","Young Red Guards attacked teachers, officials and old traditions."],["Lasting scars","It caused chaos, persecution and the deaths of hundreds of thousands or more."]],
l:"The Cultural Revolution was a violent political movement launched by Mao Zedong in China in 1966 and lasting until his death in 1976. Mao, who had lost influence after the disastrous Great Leap Forward, called on young people to purge the country of ‘bourgeois’ and ‘capitalist’ elements and to destroy the ‘Four Olds’: old customs, culture, habits and ideas. Millions of students formed Red Guard groups that attacked teachers, intellectuals and party officials, held public ‘struggle sessions’ and destroyed temples, books and works of art. Rival factions fought each other, and the army had to restore order. Schools and universities closed, and about 17 million urban youths were sent to the countryside. Estimates of deaths range from hundreds of thousands to about two million. In 1981 the Communist Party officially called the movement a catastrophe for which Mao bore chief responsibility.",
s:[["Origins","After the Great Leap Forward (1958–62) led to a famine in which tens of millions died, Mao stepped back from daily government. In May 1966 he launched the Cultural Revolution, claiming that the party was being taken over by people who wanted to restore capitalism. He swam in the Yangtze River in July 1966 to show he was still vigorous."],["Red Guards","In August 1966 Mao greeted huge rallies of Red Guards in Tiananmen Square. Waving the ‘Little Red Book’ of his quotations, they denounced and beat teachers, searched homes and smashed temples, graves and antiques. President Liu Shaoqi was purged and died in detention in 1969, and Deng Xiaoping was sent to work in a tractor factory."],["Down to the countryside","By 1968 the violence between factions was so great that Mao disbanded the Red Guards and sent millions of young people to rural villages ‘to learn from the peasants’. Many lost their education for a decade."],["The end","Mao’s chosen successor Lin Biao died in a plane crash in 1971 while apparently fleeing to the Soviet Union. After Mao died in September 1976, his wife Jiang Qing and three allies, the ‘Gang of Four’, were arrested and later tried. Deng Xiaoping returned and began economic reforms in 1978."]],
k:[["Years","1966–1976"],["Leader","Mao Zedong"],["Young activists","Red Guards"],["Book","Quotations from Chairman Mao (Little Red Book)"],["Targets","The ‘Four Olds’, intellectuals, officials"],["Official verdict (1981)","A catastrophe"]],
d:[["1966","Mao launches the Cultural Revolution; Red Guard rallies in Beijing"],["1968","Red Guards sent to the countryside"],["1969","Liu Shaoqi dies in detention"],["1971","Lin Biao dies in a plane crash"],["1976","Mao dies; the Gang of Four is arrested"],["1981","Party resolution condemns the movement"]],
s2:[["Culture under control","During these years, only a few approved ‘model operas’ and ballets, such as The Red Detachment of Women, could be widely performed. Traditional opera, foreign music and many books were banned. Some ancient sites were saved by local people or by orders from Premier Zhou Enlai."],["Remembering today","Discussion of the Cultural Revolution is still limited in China. Memoirs written abroad, such as Jung Chang’s Wild Swans, and the ‘scar literature’ that appeared in China in the late 1970s describe the suffering of ordinary families."]],
sp:["Ping-pong and Nixon","Even during the Cultural Revolution, China began opening to the West. In 1971 an American table tennis team visited China, and in February 1972 US President Richard Nixon visited Beijing and met Mao, changing Cold War politics."],
g:[["Red Guards","Student groups who carried out Mao’s campaign"],["Struggle session","A public meeting where people were humiliated and attacked"],["Purge","Removing people from power or society"],["Faction","A group within a larger group, often in conflict with others"]],
f:["The Little Red Book is one of the most printed books in history, with over a billion copies.","Universities in China barely admitted students for several years.","Deng Xiaoping, later China’s leader, was purged twice during the period."],
fun:"Mao’s swim in the Yangtze in 1966 was announced as a record-breaking feat, used to show the world he was strong enough to lead.",
tl:"Ang Rebolusyong Pangkultura ay kampanyang pampulitika ni Mao Zedong sa China mula 1966 hanggang 1976. Inatake ng mga Red Guard ang mga guro, opisyal at lumang tradisyon, at idineklara ito ng Partido Komunista noong 1981 bilang isang malaking sakuna."});

ADDART({id:"tiananmen",t:"The Tiananmen Square Protests",c:"History",i:"🕊️",w:"1989 Tiananmen Square protests and massacre",r:["beijing","modernchina","china","censorship","democ"],
p:[["Students demand change","In 1989, students and workers protested in Beijing’s Tiananmen Square."],["A violent end","The army cleared the protests on 3–4 June, killing many people."],["Still censored","The events remain heavily censored in China."]],
l:"The Tiananmen Square protests were a wave of demonstrations in Beijing and other Chinese cities in the spring of 1989. Students, later joined by workers and other citizens, called for political reform, freedom of the press and an end to corruption. At their height, a million people filled the square and nearby streets, and some students went on hunger strike. On 20 May the government declared martial law, and on the night of 3–4 June troops and tanks moved into central Beijing, firing on crowds in the surrounding streets to clear the square. The number killed is still unknown; estimates range from several hundred to several thousand. The image of a lone man standing in front of a column of tanks became one of the most famous photographs of the 20th century. In China the events are not publicly discussed and are heavily censored online, while people elsewhere hold vigils each 4 June.",
s:[["Causes","Economic reforms in the 1980s brought growth but also inflation and corruption. Students wanted political freedoms to match the economic changes. The death on 15 April 1989 of Hu Yaobang, a reform-minded leader who had been removed from his post, sparked gatherings to mourn him that grew into protests."],["The occupation of the square","Students occupied Tiananmen Square from April. In May, more than a thousand began a hunger strike just as Soviet leader Mikhail Gorbachev visited Beijing, drawing international media. Protesters built a 10-meter statue called the Goddess of Democracy facing the portrait of Mao."],["The crackdown","Party leaders were divided; Zhao Ziyang, who visited the hunger strikers, was removed and spent the rest of his life under house arrest. On the night of 3–4 June, soldiers advanced on the square, and most of the deaths happened in the streets to the west as residents tried to block them. The students remaining in the square left in the early morning."],["Afterwards","Protest leaders were arrested or fled abroad, and Western countries imposed arms embargoes on China. The government said it had stopped a ‘counter-revolutionary riot’. China continued its economic reforms while keeping tight political control."]],
k:[["Dates","15 April – 4 June 1989"],["Place","Tiananmen Square, Beijing"],["Martial law declared","20 May 1989"],["Crackdown","Night of 3–4 June 1989"],["Deaths","Unknown; hundreds to thousands"],["Famous image","‘Tank Man’, 5 June 1989"]],
d:[["15 April 1989","Death of Hu Yaobang sparks gatherings"],["13 May","Hunger strike begins"],["20 May","Martial law declared"],["30 May","Goddess of Democracy statue unveiled"],["3–4 June","Army clears central Beijing"],["5 June","A lone man blocks a line of tanks"]],
s2:[["Tank Man","On 5 June 1989, a man carrying shopping bags stood in front of a column of tanks on Chang’an Avenue, climbing onto the lead tank before being pulled away by bystanders. His identity and fate have never been confirmed. The scene was filmed and photographed from a nearby hotel."],["Censorship and remembrance","In mainland China, searches for the date and many related words are blocked online. Hong Kong held large candlelight vigils every year until 2020, when they were banned; organizers were later prosecuted. Groups abroad and the Tiananmen Mothers, relatives of the dead, continue to call for an accounting."]],
sp:["1989, a year of change","The same year as Tiananmen, peaceful revolutions swept Eastern Europe. The Berlin Wall fell on 9 November 1989, and communist governments in Poland, Hungary, Czechoslovakia and elsewhere gave way. China’s leaders drew the opposite lesson, determined not to lose control."],
g:[["Martial law","Military rule imposed in an emergency"],["Hunger strike","Refusing food as a form of protest"],["Crackdown","Strong action to stop protests"],["Vigil","A gathering, often with candles, to remember people"]],
f:["The Goddess of Democracy statue stood for only five days before soldiers destroyed it.","No one knows for sure who Tank Man was.","Hong Kong held vigils for the victims every 4 June for 30 years."],
fun:"One of the famous Tank Man photos survived because the photographer hid his film in a hotel toilet cistern before police searched his room.",
tl:"Noong 1989, nagprotesta ang mga estudyante at manggagawa sa Tiananmen Square sa Beijing para sa reporma at kalayaan. Noong gabi ng 3–4 Hunyo, nilinis ng hukbo ang lugar at marami ang napatay; hanggang ngayon ay mahigpit na sinasensor ang pangyayari sa China."});

ADDART({id:"chernobyl",t:"The Chernobyl Disaster",c:"History",i:"☢️",w:"Chernobyl disaster",r:["nuclear","fission","ussrfall","coldwar","radioact"],
p:[["The worst nuclear accident","In April 1986, a reactor exploded at the Chernobyl nuclear power plant in Ukraine."],["Radiation spreads","Radioactive material spread across Europe."],["An exclusion zone","A large area around the plant is still closed to most people."]],
l:"The Chernobyl disaster was the worst accident in the history of nuclear power. Early on 26 April 1986, during a safety test, reactor number 4 at the Chernobyl power plant in Soviet Ukraine went out of control and exploded, starting a fire that burned for ten days. Radioactive material spread over Ukraine, Belarus and Russia and was detected across Europe. Two workers died in the explosion, and 28 firefighters and plant staff died within months from radiation sickness. More than 300,000 people were eventually moved from their homes, including all 49,000 residents of the nearby town of Pripyat. The accident was caused by a flawed reactor design together with operators breaking safety rules. The Soviet government at first hid the accident, and its handling damaged trust in the system. A huge steel arch, the New Safe Confinement, was slid over the ruined reactor in 2016.",
s:[["The accident","Operators were running a test to see whether the turbines could power the cooling pumps during a power cut. With safety systems switched off and the reactor in an unstable state, a sudden power surge caused steam explosions that blew the heavy steel and concrete lid off the reactor. Burning graphite threw radioactive material high into the air."],["The response","Firefighters arrived without proper protection. The town of Pripyat was evacuated only 36 hours later, on 27 April. Sweden detected high radiation on 28 April, forcing the Soviet Union to admit the accident. Hundreds of thousands of workers, called liquidators, later helped clean up, and a concrete ‘sarcophagus’ was built over the reactor within months."],["Health effects","Thousands of cases of thyroid cancer in people who were children at the time are linked to radioactive iodine, often from contaminated milk. Most were treated successfully. The total number of deaths caused by Chernobyl is disputed; United Nations agencies estimated up to about 4,000 eventual deaths among the most exposed groups, while some other studies give higher figures."],["The zone today","A 30-kilometer exclusion zone surrounds the plant. Wildlife such as wolves, elk and Przewalski’s horses has flourished in the absence of people. The plant’s last reactor closed in 2000. In 2022 Russian troops occupied the site for about five weeks, and in February 2025 a drone hit the New Safe Confinement, damaging its outer shell; inspectors later said the arch could no longer fully confine radioactive material until it is repaired."]],
k:[["Date","26 April 1986"],["Place","Chernobyl (Chornobyl), Ukraine"],["Reactor","Unit 4, RBMK type"],["Early deaths","30 (2 in the blast, 28 from radiation sickness)"],["Evacuated","Over 300,000 people eventually"],["Exclusion zone","About 30 km radius"]],
d:[["1977","Chernobyl’s first reactor starts operating"],["1986","Reactor 4 explodes on 26 April"],["1986","Sarcophagus completed in November"],["2000","Last reactor shut down"],["2016","New Safe Confinement moved into place"],["2022","Russian forces occupy the site for five weeks"],["2025","Drone strike damages the confinement arch"]],
s2:[["The New Safe Confinement","The arch is 108 meters high and 257 meters wide, large enough to cover a sports stadium. It was built beside the reactor and slid over it on rails in November 2016, then sealed in 2019. It was designed to last 100 years while the old reactor is dismantled."],["Chernobyl and Fukushima","The only other accident rated at the highest level, 7, on the international scale is Fukushima in Japan in 2011, caused by an earthquake and tsunami. Both disasters led some countries to rethink nuclear power, while others improved safety and kept building reactors."]],
sp:["Pripyat, the ghost town","Pripyat was a modern town built in 1970 for plant workers, with a funfair due to open on 1 May 1986. Its yellow Ferris wheel, never used by the public, has become a symbol of the disaster. Visitors with permits can tour the town on guided trips."],
g:[["Radiation","Energy given off by unstable atoms that can harm living cells"],["Reactor","The machine in which nuclear fission produces heat"],["Liquidator","A worker who cleaned up after the accident"],["Exclusion zone","An area closed to the public for safety"]],
f:["Pripyat was evacuated the day after the explosion.","Sweden detected Chernobyl’s radiation before the Soviet Union announced the accident.","Wolves, bears and wild horses now roam the exclusion zone."],
fun:"Scientists have found black fungi growing on the walls inside the ruined reactor that seem to thrive on radiation.",
tl:"Noong 26 Abril 1986, sumabog ang isang reaktor sa planta ng Chernobyl sa Ukraine, ang pinakamalalang aksidente sa kasaysayan ng nuclear power. Kumalat ang radyasyon sa Europa, inilikas ang mahigit 300,000 tao, at nananatiling sarado ang malaking lugar sa paligid nito."});

ADDART({id:"genocide",t:"Genocide",c:"History",i:"🕯️",w:"Genocide",r:["holocaust","hrights","un","polpot","rwandacountry","antisemitism"],
p:[["The worst crime","Genocide is the deliberate attempt to destroy a national, ethnic, racial or religious group."],["A word from 1944","The word was invented by lawyer Raphael Lemkin after the Holocaust."],["Never again","International law now requires countries to prevent and punish genocide."]],
l:"Genocide is the deliberate attempt to destroy, in whole or in part, a national, ethnic, racial or religious group. The word was coined in 1944 by Raphael Lemkin, a Polish-Jewish lawyer who lost much of his family in the Holocaust, by combining the Greek genos (‘race’ or ‘people’) with the Latin -cide (‘killing’). In 1948 the United Nations adopted the Genocide Convention, which makes genocide a crime under international law and obliges countries to prevent and punish it. Genocide includes not only mass killing but also causing serious harm, imposing conditions meant to destroy a group, preventing births and forcibly taking children. Events widely recognized as genocides include the Armenian genocide of 1915–16, the Holocaust, the killings in Cambodia under the Khmer Rouge, the 1994 genocide of Tutsi in Rwanda and the 1995 Srebrenica massacre in Bosnia.",
s:[["Defining genocide","What makes genocide different from other mass crimes is intent: the aim of destroying a group as such. Proving this intent in court is difficult, so international courts often convict people of crimes against humanity or war crimes instead. Political and social groups are not covered by the convention, which Lemkin and others regretted."],["Twentieth-century genocides","In 1915–16, the Ottoman government deported and massacred about one million or more Armenians; Turkey disputes that it was genocide. Nazi Germany murdered six million Jews in the Holocaust, along with Roma and others. In Rwanda in 1994, extremists killed about 800,000 people, mostly Tutsi, in about 100 days. In July 1995, Bosnian Serb forces killed about 8,000 Bosniak men and boys at Srebrenica."],["Courts and justice","Special tribunals for the former Yugoslavia and Rwanda were set up in 1993 and 1994. In 1998 the Rwanda tribunal convicted Jean-Paul Akayesu, the first conviction for genocide by an international court. The International Criminal Court, created in 2002, can try individuals, while the International Court of Justice hears cases between states. Cases at the International Court of Justice have included The Gambia’s case against Myanmar over the Rohingya and South Africa’s case against Israel over Gaza, which Israel rejects."],["Prevention","Scholars describe warning signs, such as propaganda calling a group vermin or a threat, laws removing their rights, and organized militias. The United Nations has a special adviser on the prevention of genocide, and in 2005 world leaders accepted a ‘responsibility to protect’ people from mass atrocities, although acting on it remains controversial."]],
k:[["Word coined","1944, by Raphael Lemkin"],["Genocide Convention","Adopted 9 December 1948"],["Key element","Intent to destroy a group"],["First international conviction","Jean-Paul Akayesu, 1998"],["Courts","ICC, ICJ, special tribunals"],["Day of remembrance","9 December (UN)"]],
d:[["1915–16","Armenian genocide"],["1941–45","The Holocaust"],["1944","Lemkin coins the word ‘genocide’"],["1948","UN adopts the Genocide Convention"],["1975–79","Mass killings in Cambodia"],["1994","Genocide against the Tutsi in Rwanda"],["1995","Srebrenica massacre"],["1998","First international genocide conviction"]],
s2:[["Stages of genocide","The scholar Gregory Stanton described stages that often lead to genocide, including classifying people into ‘us and them’, dehumanizing a group, organizing killers, polarizing society and denying the crimes afterwards. Recognizing early stages can help prevent violence."],["Denial","Denying that a genocide happened hurts survivors and can make future crimes more likely. Several countries have laws against denying the Holocaust, and recognition of the Armenian genocide is a major diplomatic issue between Turkey and other countries."]],
sp:["Rwanda’s recovery","After the 1994 genocide, Rwanda held community courts called gacaca, where more than a million cases were heard in villages, often with confessions and requests for forgiveness. Each April, Rwanda holds 100 days of remembrance called Kwibuka, meaning ‘to remember’."],
g:[["Genocide","Acts meant to destroy a national, ethnic, racial or religious group"],["Crimes against humanity","Widespread attacks on civilians, such as murder or enslavement"],["Intent","What a person meant to do"],["Tribunal","A special court"]],
f:["The word genocide is younger than the airplane.","The Genocide Convention was the first human rights treaty adopted by the United Nations.","Rwanda’s genocide lasted about 100 days."],
fun:"Raphael Lemkin, who invented the word genocide, was nominated for the Nobel Peace Prize several times.",
tl:"Ang henosidyo ay sinadyang pagtatangkang puksain ang isang pambansa, etniko, panlahi o panrelihiyong grupo. Nilikha ang salita noong 1944, at ginawa itong krimen sa pandaigdigang batas ng Kombensiyon ng UN noong 1948."});

ADDART({id:"antisemitism",t:"Antisemitism",c:"History",i:"🕯️",w:"Antisemitism",r:["holocaust","judaism","hitler","conspiracy","hrights"],
p:[["Hatred of Jews","Antisemitism is prejudice, hostility or discrimination against Jewish people."],["An ancient hatred","It has existed for more than 2,000 years and takes many forms."],["Still a problem","Antisemitic incidents remain a concern in many countries today."]],
l:"Antisemitism is hostility, prejudice or discrimination against Jews as a people or religious group. It is one of the oldest forms of hatred. In the ancient world Jews were sometimes persecuted for refusing to worship other gods. In medieval Christian Europe, Jews were falsely blamed for the death of Jesus, accused of poisoning wells during the Black Death and of ritual murder, forced into ghettos and expelled from countries including England in 1290 and Spain in 1492. In the 19th century, a racial form of antisemitism claimed Jews were a separate and dangerous race. The forged Protocols of the Elders of Zion spread the conspiracy theory of a secret Jewish plan to rule the world. Nazi Germany made antisemitism state policy and murdered six million Jews in the Holocaust. Today, governments and organizations monitor antisemitic incidents and teach about the Holocaust to combat it.",
s:[["Religious hostility","For centuries, many Christians blamed Jews collectively for the crucifixion of Jesus, a teaching the Catholic Church formally rejected in 1965. Jews were barred from many jobs, so some worked as moneylenders, which then fed stereotypes about money. In 1096 crusaders massacred Jewish communities along the Rhine."],["Expulsions and ghettos","England expelled its Jews in 1290, France several times in the 14th century and Spain in 1492. In 1516 Venice created a walled Jewish quarter called the Ghetto, a word later used for such districts elsewhere. In the Russian Empire, violent attacks called pogroms struck Jewish communities in the 1880s and early 1900s."],["Racial antisemitism","The word ‘antisemitism’ became popular in Germany around 1879. It presented hatred of Jews as science, claiming Jews were a race rather than a religion. In France, the Dreyfus Affair of 1894–1906, when a Jewish army officer was falsely convicted of treason, divided the country."],["After the Holocaust","After 1945, open antisemitism became less acceptable, but it did not disappear. Holocaust denial, conspiracy theories online and attacks on synagogues continue. Debates also arise about where criticism of Israeli government policy ends and antisemitism begins; most definitions say criticizing a government is not antisemitic, while holding all Jews responsible for it is."]],
k:[["Meaning","Hostility or prejudice against Jews"],["Word popularized","About 1879, Germany"],["Famous forgery","The Protocols of the Elders of Zion (1903)"],["Worst result","The Holocaust (1941–45)"],["Remembrance day","27 January, International Holocaust Remembrance Day"]],
d:[["1096","Crusaders attack Jewish communities in the Rhineland"],["1290","Jews expelled from England"],["1492","Jews expelled from Spain"],["1516","Venice creates the Ghetto"],["1894","Dreyfus Affair begins in France"],["1903","The Protocols forgery is published in Russia"],["1938","Kristallnacht attacks in Germany"],["2005","UN declares International Holocaust Remembrance Day"]],
s2:[["The Protocols forgery","The Protocols of the Elders of Zion claimed to record secret Jewish plans for world domination. In 1921 the London Times showed it had been copied largely from a French satire about Napoleon III that had nothing to do with Jews. Despite being exposed as a fake, it was used by the Nazis and is still spread today."],["Fighting prejudice","Ways of countering antisemitism include education about Jewish history and the Holocaust, visits to memorials, laws against hate crimes and interfaith dialogue. Many historians say learning to recognize old stereotypes helps people resist all forms of prejudice."]],
sp:["Anne Frank","Anne Frank was a Jewish girl who hid with her family in a secret annex in Amsterdam from 1942 until they were discovered in 1944. She died in the Bergen-Belsen camp in 1945. Her diary, published by her father in 1947, has been translated into more than 70 languages."],
g:[["Prejudice","A negative opinion formed without knowledge or reason"],["Pogrom","A violent attack on a Jewish community"],["Ghetto","A district where a minority group is forced to live"],["Stereotype","An oversimplified idea about a group of people"]],
f:["The word ‘ghetto’ comes from a district of Venice.","The Protocols of the Elders of Zion was proven to be a forgery in 1921.","27 January marks the day Auschwitz was liberated in 1945."],
fun:"Anne Frank received her famous diary as a present on her 13th birthday.",
tl:"Ang antisemitismo ay poot, pagkiling o diskriminasyon laban sa mga Hudyo. Umiral ito nang mahigit 2,000 taon, at humantong ito sa Holocaust, kung saan anim na milyong Hudyo ang pinatay ng Nazi Germany."});

ADDART({id:"comfortwomen",t:"The Comfort Women",c:"History",i:"🕯️",w:"Comfort women",r:["ww2","pacificwar","japocc","hrights","japan"],
p:[["Wartime abuse","‘Comfort women’ were women and girls forced into sexual slavery by Japan’s military during World War II."],["From many countries","They came from Korea, China, the Philippines, Indonesia and elsewhere."],["Breaking the silence","Survivors began speaking out in the 1990s to demand justice."]],
l:"‘Comfort women’ is the term used for women and girls forced into sexual slavery by the Imperial Japanese Army before and during World War II, from the early 1930s until 1945. The army set up ‘comfort stations’ in occupied areas, and women were recruited by deception, sold or taken by force. Estimates of their number range from about 20,000 to 200,000 or more. Many came from Korea, then a Japanese colony, as well as China, the Philippines, Taiwan, Indonesia, Malaysia and other occupied lands, with some Dutch women in Indonesia. Many died, and survivors often lived for decades in silence and shame. In 1991 the Korean survivor Kim Hak-sun testified publicly, encouraging others to come forward. Japan issued an apology in 1993 and later set up funds for survivors, but the issue of official responsibility and compensation remains a source of tension in East Asia.",
s:[["The comfort stations","The first military brothels were set up in Shanghai in 1932. As the war spread, the army established hundreds of comfort stations across Asia and the Pacific. Military documents show that the army planned and controlled the system, saying it would prevent soldiers attacking local women and spreading disease."],["The women’s experiences","Many of the women were teenagers. They were held under guard, beaten and abused, and some were killed. At the end of the war, some were abandoned far from home. Survivors suffered lasting injuries, illness and trauma, and many never married or spoke about what happened."],["Speaking out","On 14 August 1991, Kim Hak-sun became the first survivor to testify publicly in South Korea. In 1992, Maria Rosa Henson became the first Filipina survivor to come forward, followed by many others in groups such as Lila Pilipina. In 2000, a citizens’ tribunal in Tokyo heard survivor testimony from across Asia."],["Apologies and disputes","In 1993 Japan’s chief cabinet secretary Yōhei Kōno acknowledged the army’s involvement and apologized. Japan set up the Asian Women’s Fund in 1995, though many survivors rejected it because it was funded largely by private donations. A 2015 agreement between Japan and South Korea was meant to settle the issue but was criticized by survivors. Statues of a seated girl commemorating the women have been placed in Seoul and many other cities, sometimes causing diplomatic protests; one in Manila was removed in 2018."]],
k:[["Period","About 1932–1945"],["Estimated number","About 20,000 to 200,000 or more"],["First public testimony","Kim Hak-sun, 14 August 1991"],["Japanese apology","Kōno Statement, 1993"],["Remembrance day","14 August, International Memorial Day for Comfort Women"]],
d:[["1932","First military comfort stations in Shanghai"],["1937–45","System spreads across occupied Asia"],["1991","Kim Hak-sun testifies publicly"],["1992","Maria Rosa Henson comes forward in the Philippines"],["1993","Kōno Statement apologizes"],["1995","Asian Women’s Fund set up"],["2015","Japan–South Korea agreement"]],
s2:[["Historical evidence","In 1992 the historian Yoshimi Yoshiaki found documents in Japan’s defense agency library showing the army’s role in setting up comfort stations. Some Japanese politicians have disputed whether women were forcibly recruited, which has angered survivors and neighboring countries."],["Why the term is controversial","The phrase ‘comfort women’ is a translation of a Japanese euphemism. Many survivors and historians prefer to call them survivors of military sexual slavery, while the term remains in use because it is widely recognized."]],
sp:["Lola Rosa","Maria Rosa Henson, known as Lola Rosa, was taken by Japanese soldiers in 1943 at age 15. Her decision to speak publicly in 1992 inspired other Filipina survivors, called lolas (grandmothers). Her autobiography, Comfort Woman: Slave of Destiny, was published in 1996."],
g:[["Sexual slavery","Forcing people into sexual abuse while holding them captive"],["Testimony","A formal account of what someone experienced"],["Euphemism","A mild word used in place of a harsh one"],["Survivor","A person who lived through a terrible experience"]],
f:["The issue was largely hidden for almost 50 years after the war.","Survivors held weekly protests outside the Japanese embassy in Seoul starting in 1992.","Statues of a seated girl honor the women in several countries."],
fun:"The Wednesday protests in Seoul, held every week since January 1992, are among the longest-running regular demonstrations in the world.",
tl:"Ang ‘comfort women’ ay mga babae at batang babae na sapilitang ginawang alipin sa sekswal na pang-aabuso ng hukbong Hapon noong Ikalawang Digmaang Pandaigdig. Galing sila sa Korea, China, Pilipinas at iba pang bansa, at nagsimula silang magsalita noong dekada 1990 upang humingi ng katarungan."});

ADDART({id:"colonialism",t:"Colonialism and Its Legacy",c:"History",i:"🗺️",w:"Colonialism",r:["decol","slavetrade","spanph","explor","independence"],
p:[["Empires overseas","Colonialism is when one country takes control of another land and its people."],["Europe’s empires","From the 1400s to the 1900s, European powers ruled much of the world."],["A debated legacy","Its effects on borders, economies and cultures are still debated."]],
l:"Colonialism is the practice of one country taking control of another territory, settling it or ruling its people, and using its land and resources. Empires have existed throughout history, but the term usually refers to the overseas empires built by European powers, beginning with Portugal and Spain in the 15th century and followed by the Netherlands, Britain, France and others. By the early 20th century, European countries, joined by the United States and Japan, controlled most of Africa, South and Southeast Asia, the Pacific and the Caribbean. Colonial rule brought railways, schools and new crops to some places, but also conquest, forced labor, slavery, famine, the loss of land and the suppression of local cultures. Most colonies won independence between 1945 and 1975. Debates continue about colonialism’s legacy, including borders drawn by outsiders, economic inequality, apologies, reparations and the return of objects held in museums.",
s:[["The age of empires","After Columbus reached the Americas in 1492, Spain and Portugal divided the newly reached lands between them in the Treaty of Tordesillas (1494). European diseases, conquest and forced labor caused a catastrophic collapse of the Indigenous population of the Americas. The Atlantic slave trade carried about 12.5 million Africans across the ocean to work on plantations."],["Companies and colonies","Trading companies such as the British East India Company (1600) and the Dutch East India Company (1602) had their own armies and ruled territories. Britain took over the government of India from its company in 1858. Settler colonies such as Australia, Canada and South Africa saw large numbers of Europeans settle on Indigenous land."],["The Scramble for Africa","At the Berlin Conference of 1884–85, European powers set rules for claiming Africa, and by 1914 only Ethiopia and Liberia remained independent. In the Congo Free State, the personal colony of King Leopold II of Belgium, forced labor to collect rubber caused millions of deaths."],["Legacy debates","Some people stress benefits such as infrastructure, education and legal systems; many historians argue these mainly served the colonizers and came at enormous cost. Former colonies often inherited borders that split peoples or forced rivals together. In recent years, countries such as Germany and the Netherlands have apologized for colonial crimes, and museums have begun returning objects, such as the Benin Bronzes, to their countries of origin."]],
k:[["Main period","15th–20th centuries"],["Largest empire","British Empire, about a quarter of the world’s land by 1920"],["Berlin Conference","1884–85"],["Main era of independence","1945–1975"],["Related term","Imperialism"]],
d:[["1494","Treaty of Tordesillas divides lands between Spain and Portugal"],["1600","British East India Company founded"],["1858","British Crown takes over rule of India"],["1884–85","Berlin Conference on Africa"],["1947","India and Pakistan win independence"],["1960","‘Year of Africa’: 17 African countries become independent"],["2022","Germany returns Benin Bronzes to Nigeria"]],
s2:[["Colonialism and language","Colonial rule spread European languages around the world. English, French, Spanish and Portuguese are official languages in dozens of former colonies, linking them to world trade and science but sometimes pushing aside local languages."],["Neocolonialism","Some thinkers argue that powerful countries and companies still control poorer nations through debt, trade and investment, a situation called neocolonialism. Others say this term is overused and that former colonies make their own choices."]],
sp:["The Benin Bronzes","The Benin Bronzes are thousands of brass and ivory sculptures from the Kingdom of Benin, in today’s Nigeria. British troops took them in 1897, and they were sold to museums worldwide. Since 2021, museums in Germany, the United States and elsewhere have begun returning them."],
g:[["Colony","A territory ruled by another country"],["Imperialism","Extending a country’s power over other lands"],["Settler colony","A colony where large numbers of colonists settle"],["Reparations","Payments to make up for past wrongs"]],
f:["By 1914, only two African countries were independent.","The British Empire was once called ‘the empire on which the sun never sets’.","Most of today’s countries were once colonies or colonial powers."],
fun:"The board game Risk, about conquering the world, was invented in France in 1957, just as Europe’s empires were breaking up.",
tl:"Ang kolonyalismo ay pagsakop ng isang bansa sa ibang lupain at mga tao nito. Mula 1400s hanggang 1900s, pinamahalaan ng mga kapangyarihang Europeo ang malaking bahagi ng mundo, at pinagtatalunan pa rin ang epekto nito sa mga hangganan, ekonomiya at kultura."});

ADDART({id:"eugenics",t:"Eugenics",c:"History",i:"🧬",w:"Eugenics",r:["mendel","holocaust","crispr","hrights","evolution"],
p:[["A discredited idea","Eugenics was the idea of ‘improving’ humans by controlling who could have children."],["Forced sterilization","Many countries sterilized people against their will in the 20th century."],["Lessons today","Its history shapes debates about genetic testing and gene editing."]],
l:"Eugenics was a movement that aimed to ‘improve’ the human population by encouraging people considered fit to have children and preventing those considered unfit from doing so. The word was coined in 1883 by the English scientist Francis Galton, a cousin of Charles Darwin, from Greek words meaning ‘good birth’. In the early 20th century eugenics was popular among scientists, politicians and reformers in many countries. It led to laws allowing the forced sterilization of tens of thousands of people judged disabled, mentally ill, criminal or poor, especially in the United States, Sweden and Germany, and to immigration limits based on race. Nazi Germany took eugenics to its most extreme, sterilizing about 400,000 people and murdering around 70,000 disabled people in the T4 program before the Holocaust. Today eugenics is rejected as unscientific and a violation of human rights.",
s:[["Origins","Galton studied families of famous men and wrongly concluded that talent and character were mainly inherited. After Mendel’s laws of heredity were rediscovered in 1900, eugenicists claimed complex traits such as intelligence, poverty or crime were passed down simply in genes, which modern genetics shows is false."],["Eugenics in practice","Indiana passed the first compulsory sterilization law in 1907, and more than 30 US states followed; around 60,000 people were sterilized. In the 1927 case Buck v. Bell, the US Supreme Court upheld forced sterilization. Sweden sterilized about 63,000 people between 1934 and 1976, and similar laws existed in Canada, Switzerland, Japan and elsewhere."],["Nazi Germany","Germany’s 1933 law for the prevention of hereditarily diseased offspring led to around 400,000 sterilizations. From 1939, the secret T4 program murdered disabled children and adults in gas chambers and by other means; the methods and staff were later used in the death camps of the Holocaust."],["Apologies and lessons","After 1945, eugenics lost scientific respect, though some sterilizations continued for decades. Governments including Sweden, several US states and Japan have apologized or paid compensation to victims. Modern debates about prenatal testing, embryo selection and gene editing often refer to this history, while emphasizing individual choice rather than state control."]],
k:[["Word coined","1883, Francis Galton"],["Meaning","‘Good birth’ (Greek)"],["First sterilization law","Indiana, USA, 1907"],["Nazi sterilizations","About 400,000"],["Status today","Rejected as unscientific and abusive"]],
d:[["1883","Galton coins the word"],["1907","Indiana passes the first forced sterilization law"],["1927","Buck v. Bell upholds forced sterilization in the USA"],["1933","Nazi Germany passes its sterilization law"],["1939","T4 program begins murdering disabled people"],["1976","Sweden ends forced sterilization"],["2019","Japan passes a law to compensate victims"]],
s2:[["Why it was popular","Many supporters of eugenics thought they were being modern and scientific, and some were also reformers in other areas. They feared that society was ‘degenerating’. Their ideas were shaped by racism and class prejudice and by misunderstanding how heredity works."],["Gene editing today","In 2018 the Chinese scientist He Jiankui announced the birth of twin girls whose genes he had edited as embryos. Scientists worldwide condemned the experiment, and he was jailed. The case revived questions about who should decide which genes are desirable."]],
sp:["Carrie Buck","Carrie Buck was a young woman in Virginia sterilized in 1927 after being labelled ‘feeble-minded’. Later research showed she had average intelligence and had become pregnant after being assaulted. Her case, Buck v. Bell, has never been formally overturned, but it is now widely condemned."],
g:[["Sterilization","An operation that makes a person unable to have children"],["Heredity","The passing of traits from parents to children"],["Genetics","The study of genes and heredity"],["Compulsory","Required by law, not voluntary"]],
f:["The word eugenics was invented by Darwin’s cousin.","Some US states continued forced sterilizations into the 1970s.","Modern genetics shows traits like intelligence depend on many genes and the environment."],
fun:"Francis Galton also pioneered weather maps and the study of fingerprints.",
tl:"Ang eugenics ay kilusang naglalayong ‘pahusayin’ ang tao sa pagkontrol kung sino ang maaaring magkaanak. Humantong ito sa sapilitang isterilisasyon sa maraming bansa at sa pagpatay ng Nazi Germany sa mga taong may kapansanan; itinatakwil na ito ngayon bilang hindi siyentipiko at labag sa karapatang pantao."});

