// New articles, 15 November 2026 (part 3): controversial chapters in recent Philippine history.
ADDART({id:"phdrugwar",t:"The Philippine Drug War",c:"Philippines",i:"⚖️",w:"Philippine drug war",r:["drugtrade","martiallaw","hrights","police","phgov"],
p:[["A deadly campaign","President Rodrigo Duterte’s anti-drug campaign ran from 2016 to 2022."],["Thousands killed","Official figures count over 6,000 deaths in police operations; rights groups estimate far more."],["On trial","Duterte was arrested in 2025 and faces trial at the International Criminal Court."]],
l:"The Philippine drug war was the anti-drug campaign led by Rodrigo Duterte, president of the Philippines from 2016 to 2022. Duterte, who as mayor of Davao City had been linked to killings of suspected criminals, won the 2016 election promising to end illegal drugs, especially methamphetamine, known locally as shabu, within months. Police carried out door-to-door operations called Oplan Tokhang and raids across the country. The government said more than 6,000 people were killed in police operations by 2022, mostly suspects it said had fought back; human rights groups estimated that between 12,000 and 30,000 people died, including victims of unidentified gunmen. Supporters praised the campaign for making streets feel safer, and Duterte remained popular. Critics, the Catholic Church and human rights groups condemned extrajudicial killings, mostly of poor people. In March 2025, Duterte was arrested on a warrant of the International Criminal Court, which confirmed charges of crimes against humanity in April 2026.",
s:[["The campaign","After Duterte took office on 30 June 2016, police compiled lists of suspected users and pushers and visited homes asking them to surrender; over a million people did. Operations were often deadly, and bodies were found in streets with cardboard signs labelling them drug dealers. Duterte publicly encouraged police and said he would protect officers who killed suspects."],["Kian delos Santos","On 16 August 2017, police in Caloocan shot 17-year-old Kian delos Santos. Security camera footage contradicted their claim that he had fired at them, and the case caused national outrage. In November 2018, three police officers were convicted of murder, one of the few cases to reach a conviction."],["Support and criticism","Many Filipinos supported the campaign, which polls showed was popular, believing it reduced crime and drug use. Critics, including Senator Leila de Lima, the Commission on Human Rights, the Catholic Church and journalists such as those at Rappler, documented abuses. De Lima was jailed in 2017 on drug charges she called false and was later acquitted of all of them."],["The International Criminal Court","The ICC prosecutor began examining the killings in 2018, and Duterte withdrew the Philippines from the court, effective March 2019. Judges authorized an investigation in 2021 covering the time the country was a member. On 11 March 2025, Philippine authorities arrested Duterte in Manila and flew him to The Hague. After judges ruled him fit to stand trial, they held a hearing in February 2026 and on 23 April 2026 confirmed charges of murder and attempted murder as crimes against humanity. His trial was scheduled to open on 30 November 2026. He denies the charges."]],
k:[["President","Rodrigo Duterte (2016–2022)"],["Main operation","Oplan Tokhang"],["Deaths in police operations (official)","Over 6,000"],["Estimated total deaths","12,000–30,000 (rights groups)"],["Arrested on ICC warrant","11 March 2025"],["ICC charges confirmed","23 April 2026"]],
d:[["2016","Duterte becomes president and launches the drug war"],["2017","Killing of Kian delos Santos"],["2018","ICC prosecutor opens a preliminary examination"],["2019","Philippine withdrawal from the ICC takes effect"],["2021","ICC judges authorize an investigation"],["2022","Duterte leaves office"],["2025","Duterte arrested and taken to The Hague"],["2026","ICC confirms charges; trial set for 30 November"]],
s2:[["Families of victims","Many of those killed were poor men from crowded city neighborhoods, often the main earners in their families. Widows, mothers and children have formed support groups, and church groups have helped families bury their dead and seek justice. Victims’ families have taken part in the ICC case."],["Debates about the drug war","Supporters argue that drugs were destroying communities and that strong action was needed. Opponents argue that killing suspects without trial breaks the rule of law, that small users rather than drug lords were the main victims, and that treatment and addressing poverty are better solutions."]],
sp:["Davao City","Before becoming president, Duterte was mayor of Davao City for over 20 years. Human rights groups linked a so-called Davao Death Squad to killings of suspected criminals there. Even while detained in The Hague, Duterte was elected mayor of Davao again in May 2025."],
g:[["Extrajudicial killing","Killing by state agents without a legal trial"],["Tokhang","From the Cebuano words for ‘knock’ and ‘plead’"],["Crimes against humanity","Widespread or systematic attacks on civilians"],["Warrant","A legal order to arrest someone"]],
f:["Tokhang combines the Cebuano words toktok (knock) and hangyo (plead).","Over a million people surrendered to authorities in the campaign’s first months.","Duterte is the first former Asian head of state to be tried at the ICC."],
fun:"Duterte was arrested at Manila’s airport as he returned from a trip to Hong Kong, and was on a plane to The Hague the same night.",
tl:"Ang drug war sa Pilipinas ay kampanya kontra droga ni Pangulong Rodrigo Duterte mula 2016 hanggang 2022. Libo-libo ang napatay, at inaresto si Duterte noong 2025 sa utos ng International Criminal Court, na nagtakda ng paglilitis sa kaniya."});

ADDART({id:"phdynasties",t:"Political Dynasties in the Philippines",c:"Philippines",i:"🏛️",w:"Political dynasties in the Philippines",r:["phgov","elections","democ","constitution","marcos","barangay"],
p:[["Family politics","Political dynasties are families whose members hold many elected posts."],["Banned on paper","The 1987 Constitution says the state should prohibit them, but no law did for decades."],["A first step","In 2026 the House passed an anti-dynasty bill for the first time."]],
l:"A political dynasty is a family whose members hold elected offices, often at the same time or one after another, keeping power within the family for generations. In the Philippines, political families dominate many provinces, cities and towns, and many members of Congress have relatives in other elected posts. The 1987 Constitution states that the state shall ‘prohibit political dynasties as may be defined by law’, but for nearly four decades Congress, whose members included many from political families, did not pass such a law. Supporters of families in politics say voters freely choose them and that experience and name recognition help get things done. Critics argue that dynasties limit competition, encourage corruption and patronage, and are linked to poverty in some regions. In June 2026 the House of Representatives passed an anti-dynasty bill for the first time; to become law it also needs the Senate’s approval.",
s:[["Roots","Powerful landowning families, sometimes called the principalía, held local power during the Spanish colonial era. Under American rule, elections from the early 1900s let these families win public office. After independence in 1946, families built networks of support, and some used private armies and violence to protect their power."],["Dynasties today","Studies by political scientists have found that a large majority of provinces have governors from political families and that ‘fat dynasties’, with many relatives in office at once, have grown. Term limits introduced in 1987 sometimes led politicians to pass their posts to spouses, children or siblings, then return after a break."],["The anti-dynasty bill","Anti-dynasty bills were filed in nearly every Congress after 1987 without becoming law. In June 2026, the House approved House Bill 8389 by 267 votes to 20, with 7 abstentions. It would bar spouses and relatives up to the second degree from holding or seeking elective posts in the same area at the same time. Critics said it was too weak because it allows relatives to succeed each other or hold offices in different areas."],["Arguments","Defenders say a ban would limit voters’ freedom and unfairly punish capable people for their surnames. Critics point to studies linking dynasties to slower development and say the Constitution clearly intended a ban. Some reformers argue that stronger political parties, campaign finance rules and anti-corruption measures are also needed."]],
k:[["Constitutional basis","1987 Constitution, Article II, Section 26"],["House anti-dynasty bill","HB 8389, passed 3 June 2026"],["House vote","267 yes, 20 no, 7 abstentions"],["Ban in the bill","Relatives up to the second degree, same area, same time"],["Next step","Senate approval"]],
d:[["1907","First elected Philippine Assembly"],["1946","Philippine independence"],["1987","New Constitution calls for a ban on political dynasties"],["2026","House passes anti-dynasty bill for the first time"]],
s2:[["Degrees of relation","In Philippine law, relatives are counted by degrees. Parents and children are first-degree relatives; grandparents, grandchildren, brothers and sisters are second-degree. The same rules apply to in-laws, called relatives by affinity."],["Dynasties elsewhere","Political families exist in many democracies, such as the Kennedys and Bushes in the United States, the Nehru–Gandhi family in India and political families across Asia and Latin America. The Philippines stands out for how common they are at the local level."]],
sp:["Why the Constitution says ‘as may be defined by law’","The framers of the 1987 Constitution agreed that dynasties should be banned but left it to Congress to define them. The Supreme Court has said this provision is not self-executing, which means it has no effect until Congress passes a law."],
g:[["Dynasty","A family that holds power for generations"],["Patronage","Giving jobs or favors in exchange for political support"],["Term limit","A rule limiting how long a person can hold office"],["Affinity","Relation by marriage"]],
f:["The 1987 Constitution calls for a ban on political dynasties.","Anti-dynasty bills were filed for decades before one passed the House in 2026.","Second-degree relatives include grandparents, grandchildren and siblings."],
fun:"Some Philippine provinces have had a governor from the same family for more than half a century.",
tl:"Ang political dynasty ay pamilyang maraming kasapi ang humahawak ng mga halal na posisyon. Sinasabi ng Saligang Batas ng 1987 na dapat itong ipagbawal ayon sa batas, at noong Hunyo 2026, ipinasa ng Kamara sa unang pagkakataon ang isang anti-dynasty bill."});

ADDART({id:"maguindanao",t:"The Maguindanao Massacre",c:"Philippines",i:"🕯️",w:"Maguindanao massacre",r:["journalism","mindanao","elections","phdynasties","hrights"],
p:[["A political killing","On 23 November 2009, 58 people were killed in Maguindanao, Mindanao."],["Journalists targeted","Thirty-two of the victims were journalists, making it the deadliest single attack on the press ever recorded."],["Justice after ten years","In 2019 a court convicted leaders of the powerful Ampatuan clan."]],
l:"The Maguindanao massacre, also called the Ampatuan massacre, was the killing of 58 people on 23 November 2009 in the town of Ampatuan, Maguindanao province, in Mindanao. The victims were traveling in a convoy to file the election candidacy of Esmael Mangudadatu, a politician challenging the powerful Ampatuan family, which controlled the province. Armed men stopped the convoy and killed everyone, including Mangudadatu’s wife and relatives, lawyers, drivers, passers-by and 32 journalists who had come to cover the event. The Committee to Protect Journalists called it the deadliest single attack on journalists in history. President Gloria Macapagal Arroyo, an ally of the Ampatuans, declared martial law in the province. After a trial lasting ten years, a court in December 2019 convicted clan leaders, including Andal Ampatuan Jr. and Zaldy Ampatuan, of murder and sentenced them to life imprisonment. The massacre became a symbol of political violence and impunity.",
s:[["The convoy","Mangudadatu, vice mayor of Buluan, had received threats and sent his wife and female relatives, believing women would not be harmed, along with journalists, to file his certificate of candidacy for governor. On the highway, about 100 armed men stopped the convoy and took the victims to a hilltop, where they were shot. Two passing vehicles whose passengers had nothing to do with the convoy were also attacked."],["The aftermath","A backhoe belonging to the provincial government was found at the site, where victims and vehicles had been buried in pits. Martial law was declared in Maguindanao on 4 December 2009, the first since the Marcos era, and was lifted eight days later. Large stores of weapons were found on Ampatuan properties."],["The trial","Nearly 200 people were charged. The trial, held in Quezon City, lasted ten years, and several witnesses were killed. Andal Ampatuan Sr., the clan patriarch, died in 2015 before a verdict. On 19 December 2019, Judge Jocelyn Solis-Reyes convicted 28 people as principals and sentenced them to life imprisonment without parole; 15 were convicted as accessories and 56 were acquitted. Dozens of suspects remained at large."],["Lasting impact","Press freedom groups mark 23 November each year, and the massacre inspired the International Day to End Impunity. It showed how private armies and family rivalries could fuel violence in Philippine local politics, and it led to calls for disbanding armed groups and protecting journalists. Esmael Mangudadatu won the 2010 election for governor."]],
k:[["Date","23 November 2009"],["Place","Ampatuan, Maguindanao, Mindanao"],["Victims","58, including 32 journalists"],["Accused leaders","The Ampatuan clan"],["Verdict","19 December 2019"],["Main sentence","Life imprisonment without parole"]],
d:[["2009","Massacre on 23 November"],["2009","Martial law in Maguindanao, 4–12 December"],["2010","Trial begins; Mangudadatu elected governor"],["2015","Andal Ampatuan Sr. dies"],["2019","Clan leaders convicted on 19 December"]],
s2:[["Danger for journalists","The Philippines has long been one of the most dangerous countries for journalists, especially local radio broadcasters in the provinces. Many killings have gone unpunished, which press groups call a culture of impunity."],["Private armies","Before the massacre, some powerful families kept armed groups made up of militiamen, police auxiliaries and others. A commission created after the massacre identified more than a hundred private armed groups across the country and recommended disbanding them."]],
sp:["The fight for justice","Families of the murdered journalists kept attending hearings for ten years, some traveling long distances from Mindanao to Manila. Journalists’ unions and media organizations followed the case closely, and the verdict was broadcast live on television."],
g:[["Massacre","The killing of many defenseless people"],["Impunity","Escaping punishment for a crime"],["Convoy","A group of vehicles traveling together"],["Accessory","A person who helps a crime without being the main offender"]],
f:["The massacre is the deadliest single attack on journalists ever recorded.","The trial took ten years.","Martial law was declared in Maguindanao for eight days after the massacre."],
fun:"The verdict in the Maguindanao massacre case ran to more than 700 pages.",
tl:"Noong 23 Nobyembre 2009, 58 katao ang pinatay sa Ampatuan, Maguindanao, kabilang ang 32 mamamahayag na kasama sa kombos ng kandidatong si Esmael Mangudadatu. Noong 2019, hinatulan ng korte ang mga pinuno ng angkang Ampatuan."});

;
