// New articles, 15 November 2026 (part 4): more difficult chapters of history.
ADDART({id:"kkk",t:"The Ku Klux Klan",c:"History",i:"🔥",w:"Ku Klux Klan",r:["segregation","racism","mlk","slavetrade","terrorism"],
p:[["A hate group","The Ku Klux Klan is an American white supremacist group founded in 1865."],["Terror and violence","It used threats, beatings, lynchings and bombings against Black Americans and others."],["Three waves","It rose and fell three times; today it is small but still exists."]],
l:"The Ku Klux Klan, often called the KKK or the Klan, is a white supremacist hate group that has existed in three main waves in the United States. The first Klan was founded in Pulaski, Tennessee, in December 1865 by six former Confederate soldiers after the Civil War. Wearing robes and masks, its members terrorized formerly enslaved Black people and their white allies to stop them voting and gaining rights, until federal laws crushed it in the early 1870s. A second Klan, founded in 1915, grew into a mass movement of millions in the 1920s, targeting Black Americans, immigrants, Catholics and Jews. A third wave fought violently against the civil rights movement in the 1950s and 1960s, including the bombing of a Birmingham church in 1963 that killed four girls. Today the Klan is split into small groups with a few thousand members, but it remains a symbol of racist terror.",
s:[["The first Klan","After the Civil War ended slavery, the Klan used night raids, whippings, arson and murder to keep Black Americans from voting, owning land or going to school during Reconstruction. Congress passed the Enforcement Acts, including the Ku Klux Klan Act of 1871, which allowed federal troops and courts to act, and the first Klan collapsed."],["The second Klan","In 1915, inspired by the film The Birth of a Nation, which glorified the first Klan, William J. Simmons revived the group at Stone Mountain, Georgia. It introduced the burning cross and became a nationwide organization with millions of members by the mid-1920s, holding marches in Washington and influencing politics in several states. Scandals, including the conviction of a leader for murder, caused its collapse by the late 1920s."],["Fighting civil rights","In the 1950s and 1960s, Klan groups attacked civil rights workers. On 15 September 1963, Klansmen bombed the 16th Street Baptist Church in Birmingham, Alabama, killing four girls. In 1964, three young civil rights workers were murdered in Mississippi. Many of these crimes went unpunished for decades; some killers were convicted in the 1990s and 2000s."],["The Klan today","Lawsuits by civil rights groups have bankrupted several Klan organizations. Today the Klan is fragmented and much smaller than other extremist movements, though its symbols are still used to intimidate. Its robes and burning crosses are banned or restricted in some places as hate symbols."]],
k:[["Founded","December 1865, Pulaski, Tennessee"],["Ideology","White supremacy"],["Peak membership","Millions, mid-1920s"],["Symbols","White robes, hoods, burning cross"],["Major law against it","Ku Klux Klan Act, 1871"]],
d:[["1865","First Klan founded in Tennessee"],["1871","Ku Klux Klan Act passed"],["1915","Second Klan founded at Stone Mountain"],["1925","Klan marches in Washington, D.C."],["1963","Klansmen bomb a Birmingham church"],["1964","Three civil rights workers murdered in Mississippi"]],
s2:[["Why the masks?","Hoods hid members’ identities, making it hard to prosecute them and spreading fear. Many states later passed anti-mask laws aimed at the Klan."],["Standing up to the Klan","Black communities, journalists and civil rights groups resisted the Klan, often at great risk. Ida B. Wells documented lynchings in the 1890s, and newspapers that exposed the Klan in the 1920s won Pulitzer Prizes."]],
sp:["The film that revived the Klan","The Birth of a Nation (1915) was a technically groundbreaking silent film but presented the Klan as heroes. The NAACP protested against it, and historians see its popularity as a key reason the Klan returned."],
g:[["White supremacy","The racist belief that white people are superior"],["Reconstruction","The period after the US Civil War (1865–1877)"],["Lynching","Murder by a mob without trial"],["Hate group","An organization that attacks people because of their identity"]],
f:["The Klan was founded by six Confederate veterans.","The burning cross was introduced by the second Klan in 1915.","Lawsuits have bankrupted several Klan groups."],
fun:"In the 1940s, the radio show The Adventures of Superman used secret Klan code words revealed by an investigator, helping to make the group look ridiculous.",
tl:"Ang Ku Klux Klan ay isang Amerikanong grupo ng poot na naniniwala sa supremasya ng mga puti, itinatag noong 1865. Gumamit ito ng pananakot at karahasan laban sa mga Black American at iba pa, at maliit na lamang ito ngayon."});

ADDART({id:"segregation",t:"Racial Segregation and Civil Rights",c:"History",i:"✊",w:"Racial segregation in the United States",r:["mlk","kkk","apartheid","racism","hrights"],
p:[["Separate and unequal","Segregation was the forced separation of people by race."],["Jim Crow","Laws in the American South kept Black and white people apart for decades."],["The civil rights movement","Protests and court cases ended legal segregation in the 1950s and 1960s."]],
l:"Racial segregation is the separation of people by race in housing, schools, transport, work and public places, enforced by law or custom. In the United States, after the end of slavery and Reconstruction, Southern states passed ‘Jim Crow’ laws requiring separate schools, railway cars, restaurants, toilets and drinking fountains for Black and white people, and used poll taxes and literacy tests to stop Black citizens from voting. In 1896 the Supreme Court ruled in Plessy v. Ferguson that ‘separate but equal’ facilities were legal, although Black facilities were almost always worse. The civil rights movement challenged segregation through lawsuits, boycotts, sit-ins and marches. The Supreme Court’s Brown v. Board of Education decision in 1954 declared school segregation unconstitutional, and the Civil Rights Act of 1964 and the Voting Rights Act of 1965 outlawed legal segregation and discrimination. Other countries, most notably South Africa under apartheid, also enforced segregation.",
s:[["Jim Crow","The name came from a mocking stage character. From the 1870s to the 1960s, segregation laws covered almost every part of life in the South, and breaking them could lead to arrest or violence. In the North, segregation was often enforced through housing discrimination and unwritten rules."],["The courts","Lawyers of the NAACP, led by Thurgood Marshall, challenged segregation case by case. On 17 May 1954, in Brown v. Board of Education, the Supreme Court ruled unanimously that separate schools were ‘inherently unequal’. Many Southern states resisted; in 1957, federal troops escorted nine Black students, the Little Rock Nine, into a school in Arkansas."],["Protest","On 1 December 1955, Rosa Parks refused to give up her bus seat to a white passenger in Montgomery, Alabama, starting a 381-day bus boycott led by Martin Luther King Jr. Students held sit-ins at segregated lunch counters from 1960, and Freedom Riders tested bus segregation in 1961. The 1963 March on Washington drew about 250,000 people."],["New laws and lasting effects","The Civil Rights Act of 2 July 1964 banned segregation in public places and discrimination in employment, and the Voting Rights Act of 1965 protected voting. In 1967, Loving v. Virginia struck down bans on interracial marriage. Historians note that the effects of segregation, such as differences in wealth and housing, lasted long after the laws ended."]],
k:[["‘Separate but equal’","Plessy v. Ferguson, 1896"],["School segregation ended","Brown v. Board of Education, 1954"],["Montgomery bus boycott","1955–1956, 381 days"],["Civil Rights Act","1964"],["Voting Rights Act","1965"]],
d:[["1896","Plessy v. Ferguson upholds segregation"],["1954","Brown v. Board of Education"],["1955","Rosa Parks starts the Montgomery bus boycott"],["1957","Little Rock Nine enter school under federal protection"],["1963","March on Washington"],["1964","Civil Rights Act"],["1965","Voting Rights Act"],["1967","Loving v. Virginia"]],
s2:[["Ruby Bridges","In 1960, six-year-old Ruby Bridges became the first Black child to attend an all-white elementary school in New Orleans, escorted by federal marshals past angry crowds. Norman Rockwell’s painting The Problem We All Live With shows her walk to school."],["Segregation around the world","South Africa’s apartheid system (1948–1994) was the most complete legal segregation of the 20th century. Segregation by caste, ethnicity or religion has also existed in other countries, and international law now bans racial segregation."]],
sp:["The Green Book","From 1936 to 1966, Black travelers in the United States used The Negro Motorist Green Book, a guide to hotels, restaurants and gas stations that would serve them. It helped people avoid humiliation and danger on the road."],
g:[["Segregation","Separating people by race or another group"],["Jim Crow laws","Southern US laws enforcing segregation"],["Boycott","Refusing to use a service as a protest"],["Sit-in","A protest where people sit and refuse to leave"]],
f:["The Montgomery bus boycott lasted 381 days.","Brown v. Board of Education was a unanimous decision.","Interracial marriage was banned in some US states until 1967."],
fun:"Rosa Parks’s bus is now on display at the Henry Ford Museum in Michigan, where visitors can sit in her seat.",
tl:"Ang segregasyon ay sapilitang paghihiwalay ng mga tao ayon sa lahi. Sa Estados Unidos, pinaghiwalay ng mga batas na Jim Crow ang mga Black at puti, hanggang wakasan ito ng kilusan para sa karapatang sibil at ng mga batas noong dekada 1950 at 1960."});

ADDART({id:"nanjing",t:"The Nanjing Massacre",c:"History",i:"🕯️",w:"Nanjing Massacre",r:["ww2","pacificwar","comfortwomen","china","genocide"],
p:[["A wartime atrocity","In December 1937, Japanese troops killed huge numbers of civilians and prisoners in Nanjing, China."],["Weeks of terror","Mass killings, rape and looting went on for about six weeks."],["Still disputed","The death toll and memory of the massacre remain sensitive between China and Japan."]],
l:"The Nanjing Massacre, also called the Rape of Nanking, was the mass murder, rape and looting carried out by Japanese troops after they captured Nanjing, then China’s capital, on 13 December 1937, during the Second Sino-Japanese War. Over about six weeks, soldiers killed prisoners of war and civilians, raped tens of thousands of women and girls and burned much of the city. Estimates of the number killed range widely; the postwar Tokyo tribunal estimated more than 200,000, and China’s official figure is 300,000, while some Japanese historians give lower numbers. A small group of foreigners set up the Nanking Safety Zone, which sheltered around 250,000 people. After the war, Japanese commanders were tried and executed for the crimes. The massacre remains a painful issue in relations between China and Japan, especially when some Japanese politicians have denied or minimized it.",
s:[["The fall of Nanjing","Japan invaded China in full in July 1937. After a long battle for Shanghai, Japanese forces advanced on Nanjing. Chinese leader Chiang Kai-shek’s government left, and the city fell on 13 December. Many Chinese soldiers who had surrendered or changed into civilian clothes were rounded up and executed."],["The massacre","Soldiers carried out mass executions along the Yangtze River and committed widespread sexual violence. Japanese newspapers even reported a ‘contest’ between two officers to kill 100 people with swords. Diaries, photographs and the testimony of survivors and foreign witnesses record what happened."],["The Safety Zone","About 20 Westerners, including the German businessman John Rabe, a member of the Nazi Party, and the American teacher Minnie Vautrin, created a safety zone that protected hundreds of thousands of refugees. Rabe wrote to Hitler protesting at the killings. His diary was published in 1996."],["Justice and memory","The International Military Tribunal for the Far East in Tokyo and the Nanjing War Crimes Tribunal convicted Japanese officers, including General Iwane Matsui, who was executed in 1948. The Nanjing Massacre Memorial Hall opened in 1985, and since 2014 China has held a national memorial day on 13 December. Japan’s government has acknowledged that killings took place and expressed remorse, but disputes over textbooks and visits to the Yasukuni Shrine continue."]],
k:[["Began","13 December 1937"],["Place","Nanjing, China"],["Duration","About six weeks"],["Estimated deaths","Over 200,000 (Tokyo tribunal); 300,000 (China)"],["Remembrance day","13 December (China)"]],
d:[["July 1937","Full-scale Japanese invasion of China"],["13 December 1937","Nanjing falls"],["1946–48","War crimes trials in Tokyo and Nanjing"],["1985","Memorial Hall opens in Nanjing"],["1997","Iris Chang publishes The Rape of Nanking"],["2014","China declares a national memorial day"],["2015","Nanjing documents added to UNESCO’s Memory of the World"]],
s2:[["Iris Chang’s book","In 1997, the Chinese-American writer Iris Chang published The Rape of Nanking, which became a best-seller and brought the massacre to the attention of many people outside Asia for the first time. Some historians criticized details, but the book sparked new research."],["History and diplomacy","Arguments over how the massacre is taught in Japanese schools and described by politicians have caused protests in China. Japanese and Chinese historians held joint research projects in 2006–2010 but could not agree on the death toll."]],
sp:["John Rabe, ‘the good Nazi’","John Rabe used his Nazi Party membership to try to stop Japanese soldiers, sometimes showing his swastika armband to drive them off. He sheltered about 600 refugees in his own garden. After the war he lived in poverty in Germany, and grateful Nanjing residents sent him food parcels."],
g:[["Massacre","The killing of many defenseless people"],["War crime","A serious violation of the laws of war"],["Safety zone","An area set aside to protect civilians"],["Tribunal","A special court"]],
f:["The Nanking Safety Zone sheltered around 250,000 people.","A German Nazi Party member helped save many Chinese lives.","China has held a national day of remembrance for the massacre since 2014."],
fun:"The American missionary John Magee secretly filmed scenes of the massacre with a 16 mm camera; his footage is some of the only film evidence.",
tl:"Noong Disyembre 1937, pinatay ng mga sundalong Hapon ang napakaraming sibilyan at bilanggo sa Nanjing, China, at gumawa ng malawakang pang-aabuso sa loob ng mga anim na linggo. Pinagtatalunan pa rin ang bilang ng namatay at sensitibo ito sa ugnayan ng China at Japan."});

ADDART({id:"greatleap",t:"The Great Leap Forward",c:"History",i:"🌾",w:"Great Leap Forward",r:["mao","culturalrev","foodsecurity","china","socialism"],
p:[["A rush to industrialize","The Great Leap Forward (1958–1962) was Mao Zedong’s plan to transform China quickly."],["Communes and furnaces","Peasants were organized into huge communes and made to produce steel in backyard furnaces."],["The great famine","The result was one of the deadliest famines in history, killing tens of millions."]],
l:"The Great Leap Forward was a campaign launched by Mao Zedong in 1958 to turn China rapidly from a farming country into an industrial and communist society, aiming to overtake Britain in steel production within 15 years. Farmers were organized into vast people’s communes, private plots were abolished and communal kitchens set up. Millions of people were ordered to make steel in backyard furnaces, melting pots and tools, but much of the metal was useless. Local officials, afraid of punishment, reported impossibly high grain harvests, so the state took grain that people needed to survive. Bad farming methods, the destruction of sparrows that ate pests, and bad weather made things worse. The resulting famine of 1959–1961 killed an estimated 15 to 45 million people, most historians say, making it the deadliest famine in history. Mao stepped back from daily leadership, but the disaster was long hidden.",
s:[["The plan","Mao believed that mass enthusiasm could achieve rapid progress. In 1958 the Communist Party set very high targets for steel and grain. Some 700 million peasants were organized into about 26,000 communes, each with tens of thousands of people working, eating and raising children collectively."],["Backyard furnaces and pseudo-science","Villages built small furnaces and burned wood, doors and furniture to fuel them, while crops rotted unharvested. The party promoted ideas such as close planting and deep plowing, based on the discredited theories of Soviet biologist Trofim Lysenko, which reduced harvests."],["Famine","Exaggerated harvest reports meant grain was taken for the cities and export while villages starved. People ate bark, grass and mud. When defense minister Peng Dehuai criticized the policies at the Lushan Conference in 1959, Mao purged him, and other officials became too afraid to speak. Estimates of deaths come from population data released after Mao’s death."],["Aftermath","In 1962 leaders such as Liu Shaoqi and Deng Xiaoping reversed many policies, and farmers were allowed small private plots again. Mao’s resentment of these leaders helped lead to the Cultural Revolution in 1966. The Chinese government acknowledged serious mistakes in 1981, but the famine is still rarely discussed openly in China."]],
k:[["Years","1958–1962"],["Leader","Mao Zedong"],["Main policies","People’s communes, backyard steel furnaces"],["Famine years","1959–1961"],["Estimated deaths","15–45 million (most estimates)"]],
d:[["1958","Great Leap Forward launched; communes formed"],["1958","Campaign to kill sparrows"],["1959","Peng Dehuai purged at the Lushan Conference"],["1959–61","Great Chinese Famine"],["1962","Policies reversed"],["1981","Party resolution admits serious errors"]],
s2:[["The war on sparrows","In 1958 the Four Pests campaign ordered people to kill rats, flies, mosquitoes and sparrows. Millions banged pots so sparrows could not land and dropped dead from exhaustion. Without sparrows to eat them, locusts and other insects multiplied and destroyed crops, and sparrows were later replaced on the list by bedbugs."],["Tombstone","The Chinese journalist Yang Jisheng, whose father died in the famine, spent years researching official records and published Tombstone in Hong Kong in 2008. It estimates about 36 million deaths and is banned in mainland China."]],
sp:["Why famines happen","The economist Amartya Sen argued that famines are usually caused not simply by a lack of food but by a lack of access to it, and that no major famine has happened in a functioning democracy with a free press. China’s famine, where bad news could not be reported, is often cited as an example."],
g:[["Commune","A large collective farm community"],["Famine","An extreme shortage of food causing mass hunger"],["Quota","A fixed amount that must be produced"],["Purge","Removing people from power"]],
f:["The Great Chinese Famine is considered the deadliest famine in history.","Killing sparrows led to plagues of insects.","Much of the backyard steel was too poor to use."],
fun:"Some villages tried to meet impossible harvest targets by moving crops from many fields into one field for officials to photograph.",
tl:"Ang Great Leap Forward (1958–1962) ay plano ni Mao Zedong upang mabilis na gawing industriyal ang China. Inorganiza ang mga magsasaka sa malalaking komyun at pinagawa ng bakal, at nagresulta ito sa isa sa pinakamalalang taggutom sa kasaysayan na pumatay ng sampu-sampung milyon."});

ADDART({id:"saddam",t:"Saddam Hussein",c:"People",i:"📕",w:"Saddam Hussein",r:["iraqcountry","sept11","isis","irancountry","terrorism"],
p:[["Dictator of Iraq","Saddam Hussein ruled Iraq from 1979 to 2003."],["Wars and repression","He fought Iran, invaded Kuwait and used chemical weapons against Kurds."],["Overthrown","A US-led invasion removed him in 2003; he was executed in 2006."]],
l:"Saddam Hussein (1937–2006) was the president of Iraq from 1979 until 2003. A leader of the Ba’ath Party, he built a brutal dictatorship that used secret police, torture and executions to crush opposition, while using Iraq’s oil wealth to build roads, schools and hospitals. In 1980 he invaded Iran, starting an eight-year war that killed hundreds of thousands. During that war his forces used chemical weapons, including against Iraqi Kurds at Halabja in 1988, killing about 5,000 people. In 1990 he invaded Kuwait, and a US-led coalition drove Iraq out in the 1991 Gulf War. In 2003 the United States and its allies invaded Iraq, saying he had weapons of mass destruction, which were never found. Saddam was captured in December 2003, tried by an Iraqi court for crimes against humanity and hanged on 30 December 2006. The war and its aftermath remain deeply controversial.",
s:[["Rise to power","Saddam was born near Tikrit on 28 April 1937. He joined the Ba’ath Party, took part in a failed attempt to assassinate Iraq’s ruler in 1959, and became the power behind President Ahmed Hassan al-Bakr after a 1968 coup. He took the presidency in July 1979 and soon after had dozens of party members accused of treason at a filmed meeting, many of whom were executed."],["Iran–Iraq War","In September 1980 Iraq invaded Iran, hoping to gain territory after Iran’s revolution. The war became a bloody stalemate, with trench warfare and missile attacks on cities, and ended in 1988 with no winner. During the Anfal campaign of 1986–89, Iraqi forces killed tens of thousands of Kurds and destroyed thousands of villages."],["Kuwait and the Gulf War","On 2 August 1990 Iraq invaded Kuwait. A coalition of 35 countries led by the United States launched Operation Desert Storm in January 1991 and liberated Kuwait in February. Saddam stayed in power, and Iraq faced years of sanctions and weapons inspections; the sanctions caused great hardship for ordinary Iraqis."],["The 2003 war and his end","After the 11 September attacks, US President George W. Bush argued that Iraq had weapons of mass destruction and links to terrorists. US and British forces invaded on 20 March 2003, and Baghdad fell within weeks. No stockpiles of such weapons were found. Saddam was found hiding in a hole near Tikrit on 13 December 2003. The war was followed by years of insurgency and sectarian violence."]],
k:[["Born","28 April 1937, near Tikrit, Iraq"],["Died","30 December 2006, Baghdad (executed)"],["President of Iraq","1979–2003"],["Party","Ba’ath Party"],["Wars","Iran–Iraq War, Gulf War, Iraq War"]],
d:[["1937","Born near Tikrit"],["1979","Becomes president of Iraq"],["1980–88","Iran–Iraq War"],["1988","Chemical attack on Halabja"],["1990","Invades Kuwait"],["1991","Gulf War"],["2003","Overthrown by US-led invasion; captured"],["2006","Executed after trial"]],
s2:[["Weapons of mass destruction","In the 1980s Iraq did make and use chemical weapons. After the 1991 war, United Nations inspectors destroyed much of its weapons capability. In 2004 the US Iraq Survey Group concluded that Iraq had no stockpiles of such weapons by 2003, a finding that made the reasons for the war a lasting subject of debate."],["Iraq after Saddam","Iraq held elections and adopted a new constitution in 2005, but violence between armed groups killed many thousands of civilians. The Islamic State group later seized large parts of the country in 2014 before being defeated."]],
sp:["The toppled statue","On 9 April 2003, a large statue of Saddam in Firdos Square, Baghdad, was pulled down with the help of a US armored vehicle as Iraqis cheered. The televised scene became a symbol of the end of his rule."],
g:[["Ba’athism","An Arab nationalist and socialist ideology"],["Chemical weapons","Poisonous substances used as weapons"],["Coalition","A group of countries acting together"],["Sanctions","Penalties, such as trade bans, placed on a country"]],
f:["Saddam ruled Iraq for 24 years.","No weapons of mass destruction stockpiles were found after the 2003 invasion.","Halabja is remembered as the largest chemical attack on civilians in history."],
fun:"Saddam wrote several novels, including one called Zabiba and the King, published anonymously.",
tl:"Si Saddam Hussein ang diktador ng Iraq mula 1979 hanggang 2003. Nakipagdigma siya sa Iran, sinakop ang Kuwait at gumamit ng kemikal na armas laban sa mga Kurd. Pinatalsik siya ng pagsalakay ng Estados Unidos noong 2003 at binitay noong 2006."});

ADDART({id:"sept11",t:"The September 11 Attacks",c:"History",i:"🕯️",w:"September 11 attacks",r:["terrorism","isis","saddam","conspiracy","skyscrapers"],
p:[["Attacks on America","On 11 September 2001, terrorists hijacked four airliners in the United States."],["Nearly 3,000 killed","Two planes destroyed the World Trade Center towers in New York."],["A changed world","The attacks led to wars in Afghanistan and Iraq and tighter security worldwide."]],
l:"The September 11 attacks were coordinated terrorist attacks carried out by 19 members of the Islamist militant group al-Qaeda against the United States on 11 September 2001. The hijackers took control of four passenger airliners. They flew two into the North and South Towers of the World Trade Center in New York City, which collapsed within two hours, and a third into the Pentagon, the US military headquarters near Washington, D.C. On the fourth plane, United Airlines Flight 93, passengers fought back, and it crashed into a field near Shanksville, Pennsylvania. In total, 2,977 people were killed, not counting the hijackers, making it the deadliest terrorist attack in history. The United States led an invasion of Afghanistan, whose Taliban rulers sheltered al-Qaeda’s leader Osama bin Laden, and launched a global ‘war on terror’. Bin Laden was killed by US forces in Pakistan in 2011.",
s:[["The attacks","At 8:46 a.m. American Airlines Flight 11 hit the North Tower; at 9:03 a.m. United Airlines Flight 175 struck the South Tower, seen live on television around the world. At 9:37 a.m. American Airlines Flight 77 hit the Pentagon. Learning of the other attacks by phone, passengers on Flight 93 tried to retake the cockpit, and the plane crashed at 10:03 a.m."],["Rescue and loss","Firefighters, police and paramedics rushed into the burning towers; 343 New York firefighters died. The South Tower collapsed at 9:59 a.m. and the North Tower at 10:28 a.m. Thousands of rescuers and workers later developed illnesses from the toxic dust at the site, known as Ground Zero."],["Al-Qaeda","Al-Qaeda, founded by Osama bin Laden in the late 1980s, had declared war on the United States and earlier bombed US embassies in Kenya and Tanzania in 1998. The 9/11 Commission found that the hijackers, most of them from Saudi Arabia, had trained in Afghanistan and in US flight schools, and that intelligence agencies had missed warning signs."],["Consequences","In October 2001, US and allied forces invaded Afghanistan, beginning a war that lasted until 2021, when the Taliban returned to power. The 2003 invasion of Iraq was also justified in part by the war on terror. New laws such as the USA PATRIOT Act expanded surveillance, airport security was transformed, and debates about the balance between security and civil liberties continue. On 2 May 2011, US special forces killed bin Laden in Abbottabad, Pakistan."]],
k:[["Date","11 September 2001"],["Attackers","19 al-Qaeda hijackers"],["Targets","World Trade Center, Pentagon"],["Deaths","2,977 (not counting the hijackers)"],["Firefighters killed","343"],["Al-Qaeda leader","Osama bin Laden (killed 2011)"]],
d:[["1998","Al-Qaeda bombs US embassies in Kenya and Tanzania"],["11 Sept 2001","Attacks on New York and Washington"],["Oct 2001","US-led invasion of Afghanistan"],["2004","9/11 Commission Report published"],["2011","Bin Laden killed; memorial opens in New York"],["2014","One World Trade Center opens"],["2021","US leaves Afghanistan"]],
s2:[["The memorial","The National September 11 Memorial in New York has two huge pools in the footprints of the twin towers, with the names of the victims carved around them. A memorial also stands at the Pentagon, and at Shanksville a Tower of Voices holds 40 wind chimes, one for each passenger and crew member of Flight 93."],["Conspiracy theories","Some conspiracy theories claim the US government carried out or allowed the attacks. Detailed investigations, including by engineers who studied why the towers collapsed, found no evidence for these claims, which hurt victims’ families."]],
sp:["The Survivor Tree","A badly burned Callery pear tree was found in the rubble at Ground Zero in October 2001. It was nursed back to health and replanted at the memorial in 2010, where it blooms each spring as a symbol of resilience."],
g:[["Hijack","To take control of a vehicle by force"],["Terrorism","Violence against civilians to cause fear for political aims"],["Ground Zero","The site of the destroyed World Trade Center"],["War on terror","The US-led campaign against terrorist groups after 9/11"]],
f:["People from more than 90 countries died in the attacks.","The passengers of Flight 93 voted to fight back.","The twin towers were 110 stories tall."],
fun:"One World Trade Center, built near the site, is exactly 1,776 feet tall, a reference to the year of American independence.",
tl:"Noong 11 Setyembre 2001, inagaw ng 19 na terorista ng al-Qaeda ang apat na eroplano sa Estados Unidos. Winasak ng dalawa ang kambal na tore ng World Trade Center sa New York, at halos 3,000 ang namatay, na humantong sa mga digmaan sa Afghanistan at Iraq."});

ADDART({id:"mccarthyism",t:"McCarthyism and the Red Scare",c:"History",i:"🟥",w:"McCarthyism",r:["coldwar","censorship","witchhunts","democ","propaganda"],
p:[["Fear of communism","The Red Scare was a period of intense fear of communists in the United States."],["Accusations without evidence","Senator Joseph McCarthy accused many people of disloyalty with little proof."],["Lasting lessons","‘McCarthyism’ now means making reckless accusations to silence opponents."]],
l:"McCarthyism is the practice of making public accusations of disloyalty or treason without proper evidence. It is named after Joseph McCarthy, a US senator from Wisconsin who, from 1950 to 1954, claimed that communists had infiltrated the government, army and other institutions. His campaign was the most famous part of the Second Red Scare, a wave of anti-communist fear in the United States during the early Cold War. Government employees had to pass loyalty checks; congressional committees questioned actors, writers, teachers and scientists about their political beliefs; and thousands lost their jobs. Hollywood blacklisted many writers and actors. Some real Soviet spies were caught, but many innocent people had their lives ruined. McCarthy’s influence collapsed after televised hearings in 1954 exposed his bullying tactics, and the Senate condemned him. The period is remembered as a warning about fear overriding civil liberties.",
s:[["Background","After World War II, the Soviet Union took control of Eastern Europe, tested an atomic bomb in 1949, and communists won China’s civil war. Spy cases, including those of Alger Hiss and of Julius and Ethel Rosenberg, who were executed in 1953 for passing atomic secrets, convinced many Americans that communists were a threat at home."],["McCarthy’s campaign","On 9 February 1950, in Wheeling, West Virginia, McCarthy claimed to have a list of communists working in the State Department. The number changed in different speeches, and he never proved his claims. Opponents were afraid to challenge him for fear of being called communists themselves."],["The blacklist","The House Un-American Activities Committee investigated Hollywood from 1947. The ‘Hollywood Ten’, who refused to answer questions about their politics, were jailed for contempt. Studios refused to hire hundreds of people suspected of left-wing links; some writers worked under false names."],["Downfall","In 1954 McCarthy accused the US Army of harboring communists. In the televised Army–McCarthy hearings, the army’s lawyer Joseph Welch asked him, ‘Have you no sense of decency, sir?’ Journalist Edward R. Murrow also criticized him on television. On 2 December 1954, the Senate voted to censure him. He died in 1957."]],
k:[["Named after","Senator Joseph McCarthy"],["Period","Late 1940s–mid-1950s"],["Wheeling speech","9 February 1950"],["Senate censure","2 December 1954"],["Famous play","The Crucible by Arthur Miller (1953)"]],
d:[["1947","Hollywood Ten investigated"],["1950","McCarthy’s Wheeling speech"],["1953","Rosenbergs executed"],["1953","The Crucible premieres"],["1954","Army–McCarthy hearings"],["1954","Senate censures McCarthy"],["1957","McCarthy dies"]],
s2:[["Real spies, false accusations","Decoded Soviet messages, known as the Venona project and released in 1995, showed that the Soviet Union had a real network of spies in the United States in the 1940s. Historians note that most of the people McCarthy accused were not spies, and that his methods damaged genuine efforts to catch them."],["The Crucible","Arthur Miller’s play The Crucible (1953) is set during the Salem witch trials of 1692 but was written as a comment on McCarthyism. Miller himself was called before Congress in 1956 and refused to name others."]],
sp:["‘Have you no sense of decency?’","On 9 June 1954, McCarthy attacked a young lawyer who worked with Joseph Welch. Welch’s reply, broadcast to millions, turned public opinion against McCarthy, and the audience in the hearing room applauded."],
g:[["Red Scare","A period of intense fear of communism"],["Blacklist","A list of people to be refused jobs"],["Censure","An official condemnation"],["Civil liberties","Basic freedoms such as speech and belief"]],
f:["Thousands of Americans lost their jobs during the Red Scare.","McCarthy’s list of communists kept changing in number.","The Crucible uses the Salem witch trials to criticize McCarthyism."],
fun:"Blacklisted writer Dalton Trumbo won two Academy Awards for scripts written under false names.",
tl:"Ang McCarthyism ay paggawa ng mga paratang ng kataksilan nang walang sapat na ebidensya, ipinangalan kay Senador Joseph McCarthy ng Estados Unidos. Noong panahon ng Red Scare, libo-libo ang nawalan ng trabaho dahil sa takot sa komunismo."});

ADDART({id:"watergate",t:"The Watergate Scandal",c:"History",i:"🗂️",w:"Watergate scandal",r:["journalism","conspiracy","democ","whistleblowers","coldwar"],
p:[["A break-in and a cover-up","Watergate began with a burglary at the Democratic Party’s offices in 1972."],["Presidential tapes","Secret recordings proved President Nixon helped cover it up."],["The only presidential resignation","Nixon resigned in 1974 rather than face removal."]],
l:"The Watergate scandal was a political scandal in the United States in the early 1970s that led to the resignation of President Richard Nixon. On 17 June 1972, five men were arrested breaking into the headquarters of the Democratic National Committee in the Watergate building in Washington, D.C., to plant listening devices. They were linked to Nixon’s re-election campaign. Nixon and his aides tried to cover up the White House’s involvement, paying the burglars to stay silent and using the CIA to block the FBI’s investigation. Reporting by Bob Woodward and Carl Bernstein of The Washington Post, a Senate investigation and a special prosecutor gradually exposed the cover-up. When it was revealed that Nixon had secretly taped conversations in his office, a long legal battle followed. After the Supreme Court ordered him to hand over the tapes, which proved he had obstructed justice, Nixon resigned on 9 August 1974, the only US president to do so.",
s:[["The break-in","The burglars, including a former CIA officer working for the Committee to Re-elect the President, were caught after a security guard noticed tape on a door lock. At first the White House dismissed the event as a ‘third-rate burglary’, and Nixon won re-election in a landslide in November 1972."],["Investigating","Woodward and Bernstein were guided by a secret source nicknamed Deep Throat, revealed in 2005 to be Mark Felt, deputy director of the FBI. Senate hearings in 1973 were televised; a former aide revealed that Nixon had a recording system in the Oval Office."],["The Saturday Night Massacre","When special prosecutor Archibald Cox demanded the tapes, Nixon ordered him fired on 20 October 1973. The attorney general and deputy attorney general resigned rather than do it. Public outrage grew, and impeachment proceedings began in the House of Representatives."],["Resignation","On 24 July 1974, the Supreme Court ruled unanimously in United States v. Nixon that he had to release the tapes. One, the ‘smoking gun’ tape, showed him ordering the cover-up days after the break-in. Facing certain impeachment and removal, Nixon resigned. His successor, Gerald Ford, pardoned him a month later. Dozens of officials were convicted."]],
k:[["Break-in","17 June 1972"],["President","Richard Nixon"],["Reporters","Bob Woodward and Carl Bernstein"],["Secret source","‘Deep Throat’ (Mark Felt)"],["Nixon resigned","9 August 1974"],["Pardon","8 September 1974, by Gerald Ford"]],
d:[["1972","Burglars arrested at the Watergate building"],["1973","Senate hearings; tapes revealed"],["1973","Saturday Night Massacre"],["1974","Supreme Court orders release of the tapes"],["1974","Nixon resigns"],["1974","Ford pardons Nixon"],["2005","Deep Throat’s identity revealed"]],
s2:[["‘-gate’","Watergate gave English the suffix ‘-gate’, now attached to many scandals around the world, from sports controversies to political affairs."],["Lessons","Watergate is often cited as an example of a free press, independent courts and Congress holding even a president to account. It led to new laws on campaign finance and government ethics."]],
sp:["All the President’s Men","Woodward and Bernstein’s 1974 book All the President’s Men, and the 1976 film starring Robert Redford and Dustin Hoffman, made investigative journalism famous. The film’s line ‘Follow the money’ became a catchphrase for investigators."],
g:[["Cover-up","An attempt to hide wrongdoing"],["Impeachment","Formally charging an official with misconduct"],["Obstruction of justice","Interfering with an investigation"],["Pardon","Officially forgiving a crime"]],
f:["Nixon is the only US president to have resigned.","The break-in was discovered because of tape on a door lock.","Deep Throat’s identity was kept secret for over 30 years."],
fun:"An 18½-minute gap on one of Nixon’s tapes, which his secretary said she may have caused accidentally, has never been explained.",
tl:"Ang iskandalong Watergate ay nagsimula sa pagnanakaw sa tanggapan ng Partidong Demokratiko noong 1972. Pinatunayan ng mga lihim na rekording na tumulong si Pangulong Nixon sa pagtatakip, kaya nagbitiw siya noong 1974, ang tanging pangulo ng Estados Unidos na gumawa nito."});

ADDART({id:"witchhunts",t:"Witch Hunts",c:"History",i:"🧹",w:"Witch trials in the early modern period",r:["inquisition","mccarthyism","reform","conspiracy","hrights"],
p:[["Fear of witchcraft","From about 1450 to 1750, Europe and its colonies tried and executed tens of thousands of people as witches."],["Mostly women","About four out of five of those executed were women."],["Salem","The Salem witch trials of 1692 are the most famous case in America."]],
l:"Witch hunts were campaigns to find and punish people accused of witchcraft, the supposed use of magic to cause harm. The great European witch hunts took place between about 1450 and 1750, during the upheavals of the Reformation, wars, plagues and crop failures. Courts, both church and secular, tried perhaps 100,000 people, of whom around 40,000 to 60,000 were executed, usually by burning or hanging. About 80 percent of the victims were women, often poor, elderly or living alone. Confessions were frequently obtained by torture, and accused people were pressured to name others, creating chains of accusations. The worst persecutions occurred in parts of Germany, Switzerland and France. In 1692, in Salem, Massachusetts, 20 people were executed after accusations by a group of girls. Witch hunts declined with the rise of scientific thinking and legal reforms. Today, the term also describes the unfair persecution of any group.",
s:[["Causes","Belief in harmful magic was ancient, but organized witch hunting grew after the 1400s. A handbook called the Malleus Maleficarum (‘Hammer of Witches’), published in 1486, described how to find and try witches. Historians link hunts to fear and blame during hard times, religious conflict, and local quarrels among neighbors."],["Trials and torture","Accused people might be searched for a ‘devil’s mark’ or subjected to tests, such as being thrown into water to see if they floated. Under torture, many confessed to meeting the devil and named accomplices. In some German territories hundreds were executed in a few years."],["Salem","In early 1692, several girls in Salem Village began having fits and accused local women of bewitching them. More than 200 people were accused; 19 were hanged and one man, Giles Corey, was crushed to death with stones for refusing to plead. Within a few years, jurors and a judge publicly apologized, and Massachusetts later cleared the victims’ names; the last was officially exonerated in 2022."],["The end of the hunts","Skeptical writers, judges who demanded proper evidence, and the scientific revolution helped end witch trials. England repealed its witchcraft law in 1736. The last legal execution for witchcraft in Europe is usually said to be that of Anna Göldi in Switzerland in 1782; she was exonerated in 2008. In some parts of the world, people accused of witchcraft still suffer violence today."]],
k:[["Main period","About 1450–1750"],["Executions in Europe","About 40,000–60,000"],["Share of women","About 80%"],["Witch-hunting handbook","Malleus Maleficarum (1486)"],["Salem executions","20 (1692)"]],
d:[["1486","Malleus Maleficarum published"],["1580s–1630s","Peak of European witch hunts"],["1692","Salem witch trials"],["1736","England repeals its witchcraft law"],["1782","Anna Göldi executed in Switzerland"],["2008","Anna Göldi exonerated"],["2022","Last Salem convict officially cleared"]],
s2:[["Witch hunt as a metaphor","Since the McCarthy era, ‘witch hunt’ has been used for any campaign that targets people unfairly, often based on fear rather than evidence. Politicians sometimes use the phrase to dismiss investigations into themselves, which makes it a disputed term."],["Accusations today","Human rights groups report that people, often older women and sometimes children, are still accused of witchcraft and attacked in parts of Africa, South Asia and Papua New Guinea. Several countries have passed laws to protect them."]],
sp:["Swimming a witch","In the ‘swimming test’, a suspect was tied and thrown into water. Because water was thought to reject the wicked, floating meant guilt, while sinking meant innocence, though the person might drown. The test was banned in many places but used informally for a long time."],
g:[["Witchcraft","The supposed use of magic, especially to harm"],["Secular","Not religious"],["Exonerate","To officially clear of blame"],["Hysteria","Uncontrolled fear or excitement in a group"]],
f:["Most victims of the witch hunts were women.","Nobody was burned at Salem; those executed were hanged.","Some European countries executed far more accused witches than others."],
fun:"The Salem Witch Museum is one of the most visited attractions in Massachusetts, and the town celebrates Halloween for the whole month of October.",
tl:"Ang witch hunt ay kampanyang hanapin at parusahan ang mga taong pinaratangang mangkukulam. Mula mga 1450 hanggang 1750, libo-libo ang binitay sa Europa, karamihan ay mga babae, at ang mga paglilitis sa Salem noong 1692 ang pinakatanyag sa Amerika."});

ADDART({id:"inquisition",t:"The Inquisition",c:"History",i:"⚖️",w:"Inquisition",r:["crusades","reform","galileo","witchhunts","spaincountry"],
p:[["Church courts","The Inquisition was a system of Catholic Church courts that tried people accused of heresy."],["The Spanish Inquisition","Spain’s Inquisition, from 1478 to 1834, targeted converted Jews and Muslims."],["Galileo’s trial","The Roman Inquisition put Galileo on trial in 1633."]],
l:"The Inquisition was a group of institutions within the Roman Catholic Church set up to find and punish heresy, meaning beliefs that went against Church teaching. The medieval Inquisition began in the 12th and 13th centuries to fight religious movements such as the Cathars in southern France. The Spanish Inquisition, created in 1478 under the control of the Spanish monarchs, focused on Jews and Muslims who had converted to Christianity, often under pressure, and were suspected of secretly keeping their old faiths, and later on Protestants. The Portuguese Inquisition and the Roman Inquisition followed in the 16th century; the Roman Inquisition tried the astronomer Galileo in 1633. Inquisitors used secret accusations and sometimes torture, and those found guilty could be fined, imprisoned or handed over to the state to be burned. Historians estimate that the Spanish Inquisition executed around 3,000 to 5,000 people over three and a half centuries. The Church apologized for its abuses in 2000.",
s:[["The medieval Inquisition","In the 1230s, Pope Gregory IX appointed friars, mainly Dominicans, as inquisitors to investigate heresy. In 1252 Pope Innocent IV allowed the use of torture. The Cathars, who believed in two opposing gods of good and evil, were crushed by a crusade and the Inquisition."],["The Spanish Inquisition","Ferdinand and Isabella of Spain obtained permission from the Pope in 1478 to set up their own Inquisition. Tomás de Torquemada, the first grand inquisitor from 1483, became infamous for his severity. In 1492 Jews who would not convert were expelled from Spain. The Inquisition held public ceremonies called autos-da-fé, where sentences were announced."],["The Roman Inquisition","Founded in 1542 to fight Protestantism, the Roman Inquisition kept an Index of Forbidden Books. The philosopher Giordano Bruno was burned in Rome in 1600. In 1633 Galileo was forced to deny that the Earth moves around the Sun and spent his last years under house arrest."],["The end and reassessment","The Spanish Inquisition was finally abolished in 1834. Historians have found that its records show it was less bloodthirsty than its legend in some ways, but that it spread fear, encouraged denunciations and harmed intellectual life. In 1992 the Church acknowledged Galileo had been wronged, and in 2000 Pope John Paul II asked forgiveness for the use of violence in the service of truth."]],
k:[["Purpose","To find and punish heresy"],["Spanish Inquisition","1478–1834"],["First Spanish grand inquisitor","Tomás de Torquemada"],["Roman Inquisition founded","1542"],["Galileo’s trial","1633"],["Church apology","2000"]],
d:[["1230s","Pope appoints inquisitors against heresy"],["1478","Spanish Inquisition created"],["1492","Jews expelled from Spain"],["1542","Roman Inquisition founded"],["1600","Giordano Bruno burned in Rome"],["1633","Galileo tried"],["1834","Spanish Inquisition abolished"],["2000","Pope John Paul II apologizes"]],
s2:[["The Inquisition in Asia and the Americas","The Inquisition operated in Spanish and Portuguese colonies, with tribunals in Mexico City, Lima, Cartagena and Goa in India. Indigenous people in the Americas were generally exempt from it after the 1570s. The Philippines came under the Mexican tribunal through a local commissioner."],["The ‘Black Legend’","Protestant writers in England and the Netherlands exaggerated the cruelty of the Spanish Inquisition as part of a wider ‘Black Legend’ about Spain. Modern historians try to separate the real abuses from the propaganda."]],
sp:["‘Nobody expects the Spanish Inquisition!’","A famous 1970 sketch by the British comedy group Monty Python showed bumbling inquisitors bursting in with the line ‘Nobody expects the Spanish Inquisition!’ It made the Inquisition a familiar joke for millions who knew little of its history."],
g:[["Heresy","A belief contrary to official religious teaching"],["Inquisitor","An official who investigated heresy"],["Auto-da-fé","A public ceremony where Inquisition sentences were read"],["Denunciation","Accusing someone to the authorities"]],
f:["The Spanish Inquisition lasted 356 years.","Galileo was officially rehabilitated by the Church in 1992.","Inquisition tribunals operated in Mexico, Peru and India."],
fun:"The Inquisition’s archives in Spain are so detailed that historians use them to study everyday life, from cooking to clothing, in the 1500s.",
tl:"Ang Inkisisyon ay sistema ng mga hukuman ng Simbahang Katoliko na lumilitis sa mga pinaratangang erehe. Pinakatanyag ang Inkisisyong Espanyol (1478–1834), at nilitis ng Inkisisyong Romano si Galileo noong 1633."});

ADDART({id:"childsoldiers",t:"Child Soldiers",c:"Society",i:"🧒",w:"Children in the military",r:["hrights","un","genocide","trafficking","terrorism"],
p:[["Children at war","Child soldiers are children used by armed forces or groups."],["Forced to fight","Many are kidnapped, forced to fight or used as porters, spies or cooks."],["Banned by law","International law forbids using children under 18 in combat."]],
l:"Child soldiers are children under the age of 18 who are recruited or used by armed forces or armed groups, as fighters or in other roles such as cooks, porters, messengers, spies or for sexual purposes. Some are abducted and forced to join; others join because of poverty, hunger, a desire for revenge or protection, or because their families or communities pressure them. Armed groups value children because they are easy to control and frighten. The United Nations verified that more than 105,000 children were recruited and used in conflicts between 2005 and 2022, and the true number is believed to be much higher. Child soldiers have been used in wars in Africa, Asia, the Middle East and Latin America. International law makes recruiting children under 15 a war crime and sets 18 as the minimum age for taking part in hostilities. Former child soldiers need long-term help to recover and return to their communities.",
s:[["How children are recruited","Groups such as the Lord’s Resistance Army in Uganda abducted tens of thousands of children, forcing some to commit violence against their own villages so they could not return home. Others join armed groups in refugee camps or after losing their families. Girls are often forced into marriages with fighters."],["Laws and courts","The Optional Protocol to the Convention on the Rights of the Child, adopted in 2000, raised the minimum age for direct participation in hostilities to 18. In 2012, the International Criminal Court’s first-ever verdict convicted the Congolese warlord Thomas Lubanga of recruiting and using child soldiers; he was sentenced to 14 years."],["Recovery","Programs run by the UN, UNICEF and aid groups help children leave armed groups, find their families, return to school and learn trades. Many suffer from trauma and face stigma when they return, especially girls who come back with children of their own."],["Speaking out","Former child soldiers such as Ishmael Beah from Sierra Leone, whose memoir A Long Way Gone (2007) became a best-seller, have campaigned to end the practice. Each year on 12 February, Red Hand Day draws attention to the issue."]],
k:[["Definition","Under 18, recruited or used by armed forces or groups"],["UN-verified cases","Over 105,000 (2005–2022)"],["Key treaty","Optional Protocol, 2000"],["First ICC verdict","Thomas Lubanga, 2012"],["Awareness day","12 February, Red Hand Day"]],
d:[["1989","UN Convention on the Rights of the Child"],["1996","UN report by Graça Machel on children in war"],["2000","Optional Protocol sets 18 as minimum age for combat"],["2007","Paris Principles on protecting children in war"],["2007","Ishmael Beah’s memoir published"],["2012","ICC convicts Thomas Lubanga"]],
s2:[["Why children?","Children can be easier to frighten, manipulate and control than adults and may not understand danger. Modern light weapons, such as assault rifles, are simple enough for children to carry and use."],["Kony 2012","In 2012, a video about the Lord’s Resistance Army leader Joseph Kony was viewed over 100 million times within days. It raised awareness but was also criticized for oversimplifying the situation in Uganda."]],
sp:["The red hand","The symbol of Red Hand Day is a red handprint, meaning ‘stop’. People around the world make red handprints on paper, which have been presented to the UN and to governments to demand an end to the use of child soldiers."],
g:[["Armed group","A fighting force that is not a country’s army"],["Abduct","To kidnap"],["Trauma","Lasting emotional harm from a terrible experience"],["War crime","A serious violation of the laws of war"]],
f:["The ICC’s first verdict was about child soldiers.","Girls make up a significant share of children used by armed groups.","Red Hand Day is marked on 12 February."],
fun:"Ishmael Beah, a former child soldier, became a UNICEF Advocate for Children Affected by War and has written a novel as well as his memoir.",
tl:"Ang mga batang sundalo ay mga batang wala pang 18 taon na ginagamit ng mga hukbo o armadong grupo. Marami ang dinukot at pinilit lumaban, at ipinagbabawal ng pandaigdigang batas ang paggamit sa kanila sa labanan."});

;
