// New articles, 15 November 2026 (part 5): current conflicts and social debates. Current-events facts checked 15 November 2026.
ADDART({id:"ukrainewar",t:"The Russia–Ukraine War",c:"The Modern World",i:"🇺🇦",w:"Russo-Ukrainian war",r:["ukrainecountry","russiacountry","coldwar","nato","migration"],
p:[["Europe’s largest war since 1945","Russia launched a full-scale invasion of Ukraine on 24 February 2022."],["Began in 2014","The conflict started when Russia annexed Crimea and backed fighters in eastern Ukraine."],["Talks but no peace","By November 2026, peace talks had resumed, but fighting continued."]],
l:"The Russia–Ukraine war is an armed conflict that began in 2014, when Russia annexed Ukraine’s Crimean peninsula and supported armed separatists in the eastern Donbas region, and escalated on 24 February 2022, when Russia launched a full-scale invasion. It is the largest war in Europe since World War II. Russia’s government said it wanted to protect Russian speakers and stop NATO’s expansion; Ukraine and most of the world called the invasion an unprovoked attack on a sovereign country. Russian forces failed to capture Kyiv and were pushed back from the north and later from Kharkiv and Kherson regions in 2022, but by 2026 Russia occupied about a fifth of Ukraine. Hundreds of thousands of soldiers on both sides have been killed or wounded, the UN has verified more than 14,000 civilian deaths, and millions of Ukrainians have fled. Many countries imposed sanctions on Russia and sent weapons to Ukraine. US-led peace talks resumed in Abu Dhabi in November 2026, but no ceasefire had been agreed.",
s:[["Background","Ukraine, part of the Soviet Union until 1991, moved towards Europe after protests in 2013–14 drove out its pro-Russian president. Russia then seized Crimea in March 2014 and backed separatists in Donetsk and Luhansk. The Minsk agreements of 2014–15 failed to bring lasting peace, and about 14,000 people died in the Donbas fighting before 2022."],["The full-scale invasion","Russian forces attacked from the north, east and south in February 2022. Ukraine’s army and volunteers stopped the advance on Kyiv, and Russian troops withdrew from the north in April, after which evidence of mass killings of civilians was found in towns such as Bucha. Ukraine retook much of Kharkiv region and the city of Kherson in late 2022. In September 2022, Russia claimed to annex four Ukrainian regions, a move rejected by the UN General Assembly."],["A war of attrition","After a Ukrainian counteroffensive in 2023 made little progress, the front lines changed slowly, with heavy losses. Drones became central to the fighting, and both sides struck targets far behind the lines. In August 2024 Ukraine briefly seized territory inside Russia’s Kursk region. Russian missile and drone attacks repeatedly hit Ukrainian cities and power stations."],["Diplomacy and justice","In March 2023, the International Criminal Court issued an arrest warrant for President Vladimir Putin over the deportation of Ukrainian children. Talks in 2025 produced prisoner exchanges but no peace. A short Easter truce in April 2026 broke down, and new talks among Ukraine, Russia and US mediators began in Abu Dhabi in November 2026, with disputes over territory and security guarantees unresolved."]],
k:[["Began","2014 (annexation of Crimea); full-scale invasion 24 February 2022"],["Territory occupied by Russia","About one-fifth of Ukraine (2026)"],["UN-verified civilian deaths","More than 14,000"],["Refugees abroad","About 6 million"],["Status (mid-November 2026)","Fighting continues; peace talks under way"]],
d:[["2014","Russia annexes Crimea; war in Donbas begins"],["2022","Full-scale invasion on 24 February"],["2022","Russian withdrawal from around Kyiv; Kherson liberated in November"],["2023","ICC issues warrant for Putin"],["2024","Ukrainian incursion into Kursk"],["2026","Easter truce fails; Abu Dhabi talks in November"]],
s2:[["Effects around the world","The war caused energy prices to soar in Europe and disrupted grain exports from Ukraine, one of the world’s largest grain producers, raising food prices in poorer countries. A UN-backed deal allowed grain ships to sail from Ukrainian ports for a year from July 2022. Finland and Sweden joined NATO in 2023 and 2024."],["Different views","Most UN members voted to condemn the invasion. Some countries, especially in the Global South, stayed neutral, citing their own interests or criticism of Western policies, while a few, such as Belarus, North Korea and Iran, supported Russia, including with weapons or troops."]],
sp:["Drone warfare","The war in Ukraine has been called the first large-scale drone war. Both sides use thousands of small, cheap drones every day for spotting targets and attacking, and Ukraine built a huge drone industry. Military experts around the world study the conflict to understand how war is changing."],
g:[["Annexation","Taking over territory and declaring it part of one’s country"],["Sanctions","Penalties such as trade bans placed on a country"],["Attrition","Wearing down an enemy through continuous losses"],["Ceasefire","An agreement to stop fighting"]],
f:["It is the largest war in Europe since 1945.","About 6 million Ukrainians were living as refugees abroad.","The ICC issued an arrest warrant for Putin in 2023."],
fun:"Ukrainian farmers became famous early in the war for towing away abandoned Russian tanks with their tractors.",
tl:"Ang digmaang Russia–Ukraine ay nagsimula noong 2014 nang sakupin ng Russia ang Crimea, at lumaki nang lusubin ng Russia ang buong Ukraine noong 24 Pebrero 2022. Ito ang pinakamalaking digmaan sa Europa mula 1945, at hanggang Nobyembre 2026 ay patuloy ang labanan habang may usapang pangkapayapaan."});

ADDART({id:"israelpalestine",t:"The Israeli–Palestinian Conflict",c:"The Modern World",i:"🕊️",w:"Israeli–Palestinian conflict",r:["israelcountry","jerusalem","un","terrorism","migration","antisemitism"],
p:[["Two peoples, one land","The conflict is between Israelis and Palestinians over land, borders, security and self-rule."],["Decades of war and talks","It has continued since before Israel’s founding in 1948."],["The Gaza war","An attack by Hamas on 7 October 2023 led to a devastating war in Gaza."]],
l:"The Israeli–Palestinian conflict is a long-running dispute between Israelis and Palestinians over land between the Jordan River and the Mediterranean Sea, which both peoples see as their homeland. Its main issues include borders, the status of Jerusalem, Israeli settlements in the occupied West Bank, the security of Israelis, the rights of Palestinian refugees, and whether a Palestinian state should be created alongside Israel, the so-called two-state solution. The conflict grew out of rival Jewish and Arab national movements in the early 20th century. Israel was established in 1948; in the war that followed, about 700,000 Palestinians fled or were expelled, an event Palestinians call the Nakba (‘catastrophe’). Israel captured the West Bank, East Jerusalem and Gaza in 1967. Peace efforts such as the Oslo Accords of the 1990s did not lead to a final agreement. On 7 October 2023, Hamas-led militants attacked Israel, killing about 1,200 people and taking 251 hostages; the war that followed killed tens of thousands of Palestinians in Gaza before a ceasefire took effect in October 2025.",
s:[["Origins","In the late 19th century, the Zionist movement sought a Jewish homeland in historic Palestine, then part of the Ottoman Empire, where Arabs were the majority. Britain’s Balfour Declaration of 1917 supported a ‘national home for the Jewish people’, and Britain ruled Palestine from 1920. Jewish immigration, especially after Nazi persecution, and Arab opposition led to growing violence."],["1948 and 1967","In 1947 the UN proposed dividing Palestine into Jewish and Arab states; Jewish leaders accepted and Arab leaders rejected the plan. Israel declared independence on 14 May 1948, and neighboring Arab states attacked. In the Six-Day War of June 1967, Israel captured the West Bank and East Jerusalem from Jordan, Gaza and Sinai from Egypt, and the Golan Heights from Syria. Sinai was returned to Egypt after a 1979 peace treaty."],["Uprisings and peace efforts","Palestinian uprisings called intifadas broke out in 1987 and 2000. The Oslo Accords of 1993 created the Palestinian Authority with limited self-rule. Israel withdrew from Gaza in 2005, but Hamas, which rejects Israel’s existence and is designated a terrorist organization by many countries, took control there in 2007, and Israel and Egypt imposed a blockade. Several wars between Israel and Hamas followed."],["The Gaza war","After the 7 October 2023 attack, Israel launched a military campaign to destroy Hamas and free the hostages. Much of Gaza was destroyed, most of its 2.1 million people were displaced, and the UN warned of famine. Gaza’s Health Ministry reported more than 67,000 Palestinians killed by the time a US-brokered ceasefire took effect on 10 October 2025. All living hostages were released, and the UN Security Council endorsed a peace plan with a Board of Peace in November 2025. By late 2026, Israeli forces still controlled more than half of Gaza, deadly incidents continued, and an international stabilization force had not yet deployed."]],
k:[["Main issues","Borders, Jerusalem, settlements, refugees, security, statehood"],["Israel founded","14 May 1948"],["Six-Day War","June 1967"],["Oslo Accords","1993"],["7 October attack","About 1,200 killed in Israel, 251 taken hostage (2023)"],["Gaza ceasefire","10 October 2025"]],
d:[["1917","Balfour Declaration"],["1947","UN partition plan"],["1948","Israel founded; first Arab–Israeli war; Palestinian exodus"],["1967","Six-Day War"],["1987","First intifada"],["1993","Oslo Accords"],["2005","Israel withdraws from Gaza"],["2023","Hamas attack on 7 October; Gaza war begins"],["2025","Ceasefire in Gaza"]],
s2:[["Courts and recognition","South Africa brought a case at the International Court of Justice accusing Israel of genocide in Gaza, which Israel strongly denies. In November 2024 the International Criminal Court issued arrest warrants for Israel’s prime minister Benjamin Netanyahu and former defense minister Yoav Gallant, and for a Hamas commander; Israel rejects the court’s authority. About four-fifths of UN members, including several Western countries that joined in 2025, recognize a State of Palestine."],["Different narratives","Israelis often stress their historic ties to the land, the need for security after the Holocaust and repeated wars and attacks, and the rejection of past peace offers. Palestinians often stress their displacement in 1948, life under military occupation, the expansion of settlements and their right to self-determination. Understanding both narratives is a starting point for discussing the conflict."]],
sp:["Jerusalem","Jerusalem contains sites holy to Jews, Christians and Muslims, including the Western Wall, the Church of the Holy Sepulchre and the al-Aqsa Mosque on the site Jews call the Temple Mount. Israel considers the whole city its capital; Palestinians want East Jerusalem as the capital of their future state. Most countries keep their embassies in Tel Aviv."],
g:[["Occupation","Military control of territory by a foreign power"],["Settlement","An Israeli community built in occupied territory"],["Intifada","Arabic for ‘shaking off’; a Palestinian uprising"],["Two-state solution","A plan for an independent Palestine alongside Israel"]],
f:["Jerusalem is holy to three major religions.","The Oslo Accords were sealed with a famous handshake at the White House in 1993.","Gaza is about 41 kilometers long and 6 to 12 kilometers wide."],
fun:"The West-Eastern Divan Orchestra, founded in 1999 by Daniel Barenboim and Edward Said, brings together young Israeli and Arab musicians to play side by side.",
tl:"Ang tunggaliang Israeli–Palestino ay matagal nang alitan tungkol sa lupa, hangganan, seguridad at sariling pamamahala. Matapos ang pag-atake ng Hamas noong 7 Oktubre 2023, nagkaroon ng mapaminsalang digmaan sa Gaza hanggang sa tigil-putukan noong Oktubre 2025."});

ADDART({id:"isis",t:"The Islamic State Group",c:"The Modern World",i:"⚠️",w:"Islamic State",r:["terrorism","iraqcountry","saddam","sept11","genocide"],
p:[["A terrorist group","The Islamic State, also called ISIS or ISIL, is an extremist militant group."],["A self-declared ‘caliphate’","In 2014 it seized large parts of Iraq and Syria."],["Defeated but not gone","It lost its territory by 2019 but still carries out attacks."]],
l:"The Islamic State, known as ISIS, ISIL or Daesh, is a jihadist militant group that follows an extreme interpretation of Sunni Islam rejected by the vast majority of Muslims. It grew out of al-Qaeda in Iraq after the 2003 US-led invasion and took advantage of the Syrian civil war. In June 2014 it captured Mosul, Iraq’s second-largest city, and its leader Abu Bakr al-Baghdadi declared a ‘caliphate’. At its height it controlled territory roughly the size of Britain, home to millions of people, where it imposed brutal rule, carried out public executions, destroyed ancient monuments and committed genocide against the Yazidi people. It also inspired and directed attacks around the world, including in Paris in 2015. A coalition of dozens of countries, with Iraqi forces, Kurdish fighters and others, took back its territory by March 2019, and al-Baghdadi was killed in October 2019. Its branches remain active in Syria, Iraq, Africa and Afghanistan.",
s:[["Origins","Al-Qaeda in Iraq, founded by Abu Musab al-Zarqawi, fought US troops and attacked Shia Muslims to provoke sectarian war. Weakened by 2010, it rebuilt under al-Baghdadi, expanded into Syria during its civil war and broke with al-Qaeda in 2014."],["The ‘caliphate’","From 2014 the group ran its territory like a state, collecting taxes, running courts and schools and using social media propaganda to recruit tens of thousands of foreign fighters. In August 2014 it attacked the Yazidi minority in Sinjar, killing thousands of men and enslaving women and girls, crimes later recognized by UN investigators as genocide. It destroyed ancient sites such as Nimrud and parts of Palmyra."],["Attacks abroad","The group carried out or inspired attacks in many countries, including the Paris attacks of 13 November 2015, which killed 130 people, and bombings in Brussels, Istanbul and Sri Lanka. Its Afghan branch, ISIS-Khorasan, bombed Kabul airport in 2021 and attacked a concert hall near Moscow in 2024, killing 145 people."],["Defeat and persistence","Iraqi forces retook Mosul in July 2017, and Kurdish-led forces captured Raqqa in October 2017 and the last stronghold, Baghuz, in March 2019. Thousands of suspected fighters and family members were held in camps in north-east Syria. After the fall of Syria’s Assad government in December 2024, the group stepped up attacks in Syria, and its affiliates in West Africa, the Sahel and Mozambique are among the most active armed groups in the world."]],
k:[["Also called","ISIS, ISIL, Daesh"],["‘Caliphate’ declared","29 June 2014"],["Largest city held","Mosul, Iraq (2014–2017)"],["Last territory lost","Baghuz, Syria, March 2019"],["Founder of the caliphate","Abu Bakr al-Baghdadi (killed 2019)"]],
d:[["2004","Al-Qaeda in Iraq formed"],["2014","Mosul captured; caliphate declared; Yazidi genocide"],["2015","Paris attacks"],["2017","Mosul and Raqqa retaken"],["2019","Last territory lost; al-Baghdadi killed"],["2024","Concert hall attack near Moscow"]],
s2:[["Why people joined","Researchers found that recruits joined for many reasons, including anger at injustice and war, a search for identity and belonging, promises of money or marriage, and slick online propaganda. Programs to counter radicalization focus on families, schools, online platforms and former extremists."],["Muslims against ISIS","Muslim scholars around the world issued detailed rejections of the group’s ideas, and most of its victims were Muslims. Many governments and Muslim leaders prefer the name Daesh, an Arabic acronym that the group dislikes, to avoid calling it Islamic or a state."]],
sp:["Saving heritage","Before and after ISIS destroyed monuments, archaeologists used photographs and 3D scanning to record sites. Mosul’s al-Nuri Mosque and its leaning minaret, blown up by ISIS in 2017, were rebuilt with UNESCO’s help and reopened in 2025."],
g:[["Jihadist","A militant who uses violence claiming to fight a holy war"],["Caliphate","An Islamic state led by a caliph"],["Radicalization","The process of adopting extreme views"],["Affiliate","A group that joins or allies with a larger organization"]],
f:["At its height, ISIS controlled an area about the size of Britain.","The Yazidi genocide was recognized by UN investigators.","Most of the group’s victims were Muslims."],
fun:"The group dislikes the name Daesh partly because it sounds like Arabic words meaning ‘to trample’ or ‘one who sows discord’.",
tl:"Ang Islamic State, o ISIS, ay isang ekstremistang grupong militante na sumakop sa malaking bahagi ng Iraq at Syria noong 2014. Natalo ito sa teritoryo noong 2019, ngunit patuloy pa ring umaatake ang mga sangay nito sa iba’t ibang bansa."});

ADDART({id:"taiwanstrait",t:"The Taiwan Question",c:"The Modern World",i:"🌏",w:"Political status of Taiwan",r:["modernchina","china","coldwar","chip","southchinasea"],
p:[["A disputed island","Taiwan governs itself, but China claims it as part of its territory."],["Since 1949","The dispute dates from the end of China’s civil war."],["A global flashpoint","Tensions over Taiwan could involve the world’s largest powers."]],
l:"The Taiwan question is the dispute over the political status of Taiwan, an island of about 23 million people off the coast of China. Taiwan is governed by the Republic of China (ROC), whose government retreated there in 1949 after losing the Chinese civil war to the Communists, who founded the People’s Republic of China (PRC) on the mainland. The PRC says Taiwan is a province of China that must eventually be unified with the mainland, by force if necessary. Taiwan, which became a democracy in the 1990s, has its own government, military, currency and elections, and most of its people identify as Taiwanese and prefer to keep the current situation, often called the status quo. Only a dozen countries formally recognize the ROC, but many keep unofficial ties, and the United States sells it weapons. Chinese military drills around the island have grown larger, and the Taiwan Strait is considered one of the world’s most dangerous flashpoints.",
s:[["History","Taiwan was settled by Indigenous Austronesian peoples for thousands of years. Parts were ruled by the Dutch and Spanish in the 1600s, then by Qing China, and by Japan from 1895 to 1945. After Japan’s defeat, the ROC took control, and in 1949 Chiang Kai-shek’s government moved there."],["Diplomatic recognition","Until 1971 the ROC held China’s seat at the United Nations. UN Resolution 2758 gave the seat to the PRC, and most countries switched recognition. The United States recognized Beijing in 1979 but passed the Taiwan Relations Act, promising to help Taiwan defend itself while keeping ‘strategic ambiguity’ about whether it would intervene."],["Democracy","Taiwan was under martial law from 1949 to 1987. It held its first direct presidential election in 1996, despite Chinese missile tests nearby. Power has passed peacefully between the Kuomintang (KMT), which favors closer ties with China, and the Democratic Progressive Party (DPP), which emphasizes Taiwan’s separate identity. Lai Ching-te of the DPP became president in May 2024."],["Rising tensions","China has held large military exercises around Taiwan, including in 2022 after a visit by US House Speaker Nancy Pelosi, and in several drills since, simulating blockades. China calls such moves a response to ‘separatists’; Taiwan and its partners call them intimidation. Taiwan makes most of the world’s most advanced computer chips, so a conflict would affect the global economy."]],
k:[["Population","About 23 million"],["Government","Republic of China (Taiwan)"],["Claimed by","People’s Republic of China"],["Countries recognizing the ROC","About 12"],["President","Lai Ching-te (since 2024)"],["Width of the Taiwan Strait","About 130–180 km"]],
d:[["1895","Japan takes Taiwan"],["1945","Japan’s rule ends"],["1949","ROC government moves to Taiwan"],["1971","UN seat passes to the PRC"],["1979","US recognizes Beijing; Taiwan Relations Act"],["1987","Martial law ends"],["1996","First direct presidential election"],["2022","Major Chinese drills after Pelosi visit"]],
s2:[["The ‘One China’ question","Beijing’s One China principle says there is one China and Taiwan is part of it. Many countries, including the United States, have a ‘One China policy’ that acknowledges Beijing’s position without necessarily accepting it. These carefully different wordings allow countries to trade with both sides."],["Taiwanese identity","Surveys by National Chengchi University show that most people in Taiwan now identify as Taiwanese rather than Chinese, and that the largest groups prefer to maintain the status quo rather than declare formal independence or unify soon."]],
sp:["The silicon shield","Taiwan’s TSMC makes the large majority of the world’s most advanced semiconductors, used in phones, computers and artificial intelligence. Some people argue this ‘silicon shield’ makes war less likely because the whole world depends on Taiwan; others warn it makes Taiwan even more strategically important."],
g:[["Status quo","The existing state of affairs"],["Sovereignty","Full authority of a state over its territory"],["Unification","Joining together under one government"],["Strategic ambiguity","Deliberately not saying what one would do"]],
f:["Taiwan uses the name Republic of China officially.","Taiwan competes in the Olympics as ‘Chinese Taipei’.","Taiwan was the first place in Asia to legalize same-sex marriage, in 2019."],
fun:"Taiwan’s Taipei 101 was the world’s tallest building from 2004 to 2010 and has a giant steel ball inside that sways to steady the tower in typhoons and earthquakes.",
tl:"Ang usapin ng Taiwan ay alitan tungkol sa katayuan ng isla. May sariling pamahalaan at halalan ang Taiwan, ngunit inaangkin ito ng China bilang bahagi ng teritoryo nito. Itinuturing ang Taiwan Strait na isa sa pinakamapanganib na lugar ng tensyon sa mundo."});

ADDART({id:"southchinasea",t:"The South China Sea Dispute",c:"The Modern World",i:"🌊",w:"Territorial disputes in the South China Sea",r:["taiwanstrait","ph","pacific","fish","un"],
p:[["Competing claims","Several countries claim islands, reefs and waters in the South China Sea."],["The nine-dash line","China claims most of the sea; a tribunal ruled in 2016 that this has no legal basis."],["Clashes at sea","Coast guard confrontations, especially between China and the Philippines, have grown."]],
l:"The South China Sea dispute involves competing claims by China, Taiwan, Vietnam, the Philippines, Malaysia and Brunei to islands, reefs and waters in the South China Sea, one of the world’s busiest shipping routes, carrying about a third of global maritime trade. The sea also has rich fishing grounds and possible oil and gas. China claims most of the sea within a ‘nine-dash line’ drawn on its maps. In 2016, an international tribunal set up under the UN Convention on the Law of the Sea ruled in a case brought by the Philippines that China’s claims to historic rights within the line had no legal basis; China rejected the ruling. China has built artificial islands with airstrips and military facilities on reefs in the Spratly Islands. Its coast guard has used water cannons and blocking tactics against Philippine vessels at places such as Scarborough Shoal and Second Thomas Shoal, which the Philippines calls part of the West Philippine Sea. Other countries, including the United States, sail through the area to assert freedom of navigation.",
s:[["The claims","The main disputed features are the Paracel Islands, the Spratly Islands and Scarborough Shoal. Most are tiny reefs and rocks. Vietnam, the Philippines, Malaysia and Brunei base their claims mainly on the Law of the Sea, which gives coastal countries a 200-nautical-mile exclusive economic zone (EEZ). China and Taiwan point to historic records."],["The 2016 ruling","After a standoff at Scarborough Shoal in 2012, the Philippines took China to an arbitral tribunal in 2013. On 12 July 2016, the tribunal ruled that the nine-dash line had no legal basis, that none of the Spratly features could generate an EEZ and that China had violated Philippine rights. China called the ruling ‘null and void’."],["Artificial islands","From 2013 to 2016, China dredged sand onto reefs to create about 1,200 hectares of new land, building runways, ports and radar installations. Other claimants have also built on features they hold, but on a much smaller scale."],["Confrontations","Since 1999, the Philippines has kept a few marines aboard the BRP Sierra Madre, an old navy ship deliberately grounded on Second Thomas Shoal (Ayungin Shoal). Chinese vessels have repeatedly blocked resupply missions; in June 2024 a clash left a Filipino sailor severely injured. Confrontations near Scarborough Shoal increased in 2025 and 2026, including water cannon incidents and Chinese military drills."]],
k:[["Claimants","China, Taiwan, Vietnam, Philippines, Malaysia, Brunei"],["Main features","Paracels, Spratlys, Scarborough Shoal"],["Share of world sea trade","About one-third"],["Arbitral ruling","12 July 2016"],["Philippine name for its waters","West Philippine Sea"]],
d:[["1947","China’s maps show an eleven-dash line, later nine"],["1974","China seizes the Paracels from South Vietnam"],["1999","BRP Sierra Madre grounded at Second Thomas Shoal"],["2012","Scarborough Shoal standoff"],["2013–16","China builds artificial islands"],["2016","Tribunal rules for the Philippines"],["2024","Violent clash at Second Thomas Shoal"]],
s2:[["Fishermen caught in the middle","Fishermen from the Philippines, Vietnam and other countries depend on the sea’s rich fishing grounds and have been chased away, had boats rammed or been detained. Overfishing and reef destruction from island building also threaten marine life."],["Seeking rules","ASEAN countries and China have discussed a code of conduct for the South China Sea since 2002, but a binding agreement has not been finalized. The Philippines has strengthened ties with the United States, Japan and Australia, including joint patrols."]],
sp:["What makes an island?","Under the Law of the Sea, a true island that can sustain human habitation gets a 200-nautical-mile EEZ, a rock above water at high tide gets only 12 nautical miles of territorial sea, and a feature underwater at high tide gets none. Building on a reef does not change its legal status, the 2016 tribunal ruled."],
g:[["EEZ","Exclusive economic zone, 200 nautical miles from a coast"],["Arbitration","Settling a dispute through an independent tribunal"],["Reef","A ridge of rock or coral near the sea surface"],["Freedom of navigation","The right of ships to sail through international waters"]],
f:["About a third of global shipping passes through the South China Sea.","The 2016 ruling is legally binding but China does not accept it.","The Philippines named its western waters the West Philippine Sea in 2012."],
fun:"The BRP Sierra Madre, a rusting World War II-era ship, is still officially a commissioned vessel of the Philippine Navy so that it can serve as an outpost.",
tl:"Ang alitan sa South China Sea ay tungkol sa magkakasalungat na pag-angkin ng China, Taiwan, Vietnam, Pilipinas, Malaysia at Brunei sa mga isla at karagatan. Pinasyahan ng isang tribunal noong 2016 na walang batayang legal ang ‘nine-dash line’ ng China, ngunit tinanggihan ito ng China."});

ADDART({id:"racism",t:"Racism",c:"Society",i:"🤝",w:"Racism",r:["segregation","apartheid","slavetrade","antisemitism","hrights","colonialism"],
p:[["Prejudice by race","Racism is treating people unfairly because of their race or ethnicity."],["Not just individuals","It can be built into laws and institutions as well as personal attitudes."],["No biological basis","Science shows humans are one species with no separate biological races."]],
l:"Racism is the belief that some races or ethnic groups are superior to others, and the prejudice, discrimination and hostility directed at people because of their race or ethnicity. It can be expressed by individuals, through insults, violence or unfair treatment, and can also be built into laws, institutions and social systems, which is sometimes called structural or systemic racism. Racism has justified slavery, colonialism, segregation, apartheid and genocide. In the 18th and 19th centuries, so-called scientific racism falsely ranked human groups, but modern genetics shows that all humans share about 99.9 percent of their DNA and that there is more genetic variation within groups than between them, so ‘race’ is a social idea rather than a biological category. International law, including a UN convention of 1965, bans racial discrimination. Debates continue about how to measure racism and the best ways to reduce it.",
s:[["Origins of racist ideas","Prejudice against outsiders is ancient, but modern racism took shape as European powers enslaved Africans and colonized other continents. Thinkers divided humanity into ranked races to justify these systems. Such ideas shaped laws on citizenship, marriage and immigration in many countries."],["Forms of racism","Racism can be open, as in hate crimes or racial slurs, or subtle, such as unfair treatment in hiring, housing, policing or health care. Researchers have shown, for example, that identical job applications with different names receive different numbers of replies. Colorism, preference for lighter skin, exists in many societies, including in Asia."],["Fighting racism","Civil rights movements, anti-colonial struggles and the campaign against apartheid achieved major legal changes. The International Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Racial Discrimination was adopted in 1965. In 2020, the murder of George Floyd by a police officer in Minneapolis sparked Black Lives Matter protests around the world."],["Debates","People disagree about how much racism remains, how to measure it, and what to do. Some support policies such as affirmative action to correct past inequalities; others argue that laws should be strictly race-blind. Education, contact between groups and fair institutions are widely seen as ways to reduce prejudice."]],
k:[["Definition","Prejudice or discrimination based on race or ethnicity"],["UN convention","ICERD, 1965"],["International day","21 March, International Day for the Elimination of Racial Discrimination"],["Human DNA shared","About 99.9%"]],
d:[["1865","Slavery abolished in the United States"],["1948","Apartheid begins in South Africa"],["1960","Sharpeville massacre in South Africa"],["1964","US Civil Rights Act"],["1965","UN convention against racial discrimination"],["1994","Apartheid ends"],["2020","Global protests after George Floyd’s murder"]],
s2:[["Race and genetics","The Human Genome Project, completed in 2003, confirmed how genetically similar all humans are. Traits like skin color depend on a few genes that adapted to sunlight levels, and they say little about other differences. Scientists still study ancestry for medicine, but not to rank people."],["The 21 March date","The International Day for the Elimination of Racial Discrimination marks 21 March 1960, when police in Sharpeville, South Africa, killed 69 people protesting against apartheid pass laws."]],
sp:["The contact hypothesis","In 1954 the psychologist Gordon Allport proposed that prejudice between groups can be reduced when people meet as equals, work toward shared goals and have support from authorities. Hundreds of studies since then have found that such contact usually reduces prejudice."],
g:[["Prejudice","An unfair opinion formed without good reason"],["Discrimination","Treating people unfairly because of a group they belong to"],["Ethnicity","Shared culture, language or ancestry"],["Systemic","Built into a whole system or institution"]],
f:["All humans share about 99.9 percent of their DNA.","There is more genetic variation within so-called racial groups than between them.","21 March is the UN day against racial discrimination."],
fun:"Every person alive today is descended from ancestors who lived in Africa, so in a deep sense all humans are African.",
tl:"Ang rasismo ay hindi makatarungang pagtrato sa tao dahil sa kaniyang lahi o etnisidad. Maaari itong nasa ugali ng indibidwal o nakabaon sa mga batas at institusyon, at ipinakikita ng agham na iisang uri lamang ang lahat ng tao."});

ADDART({id:"lgbtrights",t:"LGBTQ Rights",c:"Society",i:"🏳️‍🌈",w:"LGBTQ rights by country or territory",r:["hrights","feminism","relig","democ","taiwanstrait"],
p:[["Equal rights?","LGBTQ rights concern lesbian, gay, bisexual, transgender and queer people."],["Huge differences","Some countries allow same-sex marriage; others punish same-sex relations."],["Ongoing debates","Issues such as marriage and transgender rights remain controversial."]],
l:"LGBTQ rights are the legal and social rights of lesbian, gay, bisexual, transgender and queer people. They include the decriminalization of same-sex relationships, protection from discrimination and violence, recognition of same-sex couples, including marriage, and the legal recognition of transgender people’s gender. Laws differ enormously around the world. The Netherlands became the first country to allow same-sex marriage in 2001, and by 2026 it was legal in nearly 40 countries, including Taiwan in 2019 and Thailand in 2025, the first in Asia and Southeast Asia respectively. At the same time, about 60 countries still criminalize same-sex relations, and a few allow the death penalty. Supporters see LGBTQ rights as human rights and equality before the law. Many religious and social conservatives disagree, often based on beliefs about marriage, family and gender, and on the rights of parents and religious freedom. Debates about transgender issues, such as sports and medical care for young people, are especially intense.",
s:[["History","Same-sex relationships have existed in all cultures and periods, viewed differently in different societies. Many countries criminalized them in the 19th and 20th centuries, sometimes through laws introduced by colonial powers. Nazi Germany sent thousands of gay men to concentration camps."],["The modern movement","The Stonewall uprising, when patrons of a New York bar resisted a police raid on 28 June 1969, is often seen as the start of the modern gay rights movement, and Pride marches are held each June in its memory. In 1990 the World Health Organization removed homosexuality from its list of mental illnesses; 17 May is now marked as a day against homophobia."],["Laws around the world","Same-sex marriage is legal in most of Western Europe and the Americas, in Australia, New Zealand, South Africa and parts of Asia. Many other countries offer civil unions. Same-sex relations are criminalized in parts of Africa, the Middle East and Asia, and some countries have recently tightened such laws."],["Areas of debate","Supporters argue for equal treatment in marriage, adoption, employment and services. Opponents argue that marriage should be between a man and a woman or that religious groups should not be forced to act against their beliefs. Debates about transgender people include access to facilities, participation in women’s sports and medical treatments for minors, where countries have taken different approaches as evidence is reviewed."]],
k:[["First same-sex marriage law","Netherlands, 2001"],["Countries with same-sex marriage","Nearly 40 (2026)"],["First in Asia","Taiwan, 2019"],["Stonewall uprising","28 June 1969"],["Removed from WHO illness list","17 May 1990"]],
d:[["1969","Stonewall uprising in New York"],["1990","WHO removes homosexuality from its illness list"],["2001","Netherlands legalizes same-sex marriage"],["2015","US Supreme Court legalizes same-sex marriage nationwide"],["2019","Taiwan legalizes same-sex marriage"],["2025","Thailand legalizes same-sex marriage"]],
s2:[["Public opinion","Surveys show acceptance of gay and lesbian people rising quickly in many countries, especially among young people, while remaining low in others. Opinion is often linked to religion, age, education and whether people know someone who is openly LGBTQ."],["The rainbow flag","The rainbow flag was designed by the artist Gilbert Baker for the 1978 San Francisco Pride celebration. Its colors stand for the diversity of the community, and newer versions add stripes for transgender people and people of color."]],
sp:["Words and identity","LGBTQ stands for lesbian, gay, bisexual, transgender and queer or questioning; some versions add more letters. Many cultures also have their own traditional terms for gender-diverse people, such as the bakla in the Philippines, the hijra in South Asia and Two-Spirit people among some Indigenous North Americans."],
g:[["Decriminalize","To stop treating something as a crime"],["Civil union","Legal recognition of a couple without marriage"],["Transgender","Having a gender identity different from one’s sex at birth"],["Homophobia","Fear or hatred of gay people"]],
f:["The Netherlands was the first country to allow same-sex marriage.","Taiwan was the first place in Asia to legalize same-sex marriage.","Pride marches are held each June in memory of Stonewall."],
fun:"The first Pride march, in New York in 1970, was called the Christopher Street Liberation Day March, after the street where the Stonewall Inn stands.",
tl:"Ang mga karapatang LGBTQ ay tumutukoy sa mga karapatang legal at panlipunan ng mga lesbiyana, gay, bisexual, transgender at queer. Pinapayagan ng halos 40 bansa ang same-sex marriage, habang pinarurusahan pa ito sa ilang bansa, at patuloy ang mga debate."});

ADDART({id:"guncontrol",t:"Gun Control",c:"Society",i:"🔫",w:"Gun control",r:["law","police","usacountry","terrorism","democ"],
p:[["Regulating firearms","Gun control means laws that limit who can own guns and what kinds."],["Very different laws","Countries range from strict bans to wide access."],["A fierce debate","In the United States especially, people disagree strongly about gun laws."]],
l:"Gun control refers to laws and policies that regulate the making, sale, ownership and use of firearms. Measures include licensing, background checks, waiting periods, bans on certain weapons such as automatic rifles, limits on magazine size, safe-storage rules and taking guns from people judged dangerous. Countries vary widely: Japan, the United Kingdom and Australia have strict laws and low rates of gun deaths, while the United States has more civilian guns than people, about 120 per 100 residents, and the highest rate of gun deaths among wealthy countries. In the United States, the Second Amendment to the Constitution protects the right to keep and bear arms, which the Supreme Court has ruled is an individual right. Supporters of stricter laws argue that they save lives; opponents argue that they punish law-abiding citizens, infringe on rights and do little to stop criminals. Mass shootings have often led to new laws in some countries.",
s:[["Australia and Britain","After a gunman killed 35 people at Port Arthur, Tasmania, on 28 April 1996, Australia banned semi-automatic and pump-action rifles and shotguns and bought back about 650,000 guns. After 16 children and a teacher were shot dead at a school in Dunblane, Scotland, in March 1996, Britain banned almost all private handguns. New Zealand banned most semi-automatic weapons after a terrorist killed 51 people at two Christchurch mosques in 2019."],["The United States","The Second Amendment, adopted in 1791, says: ‘A well regulated Militia, being necessary to the security of a free State, the right of the people to keep and bear Arms, shall not be infringed.’ In District of Columbia v. Heller (2008), the Supreme Court ruled it protects an individual’s right to own a handgun at home. More than 46,000 Americans died from guns in 2023, more than half of them by suicide."],["Arguments for stricter laws","Supporters point to studies linking more guns and weaker laws with more deaths, especially suicides and domestic violence killings, and to lower death rates in countries with strict laws. They favor measures such as universal background checks and ‘red flag’ laws."],["Arguments against","Opponents argue that gun ownership is a basic right and a means of self-defense, that criminals ignore laws, that most gun owners are responsible, and that problems such as mental health and crime should be addressed instead. Many also value hunting and sport shooting as traditions."]],
k:[["US civilian guns","About 120 per 100 people"],["US gun deaths, 2023","More than 46,000"],["Second Amendment","1791"],["Australia’s buyback","About 650,000 guns (1996–97)"],["Strictest examples","Japan, United Kingdom, Singapore"]],
d:[["1791","US Second Amendment adopted"],["1934","US regulates machine guns"],["1996","Port Arthur massacre; Australia’s gun reforms"],["1997","Britain bans handguns"],["2008","Heller decision in the USA"],["2019","New Zealand bans most semi-automatic weapons"],["2022","US passes its first major federal gun law in decades"]],
s2:[["Japan","Japan has some of the strictest gun laws in the world. Owners must attend classes, pass written and shooting tests, undergo mental health and background checks and have their guns and ammunition inspected. Gun deaths are extremely rare."],["Guns and suicide","Research shows that access to a gun makes suicide attempts far more likely to be fatal, which is why safe storage and temporary removal of guns during crises are promoted by health experts. People in distress are encouraged to talk to someone they trust or contact a local helpline."]],
sp:["Switzerland","Switzerland has many guns because of its militia army, in which many men keep service weapons at home, yet gun murders are rare. Supporters of gun rights cite it as an example; others note that Switzerland has strict licensing, registration and training rules."],
g:[["Firearm","A gun"],["Background check","Checking a buyer’s criminal and mental health records"],["Semi-automatic","A gun that fires one shot each time the trigger is pulled and reloads itself"],["Buyback","A program in which the government pays people to hand in guns"]],
f:["The United States has more civilian guns than people.","Australia bought back about 650,000 guns after 1996.","Japan’s strict laws make gun deaths extremely rare."],
fun:"Switzerland holds the world’s largest rifle shooting festival, the Feldschiessen, in which over 100,000 people take part each year.",
tl:"Ang gun control ay mga batas na naglilimita kung sino ang maaaring magmay-ari ng baril at anong uri. Iba-iba ito sa bawat bansa, at sa Estados Unidos lalo na, matindi ang pagtatalo tungkol sa mga batas sa baril."});

ADDART({id:"antivax",t:"Vaccine Hesitancy",c:"Society",i:"💉",w:"Vaccine hesitancy",r:["vacc","mrna","smallpox","misinformation","conspiracy"],
p:[["Delaying or refusing vaccines","Vaccine hesitancy is reluctance to get vaccinated despite vaccines being available."],["A global health threat","The WHO named it one of the top ten threats to global health in 2019."],["Measles returns","Falling vaccination has led to outbreaks of diseases that had nearly disappeared."]],
l:"Vaccine hesitancy is the delay in accepting or the refusal of vaccines despite their availability. It ranges from people with mild doubts to organized ‘anti-vaccine’ movements that oppose vaccination entirely. Reasons include fears about side effects, mistrust of governments, drug companies or doctors, religious or philosophical beliefs, misinformation spread online, and complacency when diseases seem rare. Vaccines are among the most effective tools in medicine, credited with eradicating smallpox and saving millions of lives every year, and scientific studies involving millions of people have found them to be very safe, though like all medicines they can have rare side effects. In 2019 the World Health Organization listed vaccine hesitancy among the ten biggest threats to global health. Falling vaccination rates have brought back outbreaks of measles, one of the most contagious diseases; Canada lost its measles elimination status in 2025, and the United States, with record cases in 2026, was close to losing it.",
s:[["A long history","Opposition to vaccination is as old as vaccines. After Edward Jenner developed the smallpox vaccine in 1796 and Britain made vaccination compulsory in the 1850s and 1860s, anti-vaccination leagues held large protests, including a march in Leicester in 1885. Britain later allowed conscientious objection."],["The MMR scare","In 1998 the doctor Andrew Wakefield published a study claiming a link between the measles, mumps and rubella (MMR) vaccine and autism. Investigations found the study was dishonest; it was retracted in 2010 and Wakefield lost his medical license. Many large studies have found no link, but the claim caused vaccination rates to fall in several countries."],["COVID-19","During the COVID-19 pandemic, vaccines were developed in record time. Billions of doses were given, and studies estimate they prevented millions of deaths. Misinformation, mandates and political divisions also increased distrust in some countries, which researchers worry has spread to other vaccines."],["Building trust","Health experts say that listening to concerns, clear honest information, trusted local health workers and making vaccines easy to get are more effective than shaming people. Some countries require vaccinations for school entry, which raises debates about personal freedom versus public health."]],
k:[["WHO top-ten threat","2019"],["First vaccine","Smallpox, Edward Jenner, 1796"],["Smallpox eradicated","1980"],["Fraudulent MMR study","1998, retracted 2010"],["Measles vaccination needed for herd immunity","About 95%"]],
d:[["1796","Jenner’s smallpox vaccine"],["1885","Anti-vaccination march in Leicester"],["1980","Smallpox declared eradicated"],["1998","Wakefield’s MMR paper"],["2010","Paper retracted"],["2019","WHO names vaccine hesitancy a top threat"],["2025","Canada loses measles elimination status"]],
s2:[["Herd immunity","When enough people are immune to a disease, it cannot spread easily, protecting babies too young to be vaccinated and people with weak immune systems. For measles, about 95 percent of people need to be immune."],["Trust and history","Some communities have reasons for mistrust, such as past unethical medical experiments. Researchers say acknowledging such history honestly helps build trust in vaccines today."]],
sp:["Measles","Measles is so contagious that one infected person can infect 9 out of 10 unvaccinated people nearby. It can cause pneumonia, brain swelling and death, and can weaken the immune system for years. Two doses of the vaccine are about 97 percent effective."],
g:[["Vaccine","A substance that trains the immune system to fight a disease"],["Hesitancy","Reluctance or delay"],["Herd immunity","Protection of a community when most people are immune"],["Retraction","Withdrawing a published study because it is wrong"]],
f:["Vaccines helped eradicate smallpox, which killed hundreds of millions of people.","The study linking MMR to autism was found to be fraudulent.","About 95 percent vaccination is needed to stop measles spreading."],
fun:"The word vaccine comes from the Latin vacca, meaning cow, because Jenner’s first vaccine used cowpox.",
tl:"Ang vaccine hesitancy ay pag-aatubili o pagtanggi sa bakuna kahit mayroon nito. Itinuring ito ng WHO na isa sa sampung pinakamalaking banta sa kalusugan noong 2019, at bumalik ang mga outbreak ng tigdas dahil sa pagbaba ng bakunahan."});

ADDART({id:"gmos",t:"The GMO Debate",c:"Science",i:"🌽",w:"Genetically modified food controversies",r:["crispr","agri","rice","mendel","foodsecurity"],
p:[["Changing crops’ genes","Genetically modified organizms (GMOs) have had their DNA altered in a laboratory."],["Widely grown","GM crops are planted on about 190 million hectares worldwide."],["Safe but controversial","Scientific bodies say approved GM foods are safe, but debates continue."]],
l:"A genetically modified organizm, or GMO, is a plant, animal or microorganizm whose DNA has been changed using genetic engineering, for example by adding a gene from another species. The first GM food sold to the public was a tomato in 1994. Today GM crops, mainly soybeans, maize, cotton and canola, are grown on about 190 million hectares, especially in the United States, Brazil, Argentina, Canada and India. Most are designed to resist insects or tolerate herbicides. Major scientific organizations, including the US National Academies of Sciences and the World Health Organization, have concluded that approved GM foods are as safe to eat as conventional foods. Critics raise concerns about environmental effects, herbicide-resistant weeds, the power of large seed companies, effects on small farmers and the right to know what is in food. Laws vary: the European Union strictly regulates GMOs, while the United States and many other countries allow them widely.",
s:[["How it works","Since ancient times, farmers have changed crops by breeding. Genetic engineering, developed in the 1970s, allows scientists to move specific genes. Bt crops contain a gene from the bacterium Bacillus thuringiensis that produces a protein toxic to certain insects but harmless to people. Newer gene-editing tools such as CRISPR make precise changes without adding foreign genes."],["Benefits claimed","Supporters say GM crops have reduced insecticide use, increased yields and farmers’ incomes, and can help feed a growing population. Examples include virus-resistant papaya, which saved Hawaii’s papaya industry in the 1990s, and Golden Rice, enriched with vitamin A to prevent childhood blindness."],["Concerns","Critics worry about weeds becoming resistant to herbicides, harm to insects, the spread of GM genes to wild plants, and corporate patents on seeds. Some consumers prefer to avoid GM foods for personal or ethical reasons, and many countries require labels. Scientists generally say these issues relate to farming practices and economics rather than food safety."],["Golden Rice","Golden Rice was developed in the late 1990s. In 2021 the Philippines became the first country to approve it for commercial planting, but in 2024 a court halted its cultivation after a petition by farmers’ and environmental groups, showing how the debate continues."]],
k:[["First GM food sold","Flavr Savr tomato, 1994"],["Area of GM crops","About 190 million hectares"],["Main GM crops","Soybeans, maize, cotton, canola"],["Scientific consensus","Approved GM foods are safe to eat"],["Golden Rice approved","Philippines, 2021"]],
d:[["1973","First genetically engineered organizm"],["1994","Flavr Savr tomato goes on sale"],["1996","Large-scale planting of GM crops begins"],["1998","Virus-resistant papaya saves Hawaii’s crop"],["2012","CRISPR gene editing developed"],["2016","US National Academies report on GM crops"],["2021","Philippines approves Golden Rice"]],
s2:[["Gene editing","Some countries now treat gene-edited crops, in which a plant’s own genes are changed without adding foreign DNA, more like conventionally bred plants. Examples include non-browning mushrooms and tomatoes with extra nutrients."],["Labelling","More than 60 countries require GM foods to be labelled. Supporters say consumers have a right to know; opponents say labels suggest a danger that science does not support."]],
sp:["The papaya that was saved","In the 1990s the papaya ringspot virus nearly destroyed Hawaii’s papaya farms. Scientists at universities developed a GM papaya resistant to the virus, released in 1998, and it is credited with rescuing the industry."],
g:[["Genetic engineering","Directly changing an organizm’s genes"],["Herbicide","A chemical that kills weeds"],["Bt crops","Crops with a bacterial gene that kills certain insects"],["Gene editing","Making precise changes to an organizm’s DNA"]],
f:["The first GM food sold was a tomato.","Most GM crops are used for animal feed, cooking oil and cotton.","The Philippines was the first country to approve Golden Rice."],
fun:"Almost all the cheese made in many countries uses an enzyme called chymosin produced by genetically modified microbes, replacing enzymes once taken from calves’ stomachs.",
tl:"Ang GMO ay organizmong binago ang DNA sa laboratoryo. Itinatanim ang mga GM crop sa mga 190 milyong ektarya, at sinasabi ng mga siyentipikong samahan na ligtas ang mga aprubadong GM na pagkain, ngunit patuloy ang debate tungkol sa kapaligiran at kontrol ng malalaking kompanya."});

ADDART({id:"animaltesting",t:"Animal Testing",c:"Science",i:"🐁",w:"Animal testing",r:["germtheory","vacc","scimeth","zoos","crispr"],
p:[["Animals in research","Animal testing uses animals in experiments for medicine, science and safety."],["Medical advances","Many medicines and vaccines were developed with animal research."],["Ethical concerns","Critics argue it causes suffering and can be replaced by other methods."]],
l:"Animal testing, or animal experimentation, is the use of animals in scientific research, including studying biology and disease, developing and testing medicines and vaccines, and checking the safety of chemicals. Mice and rats make up the large majority of animals used, along with fish, birds, rabbits, dogs, monkeys and others; estimates of the number used worldwide each year range from tens of millions to over 100 million. Animal research contributed to many medical advances, such as insulin, vaccines for polio and other diseases, and organ transplants. Opponents argue that it causes pain and suffering to sentient beings, that results in animals often do not apply to humans, and that alternatives should be used instead. Most countries regulate animal research, and scientists widely follow the ‘3Rs’: replace animals where possible, reduce their numbers and refine methods to minimize suffering. The European Union banned animal testing for cosmetics in 2013, and new methods such as organ-on-a-chip technology are growing.",
s:[["History","Ancient Greek physicians dissected animals to learn about anatomy. In the 19th century, experiments by scientists such as Louis Pasteur advanced medicine but also sparked the first anti-vivisection movements in Britain, which passed the world’s first law regulating animal experiments in 1876."],["Uses","Medicines must usually be tested in animals before being tried on people, to look for toxic effects. Genetically modified mice help scientists study diseases such as cancer and Alzheimer’s. Animals were used in space research, including the dog Laika, launched by the Soviet Union in 1957."],["The 3Rs","In 1959, the British scientists William Russell and Rex Burch proposed the principles of replacement, reduction and refinement. Ethics committees now review research plans in many countries, and animals must be given proper housing and pain relief."],["Alternatives","Scientists are developing methods such as human cells grown in the laboratory, ‘organs-on-chips’ that mimic human organs, computer models and studies of human volunteers. In 2022 the United States ended its legal requirement that all new drugs be tested on animals, and in 2025 its drug regulator announced plans to phase out animal testing for some medicines."]],
k:[["Most-used animals","Mice and rats"],["Principles","The 3Rs: replace, reduce, refine (1959)"],["First law regulating experiments","Britain, 1876"],["EU cosmetics testing ban","Fully in force 2013"]],
d:[["1876","Britain’s Cruelty to Animals Act"],["1921","Insulin discovered using research on dogs"],["1957","Laika orbits Earth"],["1959","Russell and Burch propose the 3Rs"],["2013","EU cosmetics testing ban fully in force"],["2022","US drops requirement for animal tests of new drugs"]],
s2:[["The ethical debate","Some philosophers, such as Peter Singer, argue that animals’ capacity to suffer gives them moral importance that research must respect. Many scientists argue that carefully regulated animal research is justified because it saves human and animal lives, including through veterinary medicine."],["Cosmetics","Many countries, including the EU members, India and others, have banned testing cosmetics on animals and selling newly animal-tested cosmetics. Labels such as ‘cruelty-free’ indicate products made without new animal testing."]],
sp:["Laika","Laika, a stray dog from Moscow, became the first animal to orbit the Earth aboard Sputnik 2 on 3 November 1957. She died within hours from overheating, as there was no plan to bring her back. A monument to her stands in Moscow."],
g:[["Vivisection","Experimenting on live animals"],["Sentient","Able to feel pain and pleasure"],["3Rs","Replace, reduce and refine animal use"],["Organ-on-a-chip","A small device with human cells that mimics an organ"]],
f:["Mice and rats are the most commonly used research animals.","The EU banned cosmetics testing on animals.","Britain passed the first law regulating animal experiments in 1876."],
fun:"Fruit flies are used in so much research that six Nobel Prizes have been awarded for discoveries made with them.",
tl:"Ang animal testing ay paggamit ng mga hayop sa mga eksperimento para sa medisina, agham at kaligtasan. Nakatulong ito sa pagbuo ng maraming gamot at bakuna, ngunit pinupuna ito dahil sa pagdurusa ng mga hayop, at may lumalaking mga alternatibo."});

ADDART({id:"euthanasia",t:"Euthanasia and Assisted Dying",c:"Society",i:"⚖️",w:"Euthanasia",r:["germtheory","hrights","law","relig","deathpenalty"],
p:[["End-of-life choices","Euthanasia and assisted dying involve helping a seriously ill person end their life at their request."],["Legal in some places","A growing number of countries allow it under strict rules."],["A moral debate","People disagree deeply about whether it should be allowed."]],
l:"Euthanasia and assisted dying refer to practices in which a doctor, at the request of a person who is suffering from a serious illness, either ends the person’s life or provides the means for the person to do so. Supporters argue that adults of sound mind facing unbearable suffering or terminal illness should have the right to choose a peaceful death. Opponents, including many religious groups, disability rights advocates and some doctors, argue that it devalues human life, that vulnerable people could feel pressured, that diagnoses can be wrong and that good palliative care can relieve suffering. Assisted dying is legal under strict conditions in a number of countries and regions, including the Netherlands and Belgium since 2002, Canada since 2016, Spain, New Zealand, several Australian states, Switzerland and some US states. Laws usually require repeated requests, independent medical assessments and waiting periods. In September 2026, the UK House of Commons narrowly rejected a bill to legalize assisted dying in England and Wales.",
s:[["Terms","‘Euthanasia’ comes from Greek words meaning ‘good death’. Assisted dying usually means that a doctor provides medication the person takes themselves, while euthanasia means the doctor administers it. Both are different from stopping treatment, such as turning off life support at a patient’s request, which is legal in most countries."],["Where it is legal","Switzerland has allowed assisted suicide for unselfish motives since 1942. The US state of Oregon passed its Death with Dignity Act in 1997. The Netherlands and Belgium legalized euthanasia in 2002, and Canada in 2016. Rules differ on who qualifies, for example whether people must be terminally ill."],["Arguments for","Supporters emphasize personal freedom, relief from suffering and dignity, and point to safeguards, reporting systems and surveys showing public support in many countries. They argue that people already sometimes travel abroad or die in distressing ways."],["Arguments against","Opponents worry about a ‘slippery slope’ as rules widen, pressure on elderly, poor or disabled people, effects on trust between doctors and patients, and the moral and religious belief that life should not be deliberately ended. They call for better palliative and mental health care instead."]],
k:[["Meaning of ‘euthanasia’","‘Good death’ (Greek)"],["First national laws","Netherlands and Belgium, 2002"],["Oregon law","1997"],["Canada","2016"],["UK Commons vote","Rejected, 11 September 2026 (286–270)"]],
d:[["1942","Switzerland’s law on assisted suicide"],["1997","Oregon’s Death with Dignity Act"],["2002","Netherlands and Belgium legalize euthanasia"],["2016","Canada legalizes medical assistance in dying"],["2021","Spain and New Zealand laws take effect"],["2026","UK Commons rejects an assisted dying bill"]],
s2:[["Palliative care","Palliative care focuses on relieving pain and other symptoms and supporting the emotional needs of seriously ill people and their families. The modern hospice movement was founded by Cicely Saunders in London in 1967. Both supporters and opponents of assisted dying agree it should be widely available."],["A sensitive topic","Discussions about assisted dying concern people with serious medical illness and strict legal procedures. Anyone who is struggling with thoughts of ending their life is encouraged to talk to someone they trust, a doctor or a local helpline; support is available."]],
sp:["The Hippocratic tradition","The ancient Hippocratic Oath included a promise not to give a deadly drug to anyone who asked for it. Opponents cite this tradition; supporters argue that medical ethics have changed in many ways since ancient Greece and should respect patients’ choices."],
g:[["Terminal illness","An illness that cannot be cured and will lead to death"],["Palliative care","Care that relieves suffering in serious illness"],["Safeguard","A rule meant to prevent harm or abuse"],["Hospice","A place or service caring for people near the end of life"]],
f:["The word euthanasia means ‘good death’ in Greek.","The Netherlands and Belgium were the first countries to legalize euthanasia.","Stopping treatment at a patient’s request is legal in most countries."],
fun:"The first modern hospice, St Christopher’s in London, was founded by Cicely Saunders, who trained as a nurse, social worker and doctor.",
tl:"Ang euthanasia at assisted dying ay pagtulong sa isang taong may malubhang sakit na wakasan ang buhay sa kaniyang kahilingan. Legal ito sa ilang bansa sa ilalim ng mahigpit na patakaran, ngunit malalim ang pagtatalo kung dapat ba itong payagan."});

ADDART({id:"whistleblowers",t:"Whistleblowers",c:"Society",i:"📣",w:"Whistleblower",r:["watergate","surveillance","journalism","darkweb","porkbarrel"],
p:[["Exposing wrongdoing","A whistleblower reveals illegal or unethical activity inside an organization."],["Famous cases","Whistleblowers exposed the Pentagon Papers, mass surveillance and corporate fraud."],["Heroes or traitors?","They are often praised by some and punished or condemned by others."]],
l:"A whistleblower is a person who exposes information about wrongdoing, such as fraud, corruption, danger to public health or safety, or abuse of power, within a government, company or other organization. Whistleblowers may report to managers, regulators, lawmakers or journalists. Famous examples include Daniel Ellsberg, who leaked the Pentagon Papers about the Vietnam War in 1971; Mark Felt, the ‘Deep Throat’ of Watergate; Jeffrey Wigand, who revealed tobacco industry secrets; Edward Snowden, who exposed mass surveillance in 2013; and Frances Haugen, who revealed internal Facebook research in 2021. Whistleblowers often face retaliation, including firing, lawsuits, prosecution or exile, and their actions are controversial when they involve secret government information. Many countries now have laws to protect whistleblowers who report wrongdoing through proper channels, and the European Union adopted a directive in 2019. The name comes from police officers or referees blowing a whistle to stop wrongdoing.",
s:[["The Pentagon Papers","Daniel Ellsberg, a military analyst, copied a secret government history of the Vietnam War showing that leaders had misled the public, and gave it to The New York Times, which began publishing it in June 1971. The Supreme Court allowed publication, and charges against Ellsberg were dropped because of government misconduct."],["Corporate whistleblowers","Jeffrey Wigand, a tobacco company scientist, revealed in 1996 that executives knew nicotine was addictive. Sherron Watkins warned Enron’s chairman of accounting fraud before the company collapsed in 2001. In the Philippines, Benhur Luy’s testimony exposed the pork barrel scam in 2013."],["National security leaks","Chelsea Manning, a US Army analyst, leaked hundreds of thousands of classified documents to WikiLeaks in 2010; she was sentenced to 35 years, but her sentence was commuted in 2017. Edward Snowden revealed NSA surveillance programs in 2013 and lives in Russia. WikiLeaks founder Julian Assange spent years in the Ecuadorian embassy in London and a British prison before a plea deal freed him in 2024."],["Protection and debate","Supporters say whistleblowers protect the public and democracy. Critics argue some leaks endanger lives or national security and that problems should be raised through official channels. Laws often protect people who report to proper authorities but not those who publish classified material."]],
k:[["Pentagon Papers","1971, Daniel Ellsberg"],["Snowden revelations","2013"],["Facebook Files","2021, Frances Haugen"],["EU Whistleblower Directive","2019"],["World Whistleblowers Day","23 June"]],
d:[["1971","Pentagon Papers published"],["1974","Watergate source helps bring down Nixon"],["1996","Jeffrey Wigand exposes the tobacco industry"],["2010","Chelsea Manning leaks to WikiLeaks"],["2013","Snowden reveals mass surveillance"],["2021","Frances Haugen testifies about Facebook"],["2024","Julian Assange freed after plea deal"]],
s2:[["Retaliation","Studies find many whistleblowers lose their jobs, suffer stress and struggle financially, even when they are proved right. Some laws offer rewards: in the United States, people who report fraud against the government can receive part of the money recovered."],["Secure reporting","News organizations use encrypted systems such as SecureDrop so sources can share documents anonymously. Many companies and governments now have hotlines and independent offices to receive complaints."]],
sp:["The Insider","The 1999 film The Insider, starring Russell Crowe, tells the story of Jeffrey Wigand and the TV journalists who struggled to broadcast his interview despite legal threats from the tobacco industry."],
g:[["Whistleblower","A person who exposes wrongdoing in an organization"],["Retaliation","Revenge or punishment for an action"],["Classified","Officially secret"],["Leak","Unauthorized release of secret information"]],
f:["The term ‘whistleblower’ refers to blowing a whistle to stop a foul or crime.","Daniel Ellsberg’s leak reached the US Supreme Court.","The EU adopted rules protecting whistleblowers in 2019."],
fun:"Time magazine named three whistleblowers, Cynthia Cooper, Coleen Rowley and Sherron Watkins, its Persons of the Year in 2002.",
tl:"Ang whistleblower ay taong naglalantad ng ilegal o hindi etikal na gawain sa loob ng isang organizasyon. Kabilang sa mga tanyag sina Daniel Ellsberg at Edward Snowden, at madalas silang purihin ng ilan at parusahan ng iba."});

ADDART({id:"doping",t:"Doping in Sport",c:"Sports",i:"💊",w:"Doping in sport",r:["olymp","athletics","bicycle","drugtrade"],
p:[["Cheating with drugs","Doping is using banned substances or methods to improve sports performance."],["Scandals","Famous cases include Ben Johnson, Lance Armstrong and Russia’s state-run program."],["Testing","The World Anti-Doping Agency sets rules and tests athletes worldwide."]],
l:"Doping is the use of banned substances or methods, such as steroids, stimulants, hormones or blood transfusions, to improve athletic performance. It is considered cheating because it gives an unfair advantage, and it can seriously harm athletes’ health. Doping has a long history: in the 1904 Olympic marathon, the winner was given strychnine and brandy during the race. Systematic doping grew in the 20th century, most notoriously in East Germany, which secretly gave steroids to thousands of athletes, many of them teenagers, from the 1970s to 1989. Famous scandals include Canadian sprinter Ben Johnson, stripped of his 1988 Olympic gold, cyclist Lance Armstrong, stripped of seven Tour de France titles in 2012, and Russia’s state-sponsored doping program exposed in 2015–16. The World Anti-Doping Agency, founded in 1999, sets global rules, and athletes must provide urine and blood samples and report where they are for surprise tests.",
s:[["Early doping","In the late 19th and early 20th centuries, cyclists and runners used stimulants such as caffeine, cocaine and strychnine. Danish cyclist Knud Jensen died at the 1960 Rome Olympics, and amphetamines were suspected; the IOC introduced drug testing at the 1968 Olympics."],["State and team doping","East Germany’s secret State Plan 14.25 gave performance drugs to athletes, often without their knowledge, causing lasting health damage. In cycling, the 1998 Festina affair revealed organized doping in the Tour de France. Lance Armstrong admitted in 2013 to having used banned substances throughout his career."],["Russia","Investigations by WADA, including the McLaren report of 2016, found that Russian authorities ran a doping program and swapped urine samples through a hole in a laboratory wall at the 2014 Sochi Winter Olympics. Russia was banned from competing under its own flag at several Olympic Games."],["Debates","Some argue that anti-doping rules should be relaxed, saying testing is unfair and expensive. In May 2026, the ‘Enhanced Games’ in Las Vegas let athletes use performance drugs openly; critics condemned the health risks, and only one world-best mark was set, in swimming. Most sports bodies and athletes support strict rules to protect fairness and health."]],
k:[["Global agency","World Anti-Doping Agency (WADA), 1999"],["Olympic testing began","1968"],["Ben Johnson stripped","1988"],["Lance Armstrong stripped","2012 (7 Tour de France titles)"],["Russian doping exposed","2015–2016"]],
d:[["1904","Olympic marathon winner given strychnine"],["1968","IOC begins drug testing"],["1988","Ben Johnson stripped of Olympic gold"],["1998","Festina affair in cycling"],["1999","WADA founded"],["2012","Lance Armstrong stripped of titles"],["2016","McLaren report on Russian doping"],["2026","Enhanced Games held in Las Vegas"]],
s2:[["Why athletes dope","Pressure to win, money, national pride and the belief that rivals are doping lead some athletes to cheat. Coaches and doctors are sometimes responsible. Young athletes are taught about clean sport and their responsibility for everything they consume."],["Health risks","Steroids can damage the heart, liver and hormones; blood doping can cause clots and strokes; stimulants can cause heart problems. Some former East German athletes developed serious illnesses later in life."]],
sp:["Icarus","The documentary Icarus (2017) began as a filmmaker’s experiment with doping and turned into an exposé when the head of Russia’s anti-doping laboratory, Grigory Rodchenkov, revealed the state program. It won an Academy Award."],
g:[["Doping","Using banned substances to improve performance"],["Steroids","Drugs that build muscle"],["Whereabouts","Where an athlete will be, reported for surprise tests"],["Stripped","Having a title or medal taken away"]],
f:["Drug testing at the Olympics began in 1968.","Lance Armstrong lost all seven of his Tour de France titles.","Russian officials swapped urine samples through a hole in a wall at Sochi 2014."],
fun:"Samples from athletes can be stored for 10 years and retested with new methods, so some athletes have lost medals a decade after winning them.",
tl:"Ang doping ay paggamit ng ipinagbabawal na gamot o paraan upang mapahusay ang paglalaro sa isports. Kabilang sa mga tanyag na iskandalo sina Ben Johnson, Lance Armstrong at ang programa ng Russia, at nagsusuri ang WADA ng mga atleta sa buong mundo."});

ADDART({id:"populism",t:"Populism",c:"Society",i:"📢",w:"Populism",r:["democ","elections","propaganda","fascism","phdynasties"],
p:[["The people versus the elite","Populism divides society into ‘the pure people’ and ‘the corrupt elite’."],["Left and right","Populists can be left-wing or right-wing."],["Hotly debated","Some see populism as a voice for ignored citizens; others as a threat to democracy."]],
l:"Populism is a style of politics that claims to represent ‘the people’ against an ‘elite’ that is said to be corrupt, out of touch or serving its own interests. Populist leaders often present themselves as outsiders who speak plainly and alone understand the will of ordinary people, and they may criticize courts, the media, experts and established parties. Populism can be combined with very different ideas: left-wing populists often target wealthy elites and big business, while right-wing populists often stress national identity and oppose immigration or international institutions. Political scientists such as Cas Mudde describe populism as a ‘thin ideology’ that attaches to other beliefs. Populist movements have appeared throughout history, from the People’s Party in the United States in the 1890s to Juan Perón in Argentina, and have grown in many countries in the 21st century. Supporters say populism gives a voice to neglected people; critics warn that it can weaken checks and balances and minority rights.",
s:[["History","The US People’s Party, founded in 1892 by farmers angry at banks and railroads, was one of the first movements called populist. In Latin America, leaders such as Juan Perón in Argentina built mass movements among workers in the mid-20th century. Later examples on the left include Hugo Chávez in Venezuela."],["The 21st-century rise","After the 2008 financial crisis and with rising concerns about globalization, immigration and inequality, populist parties and leaders gained support in Europe, the Americas and Asia. The 2016 Brexit referendum and the election of Donald Trump in the United States are often discussed as examples, as are leaders such as Viktor Orbán in Hungary, Jair Bolsonaro in Brazil and Rodrigo Duterte in the Philippines."],["Why people vote for populists","Research points to economic insecurity, distrust of politicians, feelings of being ignored or looked down on, cultural change and the rise of social media, which lets leaders speak directly to voters. Populists often promise quick, simple solutions."],["Effects on democracy","Some scholars argue populism can bring excluded groups into politics and force established parties to respond. Others document cases where populist governments in power weakened independent courts, the press and elections. Whether populism helps or harms democracy is debated and may depend on the country and leader."]],
k:[["Core idea","‘The people’ against ‘the elite’"],["Type","A ‘thin ideology’ (Cas Mudde)"],["Early example","US People’s Party, 1892"],["Can be","Left-wing or right-wing"]],
d:[["1892","US People’s Party founded"],["1946","Juan Perón elected in Argentina"],["1999","Hugo Chávez becomes president of Venezuela"],["2008","Financial crisis fuels discontent"],["2016","Brexit vote; Trump elected"]],
s2:[["Spotting populist language","Political scientists look for claims that one leader or party alone represents ‘the real people’, that opponents are illegitimate or enemies, and that institutions blocking the leader are part of a corrupt system. Ordinary politicians also use some of this language, so it is a matter of degree."],["Different meanings","In everyday speech, ‘populist’ is often used as an insult for any politician who promises popular things. Scholars use the word more precisely, and some politicians proudly call themselves populists."]],
sp:["The origin of the word","Populism comes from the Latin populus, ‘the people’. In ancient Rome, politicians called populares sought support from ordinary citizens against the senatorial aristocracy, an early version of the people-versus-elite idea."],
g:[["Elite","A small group with great power or wealth"],["Ideology","A set of political beliefs"],["Checks and balances","Ways that branches of government limit each other’s power"],["Globalization","The growing connection of economies and cultures worldwide"]],
f:["Populists can be on the left or the right.","The US People’s Party of the 1890s was one of the first populist movements.","‘Populus’ is Latin for ‘the people’."],
fun:"The People’s Party’s supporters were sometimes called ‘Populists’ by newspapers, and the name stuck as a label for the whole style of politics.",
tl:"Ang populismo ay estilo ng pulitika na nagsasabing kinakatawan nito ang ‘taumbayan’ laban sa isang tiwaling ‘elite’. Maaaring makakaliwa o makakanan ang mga populista, at pinagtatalunan kung nakatutulong o nakapipinsala ito sa demokrasya."});

ADDART({id:"tobacco",t:"Tobacco and Smoking",c:"Society",i:"🚭",w:"Tobacco smoking",r:["who","cancer","whistleblowers","drugtrade","propaganda"],
p:[["A deadly habit","Smoking tobacco is the world’s leading cause of preventable death."],["Hidden harm","Tobacco companies hid evidence of its dangers for decades."],["Fighting back","Taxes, ad bans and smoke-free laws have cut smoking in many countries."]],
l:"Tobacco is a plant whose dried leaves are smoked in cigarettes, cigars and pipes, chewed or sniffed. It contains nicotine, a highly addictive drug. Native peoples of the Americas used tobacco for thousands of years, and Europeans spread it worldwide after Columbus’s voyages. Mass-produced cigarettes made smoking hugely popular in the 20th century. Studies in the 1950s proved that smoking causes lung cancer, and it is now known to cause heart disease, stroke, many cancers and lung diseases. The World Health Organization says tobacco kills more than 8 million people a year, including about 1.3 million non-smokers exposed to second-hand smoke, and there are about 1.2 billion tobacco users worldwide. Tobacco companies long denied the evidence and marketed to young people, leading to huge lawsuits. Higher taxes, advertising bans, warning labels and smoke-free laws have cut smoking in many countries, while e-cigarettes and vaping have created a new debate.",
s:[["History","Tobacco was used in religious and healing ceremonies by Indigenous Americans. Jean Nicot, a French diplomat, sent tobacco to the French court in 1560, and nicotine is named after him. In 1604 King James I of England wrote A Counterblaste to Tobacco, calling smoking harmful. The cigarette-rolling machine invented by James Bonsack in 1881 made cigarettes cheap."],["Proving the harm","In 1950, studies by Richard Doll and Austin Bradford Hill in Britain and by Ernst Wynder and Evarts Graham in the United States showed a strong link between smoking and lung cancer. The US Surgeon General’s report of 11 January 1964 officially declared that smoking causes lung cancer."],["The tobacco industry","Internal documents later revealed that companies knew about nicotine’s addictiveness and the cancer risk while publicly casting doubt on the science. In 1998, major US companies agreed to pay states about 206 billion dollars over 25 years in the Master Settlement Agreement and to limit marketing to young people."],["Tobacco control","The WHO Framework Convention on Tobacco Control, adopted in 2003, is one of the most widely ratified treaties in history. Measures include high taxes, plain packaging, graphic warnings and smoke-free public places. E-cigarettes, which heat nicotine liquid, may help some smokers quit but have become popular among teenagers, and countries regulate them very differently."]],
k:[["Addictive drug","Nicotine"],["Deaths per year","More than 8 million (WHO)"],["Users worldwide","About 1.2 billion"],["Key report","US Surgeon General, 1964"],["Global treaty","WHO Framework Convention on Tobacco Control (2003)"]],
d:[["1492","Columbus’s crew encounters tobacco"],["1560","Jean Nicot sends tobacco to France"],["1881","Cigarette-rolling machine invented"],["1950","Studies link smoking to lung cancer"],["1964","US Surgeon General’s report"],["1998","Master Settlement Agreement"],["2003","WHO tobacco treaty adopted"]],
s2:[["Generational bans","Some governments have proposed banning tobacco sales to anyone born after a certain year, so that a smoke-free generation grows up. New Zealand passed such a law in 2022 but repealed it in 2024; the Maldives introduced one in 2025, and other countries have debated it."],["Quitting","Within a year of quitting, a smoker’s risk of heart disease drops sharply, and after 10–15 years the risk of lung cancer falls by about half. Nicotine replacement, medicines and counseling greatly improve the chances of quitting."]],
sp:["Doctors in ads","In the 1930s and 1940s, cigarette ads in the United States sometimes showed doctors recommending brands, with slogans claiming that more doctors smoked one brand than any other. Such ads are now seen as a classic example of misleading marketing."],
g:[["Nicotine","The addictive drug in tobacco"],["Second-hand smoke","Smoke breathed in by people near a smoker"],["Vaping","Inhaling vapor from an e-cigarette"],["Plain packaging","Cigarette packs without brand logos or colors"]],
f:["Nicotine is named after Jean Nicot.","Tobacco kills more than 8 million people a year.","Australia was the first country to require plain cigarette packaging, in 2012."],
fun:"King James I wrote in 1604 that smoking was ‘loathsome to the eye, hateful to the nose, harmful to the brain, dangerous to the lungs’.",
tl:"Ang paninigarilyo ang nangungunang sanhi ng maiiwasang kamatayan sa mundo, at pumapatay ito ng mahigit 8 milyong tao bawat taon. Itinago ng mga kompanya ng tabako ang ebidensya ng panganib nito sa loob ng maraming dekada, ngunit nabawasan ang paninigarilyo dahil sa buwis, pagbabawal sa patalastas at mga batas."});

