/* data/modern23.js */
// New articles, 15 November 2026 (part 6): Philippine corruption scandals and the Mamasapano clash. Facts checked 15 November 2026.
ADDART({id:"porkbarrel",t:"The Pork Barrel Scam",c:"Philippines",i:"🐷",w:"Priority Development Assistance Fund scam",r:["phgov","whistleblowers","floodcontrol","phdynasties","journalism"],
p:[["Stolen public funds","The pork barrel scam diverted billions of pesos of lawmakers’ development funds into fake organizations."],["Exposed in 2013","A whistleblower and newspaper reports revealed the scheme."],["Pork declared unconstitutional","The Supreme Court struck down the lawmakers’ fund in November 2013."]],
l:"The pork barrel scam, also called the PDAF scam or Napoles scam, was a scheme in the Philippines in which an estimated ₱10 billion of public money was diverted through fake non-governmental organizations. Members of Congress each received a yearly allocation, the Priority Development Assistance Fund (PDAF), popularly called ‘pork barrel’, which they could direct to projects in their districts. Businesswoman Janet Lim-Napoles was accused of setting up dozens of bogus foundations that received these funds for projects that never happened, with lawmakers allegedly receiving large kickbacks. The scheme was exposed in July 2013 by the Philippine Daily Inquirer, based on testimony from Benhur Luy, a former employee of Napoles. Public anger led to the ‘Million People March’ in August 2013, and in November 2013 the Supreme Court declared the PDAF unconstitutional. Three senators were charged with plunder; Napoles was convicted, but the senators were acquitted of plunder, and one was convicted of bribery.",
s:[["How the scheme worked","According to prosecutors and whistleblowers, a lawmaker would endorse a Napoles-linked NGO to receive PDAF money for livelihood projects, seedlings or farm kits. Government agencies released the funds, and the NGO produced fake documents and lists of beneficiaries, some of whom did not exist. Most of the money was allegedly split between Napoles and the lawmaker, who received a commission."],["Exposure","On 12 July 2013, the Inquirer published the first reports based on Benhur Luy’s files, after he had been held against his will by Napoles relatives. A Commission on Audit special report confirmed widespread irregularities. On 26 August 2013, tens of thousands gathered at Luneta Park in Manila to demand the abolition of pork barrel, and Napoles surrendered to President Benigno Aquino III two days later."],["Court rulings","On 19 November 2013, the Supreme Court ruled the PDAF unconstitutional, saying it let lawmakers interfere in spending after budgets were passed. In 2014 the Ombudsman charged Senators Juan Ponce Enrile, Jinggoy Estrada and Ramon ‘Bong’ Revilla Jr. with plunder. In December 2018, the Sandiganbayan acquitted Revilla of plunder but convicted Napoles and a former Revilla aide. Estrada was acquitted of plunder in January 2024 but convicted of bribery, and Enrile was cleared of plunder in October 2024. Napoles has been convicted in several cases."],["Legacy","Critics point out that many of the most powerful accused escaped conviction. Lawmakers continued to find other ways to influence where public funds go, an issue that returned with the flood control scandal of 2025. The case is remembered as a turning point in public awareness of corruption."]],
k:[["Estimated amount","About ₱10 billion"],["Main accused","Janet Lim-Napoles"],["Whistleblower","Benhur Luy"],["Exposed","12 July 2013, Philippine Daily Inquirer"],["PDAF ruled unconstitutional","19 November 2013"],["Million People March","26 August 2013"]],
d:[["2013","Inquirer exposes the scam in July"],["2013","Million People March at Luneta, 26 August"],["2013","Supreme Court strikes down PDAF, 19 November"],["2014","Three senators charged with plunder"],["2018","Revilla acquitted of plunder; Napoles convicted"],["2024","Estrada acquitted of plunder but convicted of bribery; Enrile cleared"]],
s2:[["What is ‘pork barrel’?","The term comes from the United States, where it described government spending that benefits a politician’s home district. In the 19th century, barrels of salted pork were sometimes given to enslaved people, and crowds rushing for them became a symbol of politicians scrambling for funds."],["Plunder","In Philippine law, plunder is the crime of a public official amassing ill-gotten wealth of at least ₱50 million. It carries a sentence of reclusion perpetua, up to 40 years in prison, and is non-bailable when evidence of guilt is strong."]],
sp:["The whistleblowers","Benhur Luy, Napoles’s second cousin and former finance officer, kept digital records of transactions. With other former employees, he testified in Senate hearings and in court for years, living under the government’s Witness Protection Program."],
g:[["PDAF","Priority Development Assistance Fund, lawmakers’ discretionary fund"],["NGO","Non-governmental organization"],["Kickback","An illegal payment in return for a favor"],["Plunder","Amassing at least ₱50 million in ill-gotten wealth by a public official"]],
f:["The Supreme Court declared the PDAF unconstitutional in 2013.","The scam was exposed by a whistleblower and a newspaper.","Many of the accused lawmakers were acquitted of plunder."],
fun:"Protesters at the Million People March wore pig masks and carried toy pigs to mock the pork barrel system.",
tl:"Ang pork barrel scam ay iskema kung saan tinatayang ₱10 bilyong pondo ng mga mambabatas ang napunta sa mga pekeng NGO. Inilantad ito noong 2013 ng isang whistleblower at ng Inquirer, at idineklarang labag sa Saligang Batas ang PDAF noong Nobyembre 2013."});

ADDART({id:"floodcontrol",t:"The Flood Control Scandal",c:"Philippines",i:"🌊",w:"Flood control projects scandal in the Philippines",r:["porkbarrel","floods","phgov","phdynasties","phtyph"],
p:[["Ghost projects","The flood control scandal revealed missing, substandard or ‘ghost’ flood control projects."],["Billions lost","Officials estimated that up to ₱118 billion was lost from 2023 to 2025."],["Powerful figures charged","In 2026, a former Speaker of the House was arrested on plunder charges."]],
l:"The flood control scandal is a corruption scandal in the Philippines that broke in 2025 over government flood control projects. After deadly floods, President Ferdinand Marcos Jr. said in his State of the Nation Address in July 2025 that some projects were substandard or did not exist, and in August he revealed that about ₱100 billion of contracts had gone to just 15 contractors. Investigations by Congress, the Department of Public Works and Highways, an Independent Commission for Infrastructure and the Ombudsman uncovered allegations of ‘ghost projects’, inflated contracts and kickbacks to officials and lawmakers. The finance secretary estimated that up to ₱118 billion was lost from 2023 to 2025. Public anger led to large protests, including the Trillion Peso March on 21 September 2025. House Speaker Martin Romualdez resigned in September 2025, and in September 2026 he was arrested after the Sandiganbayan issued a warrant on plunder charges; he denies wrongdoing. Other officials, contractors and former lawmakers also face charges.",
s:[["How it started","The Philippines is hit by many typhoons, and flood control receives a large share of public works spending: about ₱545 billion from mid-2022 to 2025. In July 2025 Marcos told Congress, ‘Mahiya naman kayo’ (‘Have some shame’), and launched a website, Sumbong sa Pangulo, where citizens reported thousands of suspicious projects."],["Investigations","Senate and House hearings in September 2025 heard testimony from contractors, including Curlee and Sarah Discaya, whose companies won many contracts, and from public works engineers who described kickbacks. Marcos created the Independent Commission for Infrastructure (ICI) on 11 September 2025. In its February 2026 report, it referred 65 people to the Ombudsman for possible charges."],["Protests","On 21 September 2025, the anniversary of the 1972 declaration of martial law, tens of thousands joined rallies in Manila, including at Luneta Park and the EDSA People Power Monument, in what was called the Trillion Peso March. Church groups, students and civil society demanded accountability."],["Charges","Engineers, contractors and officials were arrested and charged from 2025. The Ombudsman filed plunder charges against Romualdez and former congressman Elizaldy ‘Zaldy’ Co, alleging they received at least ₱7.44 billion in kickbacks. On 7 September 2026, Romualdez was arrested in a Manila hospital; an arrest warrant was also issued for Co. Cases against other lawmakers and officials were under investigation or in court as of November 2026."]],
k:[["Began","2025"],["Estimated losses","Up to ₱118 billion (2023–2025, finance department estimate)"],["Independent commission","ICI, created 11 September 2025"],["Trillion Peso March","21 September 2025"],["Former Speaker arrested","Martin Romualdez, 7 September 2026"],["Status (November 2026)","Cases ongoing"]],
d:[["July 2025","Marcos denounces flood control corruption"],["Aug 2025","₱100 billion found to have gone to 15 contractors"],["Sept 2025","Speaker Romualdez resigns; ICI created; Trillion Peso March"],["Feb 2026","ICI refers 65 people to the Ombudsman"],["Sept 2026","Romualdez arrested on plunder charges"]],
s2:[["Ghost projects","A ‘ghost project’ is one that is paid for but never built, or exists only on paper. Journalists and citizens checked project sites using satellite images and visits, sometimes finding empty riverbanks where dikes were supposed to stand."],["Why it matters","Flood control protects lives and homes. Substandard or missing structures can make floods during typhoons more deadly. The scandal renewed calls for open public contracting, independent audits and limits on lawmakers’ influence over projects."]],
sp:["Citizens as watchdogs","During the scandal, ordinary Filipinos used social media to share photos of crumbling or missing flood walls in their towns, and volunteers built online maps of suspicious projects using government data. Their posts helped investigators decide where to look."],
g:[["Ghost project","A project paid for but never built"],["Kickback","An illegal payment in return for a contract"],["Plunder","A public official amassing at least ₱50 million illegally"],["Ombudsman","The official who investigates and prosecutes government wrongdoing"]],
f:["About ₱545 billion was spent on flood control from mid-2022 to 2025.","The Trillion Peso March took place on the anniversary of martial law.","A former Speaker of the House was arrested in 2026."],
fun:"Some contractors under investigation were found to own dozens of luxury cars, which became a symbol of the scandal on social media.",
tl:"Ang flood control scandal ay iskandalo ng korapsyon noong 2025 tungkol sa mga proyektong pangkontrol ng baha na palpak o hindi umiiral. Tinatayang hanggang ₱118 bilyon ang nawala, at inaresto noong Setyembre 2026 ang dating Speaker na si Martin Romualdez sa kasong plunder."});

ADDART({id:"mamasapano",t:"The Mamasapano Clash",c:"Philippines",i:"🕯️",w:"Mamasapano clash",r:["mindanao","terrorism","maguindanao","police","phgov"],
p:[["A police raid gone wrong","On 25 January 2015, a police operation to capture a wanted terrorist turned into a deadly clash."],["The SAF 44","Forty-four police commandos were killed, along with 18 rebels and 5 civilians."],["Peace process shaken","The clash delayed the Bangsamoro peace law."]],
l:"The Mamasapano clash was a deadly encounter on 25 January 2015 in Mamasapano, Maguindanao, in Mindanao. Commandos of the Philippine National Police Special Action Force (SAF) carried out Oplan Exodus, a mission to capture or kill Zulkifli Abdhir, known as Marwan, a Malaysian bomb maker wanted by the United States, and a Filipino accomplice. The commandos killed Marwan, but as they withdrew they were caught in fighting with members of the Moro Islamic Liberation Front (MILF), which was in peace talks with the government, and other armed groups. Forty-four SAF troopers, remembered as the SAF 44, were killed, along with 18 MILF fighters and 5 civilians. The operation had not been coordinated with the army or the MILF, and it caused national grief and anger. President Benigno Aquino III faced heavy criticism, and Congress delayed passing the proposed Bangsamoro Basic Law. A peace law, the Bangsamoro Organic Law, was finally passed in 2018.",
s:[["The mission","Marwan had been linked to the 2002 Bali bombings and had a US bounty of up to 5 million dollars on his head. About 390 SAF commandos entered the area at night. A team reached Marwan’s hut before dawn and shot him, but his accomplice, Basit Usman, escaped. Usman was killed by rivals months later."],["The clash","As daylight came, the commandos found themselves in an area held by the MILF and the breakaway Bangsamoro Islamic Freedom Fighters. A blocking force of the 55th Special Action Company was surrounded in a cornfield and nearly all were killed. Army reinforcements arrived late because they had not been informed in advance."],["Investigations","Senate and police board of inquiry investigations found that the suspended police chief Alan Purisima had been involved in planning despite his suspension and that the chain of command was bypassed. Aquino said he took responsibility. The SAF commander was relieved, and charges were filed against several officials."],["The peace process","The government and the MILF had signed a peace agreement in 2014. After Mamasapano, public anger stalled the Bangsamoro Basic Law in Congress. Under President Rodrigo Duterte, Congress passed the Bangsamoro Organic Law in 2018, and voters approved the new Bangsamoro Autonomous Region in 2019."]],
k:[["Date","25 January 2015"],["Place","Mamasapano, Maguindanao"],["Operation","Oplan Exodus"],["Target","Zulkifli Abdhir (Marwan)"],["Deaths","44 SAF commandos, 18 MILF fighters, 5 civilians"],["Remembrance","25 January, National Day of Remembrance for the SAF 44"]],
d:[["2014","Government and MILF sign a peace agreement"],["2015","Mamasapano clash, 25 January"],["2015","National day of mourning, 30 January"],["2017","25 January declared a day of remembrance for the SAF 44"],["2018","Bangsamoro Organic Law passed"],["2019","Bangsamoro region approved in a plebiscite"]],
s2:[["Remembering the SAF 44","The fallen commandos were given posthumous awards, and memorials were built at Camp Bagong Diwa and elsewhere. Their families have continued to call for full accountability, and their story was told in films and television programs."],["Peace and its challenges","The Bangsamoro Autonomous Region in Muslim Mindanao, created in 2019, gives the region its own parliament and government. Supporters see it as a hard-won achievement of the peace process, while challenges such as armed splinter groups and clan feuds remain."]],
sp:["Lessons for operations","Military experts studying Mamasapano point to the importance of coordination between police, the army and peace mechanisms, clear chains of command and plans for reinforcements. The clash is taught as a case study in Philippine security training."],
g:[["Special Action Force","An elite unit of the Philippine National Police"],["MILF","Moro Islamic Liberation Front, a Moro rebel group that made peace with the government"],["Chain of command","The order of authority in an organization"],["Bangsamoro","The Moro homeland in Mindanao"]],
f:["Forty-four police commandos died in the clash.","The target, Marwan, was killed during the raid.","The Bangsamoro Organic Law was passed three years after the clash."],
fun:"The Philippine government declared a national day of mourning on 30 January 2015, and many people wore black ribbons or changed their profile pictures in honor of the SAF 44.",
tl:"Ang Mamasapano clash ay naganap noong 25 Enero 2015 sa Maguindanao nang ang operasyon ng pulisya laban sa teroristang si Marwan ay humantong sa madugong sagupaan. Apatnapu’t apat na SAF commando ang napatay, at naantala nito ang batas pangkapayapaan para sa Bangsamoro."});

;
