ADDART({id:"idiamin",t:"Idi Amin",c:"People",i:"📕",w:"Idi Amin",r:["ugandacountry","colonialism","genocide","coldwar"],
p:[["Dictator of Uganda","Idi Amin ruled Uganda from 1971 to 1979."],["A reign of terror","His regime killed an estimated 100,000 to 500,000 people."],["Expelled Asians","In 1972 he expelled Uganda’s Asian community."]],
l:"Idi Amin (about 1925–2003) was a Ugandan army officer who seized power in a military coup in January 1971 and ruled Uganda as a dictator until 1979. At first welcomed by some Ugandans and foreign governments, he soon became notorious for brutality. Soldiers and security agencies killed political opponents, members of rival ethnic groups, journalists, religious leaders and ordinary people; estimates of the dead range from 100,000 to 500,000. In 1972 he expelled about 50,000 to 80,000 people of South Asian descent, seizing their businesses, which wrecked Uganda’s economy. He gave himself grand titles and became infamous internationally for erratic behavior. In 1976 his government cooperated with hijackers who brought an airliner to Entebbe, leading to an Israeli rescue raid. After he invaded Tanzania in 1978, Tanzanian troops and Ugandan exiles drove him from power in April 1979. He lived in exile in Saudi Arabia until his death in 2003 and was never tried.",
s:[["Soldier","Amin joined the British colonial army, the King’s African Rifles, in the 1940s and served in operations including the suppression of the Mau Mau uprising in Kenya. He was a champion boxer and rose quickly. After Uganda’s independence in 1962, he became army commander under Prime Minister Milton Obote."],["The coup","On 25 January 1971, while Obote was abroad, Amin took power. Britain and Israel initially welcomed the change. Within months, soldiers from ethnic groups linked to Obote were massacred, and Amin turned to Libya and the Soviet Union for support."],["Expulsion of the Asians","In August 1972, Amin gave Uganda’s Asian residents, many of Indian descent and holding British passports, 90 days to leave. Around 27,000 resettled in Britain. Their homes and businesses were handed to Amin’s supporters, and the economy collapsed."],["Fall","Repression grew, and in 1977 the Anglican Archbishop Janani Luwum was killed. In late 1978 Amin invaded Tanzania’s Kagera region. Tanzania counter-attacked with Ugandan exiles and captured Kampala on 11 April 1979. Amin fled to Libya and later Saudi Arabia, where he died on 16 August 2003."]],
k:[["Born","About 1925, Uganda"],["Died","16 August 2003, Jeddah, Saudi Arabia"],["Ruled Uganda","1971–1979"],["Estimated killed","100,000–500,000"],["Asians expelled","About 50,000–80,000 (1972)"]],
d:[["1962","Uganda becomes independent"],["1971","Amin seizes power"],["1972","Asians expelled"],["1976","Entebbe hijacking and Israeli rescue"],["1977","Archbishop Janani Luwum killed"],["1979","Overthrown by Tanzanian and exile forces"],["2003","Dies in Saudi Arabia"]],
s2:[["Operation Entebbe","In June 1976, hijackers took an Air France plane to Entebbe airport, where Amin’s forces helped guard the hostages. On 4 July 1976, Israeli commandos flew more than 4,000 kilometers and rescued 102 hostages. Three hostages, their commander Yonatan Netanyahu and dozens of Ugandan soldiers were killed."],["The Last King of Scotland","The 2006 film The Last King of Scotland, based on a novel, portrayed Amin through the eyes of a fictional Scottish doctor. Forest Whitaker won the Academy Award for Best Actor for playing Amin. The title refers to one of the many self-awarded titles Amin used."]],
sp:["Uganda’s Asians in Britain","Many of the Ugandan Asians who resettled in Britain arrived with almost nothing but went on to build successful businesses and careers. Their story is often cited as an example of refugees contributing to their new country."],
g:[["Coup","A sudden seizure of power, often by the military"],["Dictator","A ruler with total power"],["Expulsion","Forcing people to leave a country"],["Exile","Living away from one’s home country, often by force"]],
f:["Idi Amin was Uganda’s heavyweight boxing champion for years.","About 27,000 expelled Ugandan Asians settled in Britain.","Amin was never put on trial for his crimes."],
fun:"Amin awarded himself titles including ‘Conqueror of the British Empire’ and ‘Last King of Scotland’.",
tl:"Si Idi Amin ang diktador ng Uganda mula 1971 hanggang 1979. Tinatayang 100,000 hanggang 500,000 ang pinatay ng kaniyang rehimen, pinaalis niya ang mga Asyano noong 1972, at pinatalsik siya noong 1979."});

ADDART({id:"castro",t:"Fidel Castro",c:"People",i:"🇨🇺",w:"Fidel Castro",r:["cubacountry","cubancrisis","cheguevara","coldwar","socialism"],
p:[["Cuban revolutionary","Fidel Castro led the Cuban Revolution of 1959."],["Communist ruler","He ruled Cuba for nearly 50 years as a one-party state."],["Divisive legacy","Supporters praise his health and education gains; critics condemn repression."]],
l:"Fidel Castro (1926–2016) was a Cuban revolutionary and politician who led Cuba from 1959 to 2008. After leading a guerrilla war against the dictator Fulgencio Batista, he took power on 1 January 1959 and turned Cuba into a one-party communist state allied with the Soviet Union, just 150 kilometers from the United States. His government nationalized businesses and land, expanded free health care and education, and greatly increased literacy, but banned opposition parties and independent media, jailed critics and caused more than a million Cubans to leave the island. He survived the US-backed Bay of Pigs invasion in 1961, the Cuban Missile Crisis of 1962 and many assassination plots. A US trade embargo, in place since the early 1960s, and the loss of Soviet aid in 1991 brought severe economic hardship. Castro handed power to his brother Raúl in 2008 and died in 2016. He remains admired by some as an anti-imperialist and condemned by others as a dictator.",
s:[["Revolution","Castro, a lawyer, led a failed attack on the Moncada barracks on 26 July 1953 and was jailed, declaring at his trial, ‘History will absolve me.’ Released, he sailed from Mexico in 1956 with 82 fighters, including Che Guevara, aboard the yacht Granma. From the Sierra Maestra mountains, their guerrilla army grew until Batista fled on 1 January 1959."],["Turning to communism","Castro’s government seized US-owned companies and land, and the United States cut ties and imposed an embargo. In April 1961, about 1,400 CIA-trained Cuban exiles landed at the Bay of Pigs and were defeated within days. Castro declared Cuba socialist and aligned with the Soviet Union, which placed nuclear missiles there in 1962."],["Life in Castro’s Cuba","Cuba achieved high literacy and life expectancy and sent doctors abroad. But food was rationed, political dissent was punished, and many people were imprisoned. Waves of emigration included the Mariel boatlift of 1980, when about 125,000 Cubans left for Florida."],["Later years","After the Soviet Union collapsed, Cuba’s economy shrank by about a third in the ‘Special Period’. Castro fell ill in 2006, formally handed power to Raúl in 2008 and died on 25 November 2016. Cuba remained a one-party state; in 2021 large protests broke out over shortages and blackouts, and economic hardship continued into the mid-2020s."]],
k:[["Born","13 August 1926, Birán, Cuba"],["Died","25 November 2016, Havana"],["Led Cuba","1959–2008"],["Party","Communist Party of Cuba"],["Successor","Raúl Castro"]],
d:[["1953","Attack on the Moncada barracks"],["1959","Revolution triumphs on 1 January"],["1961","Bay of Pigs invasion defeated"],["1962","Cuban Missile Crisis"],["1980","Mariel boatlift"],["1991","Soviet collapse begins the ‘Special Period’"],["2008","Hands power to Raúl Castro"],["2016","Dies in Havana"]],
s2:[["The embargo","The US embargo on Cuba, begun in 1960–62, is one of the longest in modern history. The UN General Assembly has voted almost every year since 1992 to call for its end. Supporters say it pressures the government; critics say it hurts ordinary Cubans and gives the government someone to blame."],["How he is remembered","To many in Latin America and Africa, Castro symbolized defiance of US power and support for liberation movements, including Cuban troops sent to fight apartheid South Africa’s forces in Angola. Many Cuban exiles, especially in Miami, celebrated his death, remembering executions, prisons and broken families."]],
sp:["Assassination attempts","The CIA plotted many times to kill Castro in the 1960s, with schemes reportedly including poisoned cigars and an exploding seashell. A Senate committee confirmed several plots in 1975. Castro joked that if surviving assassination attempts were an Olympic event, he would win gold."],
g:[["Guerrilla","A fighter in a small, irregular armed group"],["Nationalize","To put private businesses under state ownership"],["Embargo","A ban on trade with a country"],["One-party state","A country where only one political party is allowed"]],
f:["Castro ruled Cuba for almost 50 years.","Cuba lies about 150 kilometers from Florida.","Castro gave speeches lasting many hours; one at the UN in 1960 lasted over four hours."],
fun:"Castro was a talented baseball player as a student, giving rise to a popular but untrue story that US teams once offered him a contract.",
tl:"Si Fidel Castro ang namuno sa Rebolusyong Cubano noong 1959 at sa Cuba bilang estadong komunista hanggang 2008. Pinupuri siya ng ilan dahil sa kalusugan at edukasyon, at kinokondena ng iba dahil sa panunupil."});

ADDART({id:"cheguevara",t:"Che Guevara",c:"People",i:"⭐",w:"Che Guevara",r:["castro","cubacountry","coldwar","socialism","argentinacountry"],
p:[["Revolutionary icon","Che Guevara was an Argentine-born Marxist revolutionary."],["Cuban Revolution","He was a key leader of Castro’s guerrilla war."],["Divisive image","His face became a global symbol, but his record includes executions."]],
l:"Ernesto ‘Che’ Guevara (1928–1967) was an Argentine-born Marxist revolutionary, doctor and writer who became one of the most famous figures of the 20th century. Travels across South America as a young medical student, described in his diary The Motorcycle Diaries, convinced him that poverty and inequality could only be ended by revolution. In Mexico he joined Fidel Castro and became a commander in the guerrilla war that brought Castro to power in Cuba in 1959. In the new government he oversaw revolutionary tribunals that executed opponents, and later served as head of the national bank and minister of industry. He left Cuba to spread revolution, fighting unsuccessfully in Congo and then Bolivia, where he was captured and executed on 9 October 1967. A 1960 photograph by Alberto Korda turned his face into an image printed on posters and T-shirts worldwide. Admirers see him as a symbol of idealism and rebellion; critics point to executions and authoritarian ideas.",
s:[["Early life","Guevara was born on 14 June 1928 in Rosario, Argentina, and suffered from asthma all his life. In 1952 he traveled thousands of kilometers by motorcycle and on foot with a friend, Alberto Granado, through Chile, Peru and Colombia, witnessing poverty and the conditions of miners and Indigenous people."],["Cuban Revolution","In Guatemala he saw a reformist government overthrown in a CIA-backed coup in 1954. In Mexico he met Fidel Castro and joined the 1956 Granma expedition. His nickname ‘Che’ comes from an Argentine expression similar to ‘hey, mate’. He won a decisive battle at Santa Clara in December 1958."],["In government","As commander of La Cabaña fortress in 1959, Guevara oversaw trials of former Batista officials and others; across Cuba, several hundred people were executed in the first months of the revolution. He promoted voluntary work and the ideal of a selfless ‘new man’, led economic planning and represented Cuba abroad, including at the United Nations in 1964."],["Death","In 1965 he fought with rebels in Congo, then in 1966 went to Bolivia to start a guerrilla movement, which failed to gain local support. Bolivian troops, advised by the CIA, captured him on 8 October 1967 and executed him the next day in La Higuera. His remains were found in 1997 and reburied in Santa Clara, Cuba."]],
k:[["Born","14 June 1928, Rosario, Argentina"],["Died","9 October 1967, La Higuera, Bolivia"],["Profession","Doctor"],["Famous book","The Motorcycle Diaries"],["Famous photo","Guerrillero Heroico by Alberto Korda (1960)"]],
d:[["1928","Born in Rosario, Argentina"],["1952","Motorcycle journey across South America"],["1956","Joins the Granma expedition to Cuba"],["1959","Revolution triumphs; oversees tribunals"],["1965","Leaves Cuba to fight in Congo"],["1967","Captured and executed in Bolivia"],["1997","Remains found and reburied in Cuba"]],
s2:[["The famous photograph","Alberto Korda photographed Guevara at a memorial service in Havana on 5 March 1960. The image, later called Guerrillero Heroico, became one of the most reproduced photographs in history, appearing on flags, murals, posters and fashion, often with little connection to his politics."],["A divided legacy","Many people in Latin America and around the world remember Guevara for his opposition to poverty and imperialism and his willingness to die for his beliefs. Others, including many Cuban exiles, remember him for executions, harsh labor camps and his support for armed revolution."]],
sp:["The Motorcycle Diaries","Guevara’s diary of his 1952 journey was published decades after his death and adapted into an acclaimed 2004 film. It shows a young, curious and humorous traveler, very different from the later guerrilla commander."],
g:[["Marxist","A follower of Karl Marx’s ideas about class struggle and communism"],["Guerrilla war","Fighting by small, mobile groups against a larger force"],["Tribunal","A court, here one set up by revolutionaries"],["Icon","A widely recognized symbol"]],
f:["Che Guevara trained as a doctor.","His face is one of the most reproduced images in the world.","He was executed in Bolivia at the age of 39."],
fun:"Alberto Korda never received royalties for his famous photograph of Che, though he sued when it was used in a vodka advertisement.",
tl:"Si Che Guevara ay isang rebolusyonaryong Marxista na ipinanganak sa Argentina at naging pinuno sa Rebolusyong Cubano. Naging pandaigdigang simbolo ang kaniyang mukha, ngunit kasama sa kaniyang talaan ang mga pagbitay."});

ADDART({id:"pinochet",t:"Augusto Pinochet",c:"People",i:"📕",w:"Augusto Pinochet",r:["chilecountry","coldwar","hrights","democ","capitalism"],
p:[["Military dictator of Chile","Augusto Pinochet seized power in a coup on 11 September 1973."],["Repression","Thousands were killed or disappeared and tens of thousands tortured."],["Free-market reforms","His economic policies remain debated; Chileans voted him out in 1988."]],
l:"Augusto Pinochet (1915–2006) was a Chilean army general who led a military coup on 11 September 1973 that overthrew the elected socialist president Salvador Allende, who died during the attack on the presidential palace. Pinochet ruled Chile as dictator until 1990. His regime dissolved Congress, banned political parties and persecuted opponents: official commissions later found that more than 3,000 people were killed or ‘disappeared’ and about 38,000 were tortured or imprisoned for political reasons, while around 200,000 went into exile. Advised by economists trained at the University of Chicago, known as the ‘Chicago Boys’, the regime introduced free-market reforms such as privatization and a private pension system, which supporters credit with Chile’s later growth and critics blame for inequality. In a 1988 referendum, Chileans voted ‘No’ to eight more years of his rule, and democracy returned in 1990. Arrested in London in 1998, he faced charges but died in 2006 without being convicted.",
s:[["The coup","Allende, elected in 1970, nationalized copper mines and banks amid economic crisis and deep political division. The United States, opposed to a Marxist government, had funded opposition groups. On 11 September 1973, the armed forces bombed La Moneda palace; Allende took his own life rather than surrender. Pinochet became head of a military junta and soon sole ruler."],["Repression","Thousands were detained in stadiums and camps, including Santiago’s National Stadium. The secret police, DINA, tortured prisoners and murdered opponents, including in exile: in 1976 it killed former minister Orlando Letelier with a car bomb in Washington, D.C. The singer Víctor Jara was killed days after the coup."],["The economy","After a severe crisis in the early 1980s, Chile’s economy grew strongly. Supporters say the reforms made Chile one of Latin America’s most prosperous countries; critics point to rising inequality, privatized public services and the human cost of repression. Protests in 2019 against inequality led Chile to attempt, unsuccessfully, to replace Pinochet’s 1980 constitution."],["Return to democracy and justice","In the plebiscite of 5 October 1988, about 56 percent voted ‘No’. Pinochet handed the presidency to Patricio Aylwin in 1990 but remained army chief until 1998. That year he was arrested in London on a Spanish warrant, a landmark case for international justice; Britain released him on health grounds in 2000. He was charged in Chile but died on 10 December 2006, International Human Rights Day."]],
k:[["Born","25 November 1915, Valparaíso, Chile"],["Died","10 December 2006, Santiago"],["Ruled Chile","1973–1990"],["Killed or disappeared","More than 3,000"],["Plebiscite","5 October 1988 (‘No’ won)"]],
d:[["1970","Salvador Allende elected"],["1973","Military coup on 11 September"],["1976","Letelier assassinated in Washington"],["1980","New constitution adopted"],["1988","‘No’ wins the plebiscite"],["1990","Democracy restored"],["1998","Pinochet arrested in London"],["2006","Dies without being convicted"]],
s2:[["The ‘No’ campaign","The opposition’s 1988 television campaign used upbeat songs, humor and a rainbow logo with the slogan ‘Chile, happiness is coming’. Its story was told in the 2012 film No, starring Gael García Bernal."],["The disappeared","Families of the ‘disappeared’, mostly women, spent decades searching for their relatives. Some danced the cueca, Chile’s national dance, alone in protest. Truth commissions in 1991 and 2004 documented the crimes, and hundreds of former agents were later convicted."]],
sp:["The Pinochet case","Pinochet’s arrest in London in October 1998 was the first time a former head of state was detained abroad on the principle of universal jurisdiction, the idea that some crimes such as torture can be prosecuted anywhere. The case influenced later international justice efforts."],
g:[["Coup d’état","The violent overthrow of a government"],["Junta","A military group that rules after taking power"],["Disappeared","People secretly abducted and killed by the state"],["Plebiscite","A direct vote by the people on an important question"]],
f:["The coup took place on 11 September 1973.","Pinochet was arrested in London in 1998 on a Spanish warrant.","He died on International Human Rights Day."],
fun:"Santiago’s National Stadium, once used as a detention center, kept a section of old wooden benches as a memorial with the words ‘A people without memory is a people without a future’.",
tl:"Si Augusto Pinochet ay heneral na namuno sa kudeta sa Chile noong 11 Setyembre 1973 at naging diktador hanggang 1990. Libo-libo ang pinatay o nawala at sampu-sampung libo ang pinahirapan; ibinoto siyang palitan ng mga Chilean noong 1988."});

ADDART({id:"franco",t:"Francisco Franco",c:"People",i:"📕",w:"Francisco Franco",r:["spaincountry","fascism","hitler","mussolini","ww2"],
p:[["Dictator of Spain","Francisco Franco ruled Spain from 1939 until his death in 1975."],["Civil war","He led the Nationalist side to victory in the Spanish Civil War."],["Memory debates","Spain still debates how to remember his rule."]],
l:"Francisco Franco (1892–1975) was a Spanish general who led a military uprising against Spain’s elected Republican government in July 1936, starting the Spanish Civil War. With help from Nazi Germany and Fascist Italy, his Nationalist forces won in April 1939, and he ruled Spain as dictator, called El Caudillo (‘the leader’), until his death in 1975. His regime executed tens of thousands of opponents after the war, imprisoned many more, banned political parties and trade unions, censored the press, made Catholicism the state religion and suppressed regional languages such as Catalan and Basque. Spain stayed officially neutral in World War II and later became a US ally in the Cold War. From the 1960s, economic growth and tourism transformed the country. Franco named Prince Juan Carlos as his successor, and after Franco’s death Spain became a democracy. His legacy remains politically sensitive; in 2019 his remains were moved out of a huge state monument.",
s:[["The Spanish Civil War","The war of 1936–39 pitted Nationalists, including conservatives, monarchists, the Catholic Church and the fascist Falange, against Republicans, including liberals, socialists, communists and anarchists. Germany and Italy supported Franco; the Soviet Union and international volunteers supported the Republic. On 26 April 1937, German and Italian planes bombed the town of Guernica, inspiring Picasso’s famous painting."],["Repression","Both sides committed atrocities during the war. After his victory, Franco’s regime carried out mass executions, and historians estimate that tens of thousands were killed after 1939, with hundreds of thousands imprisoned in camps and jails. Many victims still lie in unmarked mass graves."],["Franco’s Spain","Spain was isolated after World War II but, as the Cold War grew, signed agreements with the United States in 1953 and joined the UN in 1955. The ‘Spanish miracle’ of the 1960s brought industry and mass tourism. Franco died on 20 November 1975."],["Transition and memory","King Juan Carlos I helped lead a peaceful transition to democracy, with free elections in 1977 and a new constitution in 1978. A 1977 amnesty law protected officials from prosecution. Laws on historical memory in 2007 and 2022 supported searches for mass graves. In 2019 Franco’s body was exhumed from the Valley of the Fallen, now renamed Cuelgamuros."]],
k:[["Born","4 December 1892, Ferrol, Spain"],["Died","20 November 1975, Madrid"],["Ruled Spain","1939–1975"],["Title","El Caudillo"],["Successor as head of state","King Juan Carlos I"]],
d:[["1936","Military uprising starts the Civil War"],["1937","Bombing of Guernica"],["1939","Nationalist victory"],["1953","Agreements with the United States"],["1975","Franco dies"],["1978","Democratic constitution"],["2019","Remains moved from the Valley of the Fallen"]],
s2:[["Guernica","Pablo Picasso painted Guernica in 1937 for the Paris World’s Fair as a protest against the bombing of the Basque town. He refused to let it go to Spain until democracy returned; it arrived in 1981 and hangs in the Reina Sofía museum in Madrid."],["Stolen babies","Investigations have found that during Franco’s rule and afterwards, some babies were taken from mothers, often those seen as politically unreliable or unmarried, and given to other families. Spanish courts and campaigners have tried to trace these cases."]],
sp:["International Brigades","About 35,000 to 40,000 volunteers from over 50 countries, including writers such as George Orwell and Ernest Hemingway as a journalist, went to fight or report for the Spanish Republic. Orwell’s Homage to Catalonia and Hemingway’s For Whom the Bell Tolls drew on their experiences."],
g:[["Nationalists","Franco’s side in the Spanish Civil War"],["Republicans","Supporters of Spain’s elected Republic"],["Caudillo","A military or political leader"],["Exhume","To dig up a buried body"]],
f:["Picasso’s Guernica was painted in 1937.","Spain was officially neutral in World War II.","Spain became a democracy after Franco’s death in 1975."],
fun:"For years after Franco’s death, the comedy show Saturday Night Live ran a joke news item: ‘Generalísimo Francisco Franco is still dead.’",
tl:"Si Francisco Franco ang heneral na nanalo sa Digmaang Sibil ng Espanya at naging diktador mula 1939 hanggang 1975. Pinatay at ikinulong ng kaniyang rehimen ang maraming kalaban, at naging demokrasya ang Espanya pagkamatay niya."});

ADDART({id:"gaddafi",t:"Muammar Gaddafi",c:"People",i:"📗",w:"Muammar Gaddafi",r:["africanunion","terrorism","isis"],
p:[["Ruler of Libya","Muammar Gaddafi ruled Libya from 1969 to 2011."],["Revolution and repression","He used oil wealth for development but crushed all opposition."],["Overthrown","He was killed during the 2011 civil war."]],
l:"Muammar Gaddafi (1942–2011) was the ruler of Libya for 42 years, from a military coup on 1 September 1969 until his death in 2011. A young army officer inspired by Arab nationalism, he overthrew King Idris and created a political system he called the Jamahiriya, or ‘state of the masses’, set out in his Green Book, in which he held no official office yet controlled everything. Libya’s oil wealth funded housing, education and health care, but there were no free elections, and critics were imprisoned, tortured or killed, including abroad. His government supported armed groups around the world and was blamed for terrorist attacks, including the 1988 bombing of Pan Am Flight 103 over Lockerbie, Scotland, which killed 270 people. After giving up weapons of mass destruction programs in 2003, he restored ties with the West. In 2011, an uprising during the Arab Spring, supported by NATO air strikes, ended his rule; he was captured and killed near Sirte on 20 October 2011. Libya has been divided and unstable since.",
s:[["Rise to power","Gaddafi was born in 1942 near Sirte to a Bedouin family. Admiring Egypt’s Gamal Abdel Nasser, he led a bloodless coup at age 27. He closed British and American bases and nationalized oil companies, using rising oil revenues to transform the country."],["The Green Book","Gaddafi’s Green Book, published from 1975, rejected both capitalism and communism, proposing direct democracy through people’s committees. In practice, power was concentrated in Gaddafi, his family and security forces. In 1996, about 1,200 prisoners were killed at Abu Salim prison in Tripoli."],["Terrorism and isolation","Libya was linked to the 1986 bombing of a Berlin disco, after which the United States bombed Tripoli and Benghazi, and to the Lockerbie bombing. UN sanctions followed. In 1999 Libya handed over two suspects; one was convicted in 2001. In 2003 Gaddafi agreed to give up chemical and nuclear weapons programs, and many sanctions were lifted."],["The 2011 uprising","Protests began in Benghazi in February 2011. Gaddafi threatened to hunt down rebels ‘house by house’, and the UN Security Council authorized action to protect civilians. NATO air strikes helped rebels take Tripoli in August. Gaddafi was captured and killed on 20 October 2011. Since then Libya has suffered civil wars and rival governments."]],
k:[["Born","1942, near Sirte, Libya"],["Died","20 October 2011, near Sirte"],["Ruled Libya","1969–2011"],["Book","The Green Book (1975)"],["Lockerbie bombing","21 December 1988, 270 killed"]],
d:[["1969","Gaddafi seizes power"],["1975","The Green Book published"],["1986","US bombs Libya"],["1988","Lockerbie bombing"],["2003","Gives up weapons programs"],["2011","Uprising and NATO intervention; Gaddafi killed"]],
s2:[["Debate over the intervention","Supporters of the 2011 NATO intervention say it prevented a massacre in Benghazi. Critics argue it went beyond protecting civilians to regime change and left Libya without a functioning state, allowing armed groups, people smuggling and the Islamic State to spread."],["Gaddafi and Africa","Gaddafi used Libya’s wealth to fund projects across Africa and promoted the idea of a ‘United States of Africa’. He played a major role in creating the African Union in 2002. Some African leaders admired him; others resented his interference."]],
sp:["The tent","Gaddafi often stayed in a large Bedouin tent on foreign visits, pitching it in Paris, Rome and New York, accompanied by a unit of female bodyguards. The tent became part of his famously eccentric public image."],
g:[["Coup","A sudden seizure of power"],["Jamahiriya","Gaddafi’s term for ‘state of the masses’"],["Sanctions","Penalties placed on a country"],["Arab Spring","A wave of uprisings in the Arab world from 2010–11"]],
f:["Gaddafi ruled Libya for 42 years.","He held no official title for most of his rule, calling himself ‘Brotherly Leader’.","The Lockerbie bombing is the deadliest terrorist attack in British history."],
fun:"Under Gaddafi, from 1977 to 2011, Libya’s flag was plain green with no design at all, the only national flag of a single color.",
tl:"Si Muammar Gaddafi ang namuno sa Libya mula 1969 hanggang 2011. Ginamit niya ang yaman ng langis para sa kaunlaran ngunit sinupil ang lahat ng oposisyon, at napatay siya noong digmaang sibil ng 2011."});

ADDART({id:"kimdynasty",t:"The Kim Dynasty of North Korea",c:"People",i:"🔴",w:"Kim dynasty",r:["korea","koreanwar","nukeweapons","coldwar","propaganda"],
p:[["A family dictatorship","Three generations of the Kim family have ruled North Korea since 1948."],["Total control","North Korea is one of the most closed and repressive countries in the world."],["Nuclear weapons","It has tested nuclear weapons and long-range missiles."]],
l:"The Kim dynasty is the family that has ruled North Korea, officially the Democratic People’s Republic of Korea, since its founding in 1948: Kim Il Sung (ruled 1948–1994), his son Kim Jong Il (1994–2011) and grandson Kim Jong Un (since 2011). It is the only communist state to pass power through three generations of one family. The Kims are surrounded by an intense cult of personality, and the state ideology of Juche (‘self-reliance’) is taught from childhood. Citizens cannot travel freely, access foreign media or criticize the government, and a United Nations inquiry in 2014 found crimes against humanity, including political prison camps holding tens of thousands of people. A famine in the 1990s killed hundreds of thousands or more. North Korea has developed nuclear weapons, conducting six nuclear tests between 2006 and 2017, and long-range missiles, leading to UN sanctions. In 2024 it sent troops to support Russia in its war against Ukraine.",
s:[["Kim Il Sung","Kim Il Sung was a guerrilla fighter against Japanese rule who was installed as leader with Soviet support after World War II. In 1950 he invaded South Korea, starting the Korean War, which ended in an armistice in 1953 with the peninsula still divided. He built a cult around himself as the ‘Great Leader’ and remains ‘Eternal President’ after his death in 1994."],["Kim Jong Il","Kim Jong Il took over in 1994 as the economy collapsed after the loss of Soviet aid. Floods and mismanagement caused a famine, known as the ‘Arduous March’, in the mid-1990s; estimates of deaths range from about 240,000 to over a million or more. He pursued a ‘military first’ policy and conducted North Korea’s first nuclear test in 2006."],["Kim Jong Un","Kim Jong Un, thought to have been born around 1984, came to power in December 2011. He had his uncle Jang Song Thaek executed in 2013, and his half-brother Kim Jong Nam was killed with a nerve agent at Kuala Lumpur airport in 2017. He met US President Donald Trump three times in 2018–19 without reaching a nuclear deal, and later strengthened ties with Russia."],["Life in North Korea","Most North Koreans face food shortages, limited electricity and constant surveillance. Society is ranked by loyalty to the state, called songbun. Tens of thousands have escaped, mostly through China, at great risk; many describe forced labor, public executions and punishment of whole families for one member’s crime."]],
k:[["Founded","1948"],["Rulers","Kim Il Sung, Kim Jong Il, Kim Jong Un"],["Ideology","Juche (self-reliance)"],["Nuclear tests","Six (2006–2017)"],["UN inquiry","2014, found crimes against humanity"]],
d:[["1948","Kim Il Sung founds North Korea"],["1950–53","Korean War"],["1994","Kim Il Sung dies; Kim Jong Il succeeds"],["mid-1990s","Famine"],["2006","First nuclear test"],["2011","Kim Jong Un takes power"],["2018","First summit with a US president"],["2024","Troops sent to support Russia"]],
s2:[["Propaganda and control","Every home must display portraits of Kim Il Sung and Kim Jong Il, and radios and TVs are fixed to state channels. Mass games, in which tens of thousands of people form giant moving pictures, celebrate the leaders. Smuggled USB drives with South Korean dramas have become a way for people to see the outside world, despite harsh punishments."],["Succession","Little is known publicly about Kim Jong Un’s family. Since 2022, state media have often shown his young daughter, named in South Korean reports as Kim Ju Ae, at missile launches and events, leading to speculation that she could be a future leader."]],
sp:["Defectors","More than 30,000 North Koreans have resettled in South Korea since the 1990s. Some, such as Yeonmi Park and Hyeonseo Lee, have written memoirs describing life in North Korea and their dangerous escapes."],
g:[["Cult of personality","Glorifying a leader as almost godlike"],["Juche","North Korea’s ideology of self-reliance"],["Armistice","An agreement to stop fighting, not a peace treaty"],["Defector","A person who escapes from their country"]],
f:["North Korea is the only communist state with three generations of family rule.","Kim Il Sung is still officially ‘Eternal President’.","The Korean War never formally ended with a peace treaty."],
fun:"North Korea used its own time zone, half an hour behind South Korea, from 2015 to 2018, before switching back as a gesture of goodwill.",
tl:"Ang dinastiyang Kim ay pamilyang namuno sa Hilagang Korea mula 1948: sina Kim Il Sung, Kim Jong Il at Kim Jong Un. Isa ito sa pinakasarado at mapaniil na bansa sa mundo, at nakagawa ito ng mga sandatang nukleyar."});

ADDART({id:"leopold2",t:"Leopold II and the Congo Free State",c:"History",i:"🕯️",w:"Congo Free State",r:["colonialism","drccountry","slavetrade","hrights","genocide"],
p:[["A king’s private colony","King Leopold II of Belgium owned the Congo Free State personally from 1885 to 1908."],["Rubber and terror","Forced labor to collect rubber caused enormous suffering and death."],["Exposed","Campaigners revealed the abuses, and Belgium took over the colony in 1908."]],
l:"The Congo Free State was a huge territory in central Africa, about 76 times the size of Belgium, that King Leopold II of Belgium ruled as his personal property from 1885 to 1908. Leopold claimed he would bring civilization and end the Arab-Swahili slave trade, and European powers recognized his claim at the Berlin Conference. In reality, his agents and concession companies forced Congolese people to collect ivory and wild rubber, which was in huge demand for bicycle and car tires. Villages that failed to meet quotas were attacked; soldiers of the Force Publique took hostages, burned villages and cut off hands as proof of bullets used or as punishment. Violence, famine, disease and falling birth rates caused a massive population decline, with estimates of deaths ranging from one million to as many as ten million, figures historians still debate. Journalists, missionaries and diplomats exposed the abuses, and in 1908 Belgium took over the colony, which became independent as the Democratic Republic of the Congo in 1960.",
s:[["Leopold’s ambitions","Leopold wanted Belgium to have a colony. He hired the explorer Henry Morton Stanley to sign treaties with local leaders in the Congo basin, and presented his project as humanitarian. In 1885 the Congo Free State became his personal possession, not Belgium’s."],["The rubber terror","From the 1890s, the invention of the inflatable tire created a rubber boom. People were forced to gather wild rubber from vines deep in the forest. Agents were paid by the amount collected, encouraging brutality. Photographs of people with severed hands became symbols of the regime."],["Exposure","The shipping clerk Edmund Dene Morel noticed that ships brought rubber and ivory from the Congo but carried mainly guns and soldiers back. He founded the Congo Reform Association in 1904, the same year British diplomat Roger Casement published a report documenting atrocities. Missionaries such as Alice Seeley Harris took photographs. Writers including Mark Twain and Arthur Conan Doyle joined the campaign."],["Aftermath and memory","Under pressure, Belgium annexed the Congo in 1908; Leopold burned the Free State’s archives. Belgian rule remained harsh but less murderous. In 2020, during anti-racism protests, statues of Leopold were vandalized or removed, and King Philippe expressed his ‘deepest regrets’ for colonial wounds. In 2022 Belgium returned a tooth of Congo’s first prime minister, Patrice Lumumba, to his family."]],
k:[["Ruled by","King Leopold II of Belgium"],["Period","1885–1908"],["Main export","Wild rubber and ivory"],["Estimated deaths","Debated; from one million to as many as ten million"],["Key report","Casement Report, 1904"]],
d:[["1876","Leopold founds the International African Association"],["1885","Congo Free State recognized"],["1890s","Rubber boom begins"],["1904","Casement Report; Congo Reform Association founded"],["1908","Belgium annexes the Congo"],["1960","Congo becomes independent"],["2020","Belgian king expresses regret"]],
s2:[["King Leopold’s Ghost","Adam Hochschild’s 1998 book King Leopold’s Ghost brought the story to a wide audience and helped revive debate in Belgium about its colonial past. Some historians have questioned its death toll estimates while agreeing on the scale of abuse."],["Heart of Darkness","Joseph Conrad’s novella Heart of Darkness (1899) drew on his 1890 journey up the Congo River as a steamboat officer. It depicts the greed and cruelty of colonial ivory trading, though critics such as Chinua Achebe argued that it portrays Africans in racist ways."]],
sp:["One of the first human rights campaigns","The Congo Reform Association used photographs, public meetings and magic-lantern slide shows to rally public opinion in Britain and the United States. Historians often describe it as one of the first international human rights campaigns."],
g:[["Concession","Land or rights granted to a company to exploit"],["Force Publique","The Congo Free State’s army"],["Quota","A required amount"],["Annex","To take over a territory"]],
f:["The Congo Free State was Leopold’s personal property, not Belgium’s.","The rubber boom was driven by demand for tires.","Leopold had the Free State’s archives burned in 1908."],
fun:"Mark Twain wrote a satirical pamphlet in 1905, King Leopold’s Soliloquy, in which the king complains about the camera because photographs exposed his crimes.",
tl:"Pag-aari ni Haring Leopold II ng Belgium ang Congo Free State mula 1885 hanggang 1908. Ang sapilitang pangongolekta ng goma ay nagdulot ng matinding pagdurusa at kamatayan, hanggang ilantad ito ng mga kampanyador at kunin ito ng Belgium noong 1908."});

